1. c4 e5 2. g3 Nf6 (2... d5 3. cxd5 Nf6 (3... Qxd5 4. Nf3 Nc6 (4... e4? 5. Nc3 Qc6 (5... Qe6 6. Qa4+ Bd7 7. Qxe4) (5... Qf5 6. Qa4+) 6. e3 a6 (6... exf3?? 7. Bb5!) 7. Nd4 Qg6 8. Bg2 Nf6 9. Qc2) 5. Bg2 Nf6 6. Nc3 Qd8 7. O-O) 4. Bg2) 3. Bg2 d5 { The Reversed Dragon, another very theoretical battle. It is heavily debated whether the extra tempo for White will create more chances, since it is often stated that the Accelerated Dragon does not offer Black many good winning chances. } (3... c6! { Another one of the lines I find annoying (and which I play myself as Black!). I've tried lots of different moves against the Reversed Alapin, and found d4 to be the most effective. } 4. d4! exd4 (4... e4 5. d5 { To not allow BLack to complete the pawn chain with d5 } 5... cxd5 6. cxd5 Bb4+ 7. Bd2 Bxd2+ (7... Qa5?! 8. Nc3 Bxc3 9. Bxc3 Qxd5 10. Qxd5 Nxd5 11. Bxg7 Rg8 12. Bxe4 Rxg7 13. Bxd5 { White's up a clean pawn and has wide open lines. }) 8. Qxd2 O-O 9. Nc3 Re8 10. d6 { An interesting try to cramp Black's position, though it does allow Nc6 }) 5. Qxd4 d5 6. Nf3 (6. Nc3 dxc4 7. Qxc4) 6... Be7 (6... dxc4 7. Qxc4 (7. Qxd8+!? Kxd8)) 7. O-O O-O 8. cxd5 cxd5 9. Nc3 Nc6 10. Qa4 Be6 11. Bf4 { Should not bring White a lot of trouble. Simply blockade the isolated d5 pawn and not overextend. }) 4. cxd5 Nxd5 5. Nc3 Nb6 (5... Be6?! 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. O-O Be7 (7... Bc5?! 8. Nxe5 { [%cal Gd2d4,Rd4e5,Rd4c5] } 8... Nxe5 9. d4 Nxc3 (9... Bxd4?? 10. Qxd4 Nxc3 11. Qxe5 Nd5 12. Qxg7 Rf8 13. Bh6) 10. bxc3 Bd6 (10... Bxd4?! 11. cxd4 Nc6?? (11... Ng6 12. d5 Bd7 13. Ba3) 12. d5) 11. dxe5 Bxe5 12. Qa4+ Bd7 13. Qe4 Qe7 14. Qxb7) 8. d4! exd4 (8... Nxc3 9. bxc3 e4 10. Nd2 f5 11. e3) 9. Nxd4 { [%csl Rd5,Gd4,Gc3,Rc6,Re6][%cal Rg2d5] } 9... Nxc3 10. bxc3 Nxd4 11. cxd4) 6. Nf3! (6. e3 { 6. Nf3 is more mainstream, but this 6. e3 line is one of my pet lines. If Black has not seen this before, it would throw him off. Instead of Nf3, White develops with Nge2 followed by an early f4 to try to get 2 central pawns against none for Black. } 6... Nc6 7. Nge2 Be7 (7... h5 { I've faced an early h5 multiple times. The best bet is to strike back in the center and ignore the pawn. } 8. d4 h4 (8... exd4 9. Nxd4 Nxd4 10. exd4 h4 11. O-O { This pawn is not to worry about. Black's king, on the other hand, is! } 11... hxg3 12. Re1+ Be7 13. hxg3) 9. d5 h3 10. Bf3 Qf6 11. Be4) 8. O-O O-O 9. f4 exf4 (9... f6 10. f5 Bd7 11. d4 exd4 12. exd4 Be8 13. d5 Ne5 14. Nf4 { Huge space advantage and a nice outpost on e6 to hop in anytime }) 10. Nxf4 Ne5 11. d4 Ng6) 6... Nc6 7. O-O Be7 8. a3! { [%cal Gb2b4] } (8. d3! { [%cal Gc1e3,Ga1c1] } 8... O-O 9. Be3 Be6 10. Rc1 f6 { [%cal Ga2a3,Gf3d2,Gc3e4] } 11. a3 Qd7 12. Ne4 Nd5 13. Bc5 b6 14. Bxe7 Ndxe7 { [%cal Gd1c2,Gb2b4] }) (8. b3! O-O 9. Bb2 f6 10. Rc1 { [%anno "Mikae5", mikae5] We have a super solid position with a slight advantage thanks to our strong central control, the c-file, our central majority as well as a lack of weaknesses. Next up we can play Qc2 and Rfd1 and then we'll keep gunning for the queenside. } 10... Be6 11. Qc2 Nb4 12. Qe4 c5 13. Nh4 Qxd2 14. Qb1 f5 15. Nf3 Qd6 16. Rfd1 Qb8) 8... O-O { [%cal Gb4b5,Ga1b1,Gb2b4] } 9. b4 (9. d3 Be6 { [%cal Gb2b4,Gc1b2,Ga1b1,Ge2e4,Gd1d2,Gf1e1,Gh2h3,Gg1h2,Yc1e3,Ya1c1] }) 9... Be6 { [%cal Gb4b5,Ga1b1] } (9... Re8! { [%csl Be5][%cal Be8e5] } 10. d3 Bf8 11. Bb2 a5 12. b5 Nd4 13. Nd2 a4 14. e3 Ne6 15. Nc4 Nc5 (15... Nxc4 16. dxc4 Nc5 17. Qxd8 Rxd8 18. Nd5) 16. Nxe5 Rxe5 17. d4 Re8 18. dxc5) 10. b5 (10. Rb1!? { Carlsen, M. - Topalov, V., 1-0, Cote d'Ivoire Blitz 2019, https://lichess.org/0xx8DT1n } { [%anno "Mikae5", mikae5] Indirectly targeting the e-pawn, as white cannot capture it directly without the rok on b1. } { [%csl Re5][%cal Gb4b5] } 10... f6! { [%cal Bf6e5] } 11. b5 Nd4 12. e3 Nxf3+ 13. Bxf3 Qc8 14. Qc2 Bf5 15. d3 Rd8 16. Rd1) 10... Nd4 11. Rb1 (11. Nxe5?? Bb3! 12. Qe1 Nc2 13. Bxb7 Nxe1 14. Bxa8 Qxa8) 11... Nxf3+ (11... f6! 12. e3 Nxf3+ 13. Bxf3 Qc8 14. Qc2 Rd8 15. Rd1 Bf5 16. d3 Kh8 17. e4 Bd7 18. a4 c6 19. Be3 cxb5 20. axb5 Be6 21. Qb2) 12. Bxf3 Nd5 13. Bb2 Nxc3 14. Bxc3 *


