1. c4 { [%eval 0.11] } 1... e5 { [%eval 0.14] } 2. Nc3 { [%eval 0.13] } (2. g3!) 2... Nc6 { [%eval 0.21] } 3. g3 { [%eval 0.23] } 3... g6 { [%eval 0.2] } 4. Bg2 { [%eval 0.23] } 4... Bg7 { [%eval 0.19] } { Here we have reached the Reverse Closed Sicilian position, where both players have put their bishops on the long diagonals and have plans to strike in the center and castle king side.
There are 3 main moves here for white:
1. d3
2. e3
3. Rook b1 } { [%cal Rf8g7] } 5. d3 { [%eval 0.22] } { White plays d3 to prepare ideas such as e4 and also free the dark square bishop } { [%cal Yc1g5,Ye2e4] } (5. e3 { White plays e3 here to prepare d4 to strike at blacks center. } 5... d6 { Blacks plays d6 to solidify the center and wait to see what white wants to do. } 6. Nge2 { White plays knight ge2 to further protect the d4 square and also prepare to castle } 6... Nge7 { Black mimics as to not block the bishop that is critical in the defense of d4. } 7. O-O { White castles and is in a comfortable position. White has plans to eventually play b4 and b5 as well as push d4. } { [%cal Ge2f4,Gd2d4,Gb2b4,Gb4b5,Ga1b1,Gd1c2,Gf1e1] }) (5. Rb1 { The point of rook b1 is to prepare a quick b4 to attack black on the queen side. } { [%csl Yb4][%cal Yb2b4] } 5... a5 { Black plays a5 to prevent an instant b4, but white can prepare it with a3 } 6. a3 d6 { Black plays d6 to solidify the center and free the light square bishop. } 7. b4 axb4 8. axb4 { An exchange happens on b4 and the game continues. White has ideas to play e3 or d3 and then d4 or e4. White also wants to develop the knight on f1 and castle as well as eventually play b5 to create a strong pawn chain pointing towards the queen's side. } { [%cal Ye2e4,Yd2d4,Gd1c2,Gd1a4,Gb4b5,Gd2d3,Ge2e3,Ge3e4,Gd3d4,Gg1f3,Ge1g1] }) (5. e4! d6 6. Nge2 { [%anno "Mikae5", mikae5] transposes } { [%cal Gd2d3] }) 5... d6 { [%eval 0.21] } { Black responds by mimicking, solidifying the position and waiting for white ideas. } 6. e4! { [%eval 0.17] } { White plays e4, the Botvinnik Variation to strike in the center and open up the dark square bishop, at the slight cost of blocking the light square bishop on g2. White has a strong pawn triangle in the center of the board. } { [%csl Bc4,Be4,Gd5][%cal Bd3c4,Bd3e4] } 6... Nge7 { [%eval 0.25] } { Black plays Knight ge7 to defend the center and avoid blocking black's own bishop on g7 even more. } (6... f5 7. Nge2 Nf6 (7... Nh6 8. h4 { [%cal Gc1g5] } 8... Nf7 { [%csl Bg5] } 9. h5) 8. O-O { [%cal Bd3e4,Bg3f4] } 8... O-O 9. Nd5 { [%cal Bd3e4,Bg3f4] }) (6... Be6 7. Nge2 Qd7 8. Nd5 { [%csl Rc7][%cal Gh7h5,Gh2h4] } 8... Nd8 9. d4 c6 10. Ne3 Bh3 (10... exd4 11. Nxd4) 11. Bxh3 Qxh3 12. d5 Nf6 13. Qd3 O-O 14. Bd2) (6... Nf6 { [%anno "Mikae5", mikae5] See chapter on e5 King's Indian Setup }) 7. Nge2 { [%eval 0.22] } { White mimics for the same reasons and now white has ideas of queen d2, bishop e3 and castling, as well as some rook b1 and b4 ideas. The bishop and queen battery can be used to trade off blacks strong bishop on g7. } { [%cal Gc1e3,Gd1d2,Gb2b4,Ga1b1,Ge1g1] } 7... O-O { [%eval 0.34] } 8. O-O { [%eval 0.27] } 8... f5 { [%eval 0.34] } (8... Be6 9. Nd5 { [%csl Rc7] } 9... Qd7 10. Be3 f5 11. Qd2 { [%anno "Mikae5", mikae5] transposes to 8...f5 } { [%cal Gf8f7,Ga8f8,Ga1c1,Gb2b4,Gb4b5,Ge4f5] }) (8... Nd4 9. Nxd4 exd4 10. Ne2 { [%csl Gc5][%cal Gb2b4] } 10... a5 11. b3 Rb8 { [%cal Gb7b5,Gh2h3,Ge2f4,Gc1d2] }) 9. Nd5 { [%eval 0.27] } 9... Be6 { [%eval 0.47] } 10. Be3 { [%eval 0.52] [%csl Bf4][%cal Bd3e4,Be2f4,Bg3f4,Be3f4] } 10... Qd7 { [%eval 0.55] } 11. Qd2 { [%eval 0.35] [%cal Gc6d8,Ga8e8] } 11... Nd8 { [%eval 0.78] } (11... Rf7! { [%cal Bf7g7] } 12. f3!? { [%cal Gb2b3,Ga1c1] } (12. b4 Raf8 13. b5 Nd8 14. exf5 { [%cal Ge6f5,Gd3d4,Bg6f5,Bf2f4] } 14... Bxf5 { [%cal Gd3d4] } (14... gxf5 15. f4) 15. d4) (12. Rac1 Raf8 13. b4)) 12. Bh6?! { [%eval 0.18] } { Inaccuracy. Nxe7+ was best. } { [%cal Ga1c1,Gb2b3] } (12. Nxe7+ Qxe7 13. exf5 Bxf5 14. d4 a5 15. h3 h5 16. Rad1 Kh7 17. dxe5 dxe5) 12... Bxh6 { [%eval 0.2] } 13. Qxh6 { [%eval 0.19] } 13... c6 { [%eval 0.18] } 14. Nxe7+ { [%eval 0.19] } 14... Qxe7 { [%eval 0.17] } 15. exf5 { [%eval 0.26] [%cal Ge6f5,Gd3d4,Bg6f5,Bf2f4] } *


