1. c4 c5 2. g3 g6 3. Bg2 Bg7 4. Nc3 Nc6 { Commonly stated by experts as "mutual zugzwang". There has been lots of debates of what the best move to play by white is. What if Black copies? Can white really prove an advantage with the first move? My recommendation is Nf3. } 5. Nf3! (5. e4?! { My recommendation against the Kings Indian is e4, the Botvinnik setup. From experience, I find the Botvinnik is not effective if Black has not committed either Nf6 or e5. } 5... d6 6. Nge2 e6 7. O-O Nge7 8. d3 O-O 9. Be3 Nd4 { Black is doing well here. No advantage whatsoever for White. }) (5. a3!? e5?! (5... e6 6. b4?! Nxb4! 7. axb4 cxb4 8. d4 (8. Nb5 Bxa1 9. Qa4 Bf6 10. Nd6+ Kf8) 8... bxc3) (5... d6 6. b4?! cxb4 7. axb4 Nxb4 8. Qa4+ Nc6 9. Bxc6+ bxc6 10. Qxc6+ Bd7 $17) 6. b4 cxb4 7. axb4 Nxb4? 8. Ba3 Nc6 9. Nb5 Bf8! (9... Nge7?? 10. Bd6 O-O 11. Bc7 Qe8 12. Nd6) 10. Bd6 Bxd6 11. Nxd6+ Kf8 12. c5) 5... Nf6 (5... e6! { The Fischer System, in my opinion one of the toughest systems to beat. } 6. d4 { I recommend this line as it sidesteps the center controlling Nge7 + d5 by BLack. } 6... cxd4 (6... Nxd4 7. Nxd4 cxd4 8. Nb5 Qb6 9. e3 { With pressure on the dark square holes. }) 7. Nb5 d5 8. cxd5 Qa5+ (8... exd5 9. O-O Nge7 10. Nbxd4 { With good play against the isolated d5 pawn }) 9. Nd2 Qxb5 10. dxc6 bxc6 11. a4 Qb6 { Plenty of compensation for the pawn, and likely will win back the pawn on c6 and more! } 12. Nc4 Qb4+ 13. Bd2 Qxc4 14. Rc1 Qa6 15. Bxc6+) 6. d4 { The best way to avoid symmetry: Opening up the position } 6... cxd4 7. Nxd4 Nxd4 (7... O-O 8. O-O { Kramnik, Vladimir - Anand, Viswanathan, 1/2-1/2, Wch Blitz 5th, 2009, https://lichess.org/RSwv0sZ2 } 8... Nxd4 (8... a6?! { [%cal Gc4c5] } 9. c5 Qa5 (9... Qc7 { [%cal Gd4b3] } 10. Nb3 Rd8 (10... d6 11. cxd6 exd6 12. Bf4) 11. Bf4 d6 (11... e5? 12. Bg5 d6 13. Bxf6 Bxf6 14. Nd5 { [%csl Rc7,Rf6][%cal Rd5f6,Rd5c7] }) 12. cxd6 exd6 13. Qd2) 10. Nb3 Qc7?! 11. Bf4 e5 { [%cal Gf4g5] } (11... Qd8 12. Qd2) 12. Bg5 h6 13. Bxf6 Bxf6 { [%csl Gd5] } 14. Nd5 $16 { [%cal Gc7d8] }) (8... d6 9. Nxc6 bxc6 10. Bxc6 Rb8 11. Bg2) 9. Qxd4 { [%anno "Mikae5", mikae5] transposes }) 8. Qxd4 { Black cannot easily take advantage of the discovered attack } 8... O-O 9. O-O d6 10. Qd3 { The main line. } 10... a6 (10... Rb8 11. Be3 b6 12. Bd4 Bb7 13. e4 { Again, the very standard Maroczy Bind structure. Play solid, line your rooks on the d and e-files, and slowly play for a squeeze. }) (10... Bf5 11. e4 Be6 12. b3 Rc8 13. Be3 (13. Bd2 a6 14. Rac1 Nd7 15. Nd5) 13... a6 14. Bd4 { Typical Maroczy Bind structure. Very solid. }) (10... Be6 11. Bd2 Rc8 12. b3 b6 13. e4) 11. Bd2 Bf5 12. e4 Be6 13. b3 Rc8 14. Rac1 { Most of the lines end with some sort of Maroczy Bind hold with c4 and e4. Watch for the pawn break b5 by BLack. Do not rush this position! } 14... Nd7 15. Nd5 Ne5 *


