1. c4 c5 2. g3 (2. Nc3 Nf6 3. e4! Nc6! { [%anno "Mikae5", mikae5] s. Anti-English https://lichess.org/study/SPY2vZrT/prOXGwt2 } 4. g3 g6 5. Bg2 Bg7 6. Nge2 O-O 7. O-O d6 8. d3 a6) 2... Nf6 3. Bg2 d5 { This move order is the only way for Black to break open the center first. Given White moved first, the presence of Black taking initiative first is quite bold. However, White has tricks up his sleeve, ones that break open the center when Black least expects. } 4. cxd5 Nxd5 5. Nc3 Nc7 { The main line to keep a stable center. Ideally, BLack wants to maintain the standard Maroczy Bind center with c5 and e5. } (5... Nxc3 6. bxc3 Nc6 7. Rb1 e5 8. Qa4 Qc7 9. Nf3 { White has a lot of pressure on the queenside from afar. }) 6. Nf3 (6. e4 Nc6 7. Nge2 e5) 6... Nc6 7. O-O e5 8. d3 (8. a3 Be7 (8... f6! 9. e3 Be7 10. d4 { A thematic break! Notice Black hasn't castled yet } 10... cxd4 11. exd4 exd4 12. Ne2 d3 (12... Ne6) 13. Nf4 Bf5? 14. Nh4 { Eventually the d3 pawn is regained and White's pieces are actively placed with open lines on all sides of the board. }) 9. b4! { Shocking for Black! Since Black has no castled yet, the king runs into some trouble. } 9... cxb4 (9... O-O 10. b5 Nd4 11. Nxe5) (9... f6 10. bxc5 Bxc5 11. e3 O-O 12. d4 exd4 13. Na4 { Typically any break in the center leads to an advantage for the side playing against the Maroczy Bind since now f6 is awkwardly placed. }) (9... Be6! { [%csl Rb3,Rd4][%cal Rc6d4,Re6b3,Rb4b5,Rf3e5] } 10. b5 (10. bxc5 Bxc5) 10... Nd4 11. Nxe5?? Bb3! 12. Qe1 Nc2 13. Qd1 Nxa1) 10. axb4 Bxb4 11. Nxe5 Nxe5 (11... Bxc3 12. Nxc6 bxc6 13. dxc3) 12. Qa4+ Nc6 13. Bxc6+ bxc6 14. Qxb4 { Better pawn structure and immense long term control on the queenside for white. }) 8... Be7 9. Nd2!? { [%anno "Mikae5", mikae5] Knight to d2?! The point of this move is to maneuver the knight over to the c4 square where it will be much more useful. This also unblocks the bishop on g2 and allows white to take the knight on c7 if the b-pawn ever moves. } (9. Be3 O-O 10. Rc1 Re8!? { [%anno "Mikae5", mikae5] Playing the position similar to a Classical Dragon } { [%cal Ge7f8,Gc8g4,Gc6d4] }) (9. a3 O-O 10. Rb1 Re8 { [%cal Ge7f8] } 11. Bd2 (11. Be3 Bf8) 11... h6 (11... Bf8)) 9... Bd7 (9... O-O 10. Nc4 f6 11. Bxc6 bxc6 12. Qa4 Bh3 13. Re1 Qd7 14. Be3 Nb5 15. Rac1) 10. Nc4 f6 11. f4 b5 12. Ne3 exf4 13. gxf4 O-O 14. f5 Kh8 15. Ned5 Nxd5 16. Nxd5 Bd6 17. e4 { [%anno "Mikae5", mikae5] White plays e4 and with that sits about even in this position. White wants to develop the other bishop and queen as well as push the queenside pawns. } { [%cal Gb2b4,Ga2a3,Gf1f3,Gf3h3,Gd1g4,Gc1h6] } *


