1. c4 c5 2. Nf3 Nf6 3. g3 (3. Nc3 b6 4. d4 cxd4 5. Nxd4 Bb7 6. Bg5 Nc6 (6... e6?! 7. Ndb5 d6 8. Bf4 (8. Qd2?! a6 9. Nd4 (9. Bxf6 gxf6 10. Nd4 f5 11. O-O-O Nd7 12. e3 Rc8 13. f4) 9... Nbd7 10. O-O-O Be7 11. e4) 8... e5 9. Bg5 a6) 7. e4 e6 8. Ndb5 Qb8 9. Bxf6 gxf6 10. Qd2 a6 11. O-O-O Ne5 12. f4 axb5 13. fxe5 Qc8 14. exf6 b4) 3... b6 { Black plays b6 in hopes of Bishop b7 shortly after. } 4. Bg2 Bb7 { The hedgehog position has been reached, and after White castles Black has a few choices to continue the fortress. } { [%csl Rb7][%cal Ra7c5] } 5. O-O { Black now has a few choices here to continue the fortress. } 5... e6 { Black first choice is e6. } (5... g6 { Black can also play g6 here. } 6. Nc3 { White puts the Knight on c3. } 6... Bg7 7. d4 { Black fianchettos the bishop and white strikes in the center with d4. } 7... cxd4 8. Qxd4 { White takes back with the queen as to avoid trading the light square bishops. } 8... d6 9. Rd1 { White puts the rook on the semi-open file. } 9... Nbd7 { Black develops the knight without blocking the bishop. } 10. Be3 Rc8 { Both players develop, and the c-file will be the file of contention between White and Black. } 11. Rac1 { White exerts an influence on the c-file as well. } 11... O-O 12. Qh4 { Black castles and white moves the queen to a more active square eyeing down the Black king. } 12... a6 { Black improves the position. } 13. b3 { White solidifies the pawn structure. } 13... Re8 { Black puts the rook on a better file. } 14. Bh3 { White pins the Knight on d7. } 14... Rc7 { After Black gets out of the pin and defends the bishop, White is looking to push g4 and Play Bishop to h6 to exchange the dark squared bishops. } { [%cal Gg3g4,Ge3h6,Gh4h6,Gc3e4,Gc3d5,Ge2e3] }) 6. Nc3 { White continues development. } 6... Be7 7. Re1 (7. d4 cxd4 8. Qxd4 O-O 9. e4 d6 10. Rd1 a6 11. b3 Nbd7) 7... d6 { Black plays d6 here, allowing white to play the move e4. } 8. e4 a6 { Black is creating a strong pawn wall on the queen-side. } { [%csl Ga6,Gb6,Gc5,Gd6,Ge6] } 9. d4 { White hopes to gain a lot of space with d4. } 9... cxd4 10. Nxd4 Qc7 { After an exchange in the center, Black develops the queen to c7 to nullify the knight on d4 from White. } 11. Be3 { White is completeing development. } 11... Nbd7 { Black develops the knight while not blocking the bishop. } 12. Rc1 { White optimizes the rook on the same file as Black's queen. } 12... O-O 13. f4 { Black castles and white searches for even more space with f4. } 13... Rac8 { Black optimizes the rook on the c-file as well } 14. g4 { White is grabbing all the space given by Black. } 14... Nc5 { Black improves the position of the knight. } 15. Bf2 { The e4 pawn was attack too many times to white retreats with the bishop to allow the rook to defend the pawn as well. } { [%csl Ge4][%cal Gc5e4,Gf6e4,Gb7e4,Gc3e4,Gg2e4,Ge1e4] } 15... g6 { Black plays g6 to try and hault any f5 ideas as well as improve the pawn structure. } 16. f5 { White still plays f5 here, going for an all out assault on Black's king. } 16... gxf5 { Black takes back with the g-pawn as taking back with the e pawn servely weakens the d5 square. } 17. gxf5 { After taking back the with the g-pawn White has much more space and can use it to attack, but if White over extends Black will swoop in and take the advantage away from White.
White has ideas here of bringing the Queen into the game on the king-side, and carefully pushing the h pawn. White even has some Knight to d5 ideas to block the influence of the light squared bishop on b7. } { [%cal Gh2h3,Gh3h4,Gd1f3,Gf3g3,Gf5e6,Gc3d5,Gd4f3,Gb2b4] } *


