1. c4 Nf6 2. Nc3 g6 3. g3 Bg7 4. Bg2 O-O 5. e4 d6 6. Nge2 e5 (6... c5 { [%anno "Mikae5", mikae5] see chapter on King's Indian Setup with c5 } 7. O-O Nc6 8. d3) 7. O-O Nc6 (7... c5 { [%anno "Mikae5", mikae5] see chapter on King's Indian Setup with c5 } 8. d3 Nc6 9. h3 Nd4 (9... Be6 10. Be3) 10. f4) (7... Be6 { [%anno "Mikae5", mikae5] See chapter below. }) 8. d3 Bg4 (8... Nd4 { Against an early Nd4, I recommend Nxd4 to play for either f4 or b4 and target the d4 pawn long term. } 9. Nxd4 exd4 10. Ne2 c5 (10... Nd7 11. b4 a5 12. b5 Nc5 13. Bb2 Ne6 14. f4 { The d4 pawn will definitely be more of a weakness for Black than a thorn. }) 11. b4!) (8... Be6 { [%csl Rh3,Rd7][%cal Rd8d7,Rd7h3] } 9. h3 (9. Nd5! Qd7 10. Bg5 Ne8 11. Qd2 f6 12. Be3 Nd8 13. f4 c6? 14. f5! cxd5 15. fxe6 Nxe6 16. cxd5 Nc5 17. h4 { [%cal Gh4h5,Gg3g4,Ge2g3,Gh3c8] }) 9... Qd7 10. Kh2 Nh5 (10... Ne8 11. f4 f5 12. Be3 Nf6 13. Nd5 { Qd2 followed by a queenside expansion is up next. } { [%cal Gd1d2,Gb2b4] }) 11. g4 { Black wants to play f5, but Nh5 is too ambitious. g4 forces the knight back. } 11... Nf6 (11... Nf4 12. Nxf4 exf4 13. Bxf4 { Interesting pawn sacrifice, but hard to find enough compensation for the pawn. } 13... f5 14. exf5 gxf5 15. g5 { Keep as many lines closed as possible. Do not allow Black's rook to become active. })) 9. f3 (9. h3!? { I do not recommend playing h3, even though it may be the more "obvious-looking" one if you have the Botvinnik structure (c4 and e4 pawns with a hole on d4) because Black will exchange bishop for knight and reroute a knight to d4. It can be annoying to deal with. } 9... Bxe2 10. Nxe2 Nd7 { [%cal Gd7c5,Gc5e6,Ge6d4] }) 9... Be6 10. f4 Bg4 { Yes Black could still come back and exchange, but hey, you got a free tempo with it! } 11. f5 *


