[UTCDate "2026.09.19"]
[UTCTime "15:53:18"]

{ The English is meant to be used to restrict the amount of good responses black has from the beginning. Basically "frustrate" opponent.

In this system, there is a good attack which can come into play with f2-f4, after putting our king's knight on e2 }
1. c4 { From here, black can try and go in to a number of queen's gambit, Grünfeld, and king's Indian sorts of setups. But the reluctance of playing our pawn to d4, cuts down on the effectiveness of these opening exponentially.
In example:
Black also has other answers here like c6 and e6, however, these can lead to great games (especially for black) the move e4 will subdue a great deal of these opening repertoires. } 1... Nf6 2. Nc3 g6 (2... e6 { We are also avoiding Nimzo-indian variations with... } 3. e4 { we avoid Nimzo lines because we are not playing... } (3. d4 Bb4) 3... Bb4 4. e5! { And we have freaked black out again,..the knight is forced back.. } { [%csl Gg4][%cal Gd1g4,Gg4g7] } 4... Ng8 { then we have the crushing... } 5. Qg4! { which should give white a decent advantage with a few tempi } { [%csl Gg7][%cal Gg4g7] }) 3. g3 { Here.. } (3. d4? { this move is EXACTLY what we are TRYING TO AVOID (black's dream). Hence the reason I gave it a (?). It's not a bad move, or illogically, mistake or anything like this... . It is a perfectly playable move.... for those looking to play directly into the hands of a King's Indian, or Grünfeld player. With the mainline, black will have a good attack on the d4 square } { [%csl Gd4] } 3... d5 4. cxd5 Nxd5 5. e4 Nxc3 6. bxc3 c5 { [%cal Rc5d4,Rf8g7,Rg7d4] }) 3... Bg7 { without d4, black doesn't get the same fight on the weakened square } (3... d5 4. cxd5 Nxd5 { The previous move order doesn't have the same bite to it. There is no e-pawn to attack, the knight is being pressured, and there is a potential attack after a pawn push on the h-file. } { [%cal Gh2h5] }) 4. Bg2 O-O 5. e4 $140 { e4 is what defines this as the "Botvinnik" system. The c4 and e4, light square control or bind (Maroczy bind). Many grandmasters and top International masters have employed this system due to it's solid yet attack latent theory. } 5... d6 6. Nge2 { King's Indian setup for black... } 6... e5 { Here we have the option of two good moves, d3 or O-O. Either are good here. The example I had went with O-O.. } 7. O-O Be6 8. d3 { with ideas of developing our bishop to e3, perhaps pushing h3 to stop the knight from bouncing to g3 and attacking our dark squared night and then (for the attack) f4.
The position can be played without the h3 push, especially depending on the placement black's light square bishop and queen.
If the pawn gets from f4 -> f5, white's position is normally winning. } { [%csl Bc1,Yf4,Yh3,Rd8,Rd7,Re6,Yf5][%cal Bc1e3,Rf2f4,Yf4f5,Yg3g5,Yh2h3,Yh3h5,Rd8d7,Rd7h3,Ge2g3] } 8... Qd7 { There are other strong plans for white in this position, but one of the strongest with the greatest attacking potential is f4. } { [%cal Gf2f4] } 9. f4 Bh3?! 10. f5 { [%csl Gg5][%cal Gg3g5,Gf5f6] } 10... Bxg2 11. Kxg2 { [%csl Gh2][%cal Gg3g5,Gh2h4,Ge2g3] } 11... c6 { the next part of this attack is to push the pawns to g4-g5 and then move the knight to g3, all of this supported by the h3 pawn } { [%csl Gg4][%cal Gg3g5,Ge2g3] } 12. h3 d5 { d5 is black's best response here. He has to answered or respond to the g4-g5 threat. If he doesn't meet the threat and makes a dubious move... } (12... Na6 13. g4 Nc7 14. g5 Nh5 15. f6 $18 { And here black bishop is toast. lost it way in this game of some time. }) 13. g4 { If black doesn't play the best response, then we are still going to go with the same g4 plan... } 13... d4 14. Nb1 { the knight will be rerouted, and the pawn wedge is incredibly difficult for black to break, with out giving away a lot
In this version of the english, and most version, white will gain space on one side of the board, and normally have a very strong grip on the center.
In this position, the eval shows white up by (+1.5) with black (currently) owning a horrible dark-squared bishop. } { [%csl Bd2,Rc1][%cal Bh3f5,Bd3f5,Bb1d2,Bd2f3,Rc1a3] } *


[Event "Cappelle-la-Grande Open"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/study/bducGP1F/ZgEpM1KZ"]
[Date "1995"]
[White "GM Simon Williams"]
[Black "Andrea Locatelli"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Annotator "GM Simon Williams, and personal points by Mike Morten"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "A16"]
[Opening "English Opening: Anglo-Indian Defense, Queen's Knight Variation"]
[StudyName "English Opening Repertoire"]
[ChapterName "GM Simon Williams - Andrea Locatelli"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/fTpa2qZW/RgjIkhU9"]

1. c4 Nf6 2. Nc3 d6 3. g3 g6 { Black goes for the infamous "King's Indian" setup here. } (3... e5 4. Bg2 Nbd7 5. d3 Be7 { was the "Old Indian" that doesn't look as active, especially the Bishop on e7 opposed to the long diagonal (Bg7). After, } 6. e4 { black looks/feels cramped where the bastard move (f4) is definitely in the air as a very playable and favorable move for white. }) 4. Bg2 Bg7 5. e4 { White has gotten "stage 2" onto the board. YES, there are other ways to employ the "English", but this chapter is on the "Botvinnik" way, so... } { [%cal Gg1e2,Gd2d3] } 5... e5 6. Nge2 Nc6 7. O-O O-O 8. f4!? { Here, white skips past the "stage 2.5" idea which involved (d3 either on the 6th or 7th move ) and goes straight into the f4 push.
Instead, of 8.f4!?, 8.d3 is recommended, but the this timid move is not in the style of the person behind the wheel for white, who boldly deployed the "Freddie" pawn LOL (f, for freddie).. } 8... Be6 { Pretty standard stuff for black, which is a move I see often at the "club level". The more ambitious } (8... exf4 9. gxf4 Nh5 { would have been much more challenging. Black is moving to gain more space w/ } 10. f5 Qh4 { which would show how white may have overstepped his boundaries. }) 9. Nd5?! { I mean... when does d3 happen my friend, the bishop on c1 needs fresh air,.. not to mention the board is screaming this move ! } (9. d3 Qd7 10. Nd5 { looks sensible, but ... } (10. f5!? gxf5 11. Bg5 { is a very interesting approach w/ this pawn present. Black taking on e4, white will actually wins a piece } 11... Ne8 (11... h6 12. Bxf6 (12. exf5 Bxf5 13. Nd5 { this move order leaves white with a certain grip and compensation for the pawn; Qd2 will follow and quite possibly Ne2-c3-e4 }) 12... Bxf6 13. exf5 Bxf5 14. g4 { wins a piece - a tactical motif worth remembering }))) 9... Ne8? { Far too meek. King's Indian players always want to free their f-pawn to go ...f5, but Black won't be able to because of our set-up. Much better } { [%cal Re6g4] } (9... Bg4 { making good use of Black's grip on the d4-square, which is the one downside to the Botvinnik formation.
This would really have been rather irritating for white and he would have a tough time activating his black bishop. } { [%csl Bd4,Re2][%cal Gc6d4,Rg4e2,Gd1e1,Gd1c2] }) 10. f5! { WELL THEN, CHOKE ON THAT BUDDY!! } 10... Bd7 (10... Bxd5 11. cxd5 { Almost never happens.
because with black's response } 11... Nd4 (11... Ne7 { white has } 12. d3 { and when } 12... c6 { is a freeing move for black, but after } 13. dxc6 bxc6 14. g4! { white is much better; Ng3 follows and Black is in grave danger of being squashed to death on the kingside. } { [%csl Gg4] }) 12. Nxd4 { with the 11...Nd4 line, } 12... exd4 13. d3 { white has a pleasant advantage, Black's pawn on d4 actually working against black, hindering his own force (bishop on g7).
White has:
-control of e4 and d5
-space advantage
-and, oh yes and he has the "deuce bishop"
where g4-g5 is a move to consider (seriously). }) 11. d3 { "Having made such a gain of space, it's high time to develop the remaining pieces." by GM Simon Williams } 11... f6 { Ouch! This is not what black wants, I'm sure of it.
However, the move suggested by chess engines doesn't seem much "prettier" } (11... h6 12. Be3 g5 { The bishop on g7 isn't any better off. Black is short on counter play, cramped, and rather passive, white could consider taking up residence on the queenside as well } 13. b4 { " Note that while our Botvinnik set-up does leave the f-pawn free to advance, we are by no means committed to kingside play, but can also advance on the queenside and sometimes even in the centre. " - by GM Simon Williams }) 12. Be3 Ne7 { [%cal Gd3d4,Gg3g4,Gd1b3] } 13. Nxe7+ { GM Simon Williams remembers being tempted to play } (13. g4) (13. d4!? { which both looked good for white followed by Qb3 }) 13... Qxe7 14. Qd2 g5? { black has wrongly reacted to a potential threat that he shouldn't have.
While it is an active plan of white to remove black's "active" bishop on g7, however here the bishop is enclosed by it's own pawns. Where it's more beneficial to white to leave the piece exactly where it is. } { [%csl Rf6,Rg5,Re5,Yh6] } (14... c6 { was a better try for some counter play, } 15. g4 Nc7 16. Ng3 { but here white maintains his control of space and may even deploy on the h-file }) (14... c5 15. g4 a6 16. h4! b5 17. Ng3 Rb8 18. g5 { is a very instructive line. Taking on f5 or g5 doesn't look good for black. His is in trouble in any case ... } 18... Nc7 19. Rf2 Rb7 20. Raf1 { the tension rises on f6 } 20... Ne8 21. fxg6 { once all of whites forces are "perfectly" placed } 21... hxg6 22. h5 { Rips open lines } 22... fxg5 (22... gxh5 23. g6 { where Nxh5 or Nf5 and Qe2 follow } { [%csl Rh5][%cal Gg3h5,Gg3f5,Gd2e2] }) 23. Rxf8+ Bxf8 24. hxg6 Qg7 25. Qf2 Be7 26. Nf5 Bxf5 27. Qxf5 { "and Black collapses on the light squares." }) 15. g4! { Preventing any notion of ...h5 while freeing g3 for the knight. } { [%cal Rh7h5,Ge2g3] } 15... Rf7 16. h4! { "It was again possible to switch focus with 16.b4 but with Black so tied up, Simon couldn't resist involving Harry (h-file, pawn move)" . } 16... h6 (16... gxh4? 17. Rf3 { will regain the pawn with a mighty attack after Rh3. }) 17. hxg5 hxg5 (17... fxg5 { might have put up a slightly better fight, however white still looks great after } 18. Bf3 { [%csl Gf3] } 18... Nf6 19. Ng3 { "The bolder ones amongst you will look at ideas of Kg2, Rh1, and sacrifice on g5 here; the calmer ones will notice how tied up Black is, so why not open a second front on the queenside as White?" - by GM Simon Williams }) 18. Kf2 { This move isn't a super GM move where black is centralizing his king in preparation for the endgame, but instead is making room for his rook(s) to make it to the Harry file! (h-file) } 18... c6 19. Rh1 Nc7 20. Rh3 Rff8 21. Rah1 Kf7 22. Ng3 { Is it just me, or does anyone else believe that black is as passive as they come?? NONE of his attacking pieces are past the 2 rank!,
Per, GM Williams, "This is the dream Botvinnik position" White's pieces are similar (in a way) due to the nature of the position, but he has more initiative and complete control of the open h-file, scope with his dark square bishop, control of the center's light squares and room for his knight to enter black's camp with one useful pounce. Black is what we like to call "toast" lol...
"Simon recalls he was very happy here, and no wonder!"
- Williams-Locatelli, Cappelle-la-Grande Open 1995, concluded: } 22... Rh8 23. Nh5! { Keeping black basking in his own problems... } { [%csl Gh5] } 23... Bh6 24. d4 { Opening more lines, however, it's hard to image how a GM of his caliber could have resisted playing } (24. Nxf6! { and "if" } { [%csl Gf6] } 24... Qxf6 25. Rxh6 Rxh6 26. Bxg5 { mic drops, curtains fall, I'm out! }) 24... c5 { out of ideas.. } (24... exd4 25. Bxd4 Ne8 26. Nf4! { would have been a neat way of "sealing the deal" }) (24... Bg7 { doesn't stop } 25. Nxf6 Bxf6 (25... Qxf6 26. Bxg5 { squanders the queen }) 26. Rh7+ Bg7 27. Bxg5 Qe8 28. f6 { winning material and the game } { [%csl Gf6,Gh7][%cal Gh7g7,Gf6g7] }) 25. dxc5 dxc5 26. Nxf6! { "Simon was never going to miss this for long." } { [%cal Gd2d7,Gh3h6] } 26... Bxf5 27. exf5 Rad8 28. Nd5 e4 29. Nxe7 Rxd2+ 30. Bxd2 { Andrea resigned, white has complete control of the board, and a decisive material advantage } 1-0


