1. c4 f5 2. g3 Nf6 3. Bg2 g6 (3... e6 4. Nf3 Be7 (4... d5 { [%anno "Mikae5", mikae5] Stonewall, the most fighting chance for black } 5. O-O c6 6. d3 Bd6 (6... Be7 7. Nc3 O-O 8. e4! d4 9. Ne2 fxe4 10. dxe4 d3 11. Nf4 Nxe4 12. Nxd3) 7. Nc3 O-O 8. e4! { [%anno "Mikae5", mikae5] The go-to pawn break to refute the Stonewall against the English. Although it is a pawn sacrifice, Black's pawn structure is permanently damaged. } 8... fxe4 9. dxe4 Nxe4 (9... dxe4 10. Ng5) 10. Nxe4 dxe4 11. Ng5 $16) 5. O-O O-O 6. Nc3 d6 { This move signals the Classical Dutch instead of the Stonewall. One of the differences between the Classical Dutch and this position is White's d-pawn has not been pushed yet! This makes the standard Ne4 maneuver by BLack useless. } { [%cal Gb2b4,Ga2a4,Gg2a8] } 7. b4 { Scoring well in practice. Instead of fighting in the center, White immediately expands on the queenside. Now, Black is virtually forced to push the e-pawn again, losing a tempo. } 7... e5 8. d3 { Transposing back to a very common c4 e5 line with a loss of tempo for Black. } 8... Qe8 9. b5 Nbd7 10. a4 { Once White's a-pawn reaches a6, it activates White's Bg2, which turns into a monster. }) 4. Nc3 Bg7 5. d3 O-O 6. e4! { [%anno "Mikae5", mikae5] Against the Leningrad, we play a Botvinnik type of setup. } 6... d6 (6... fxe4?! 7. dxe4 d6 8. Nge2 e5 9. O-O c5 10. h3 Nc6 { [%csl Ge3] } 11. Be3) 7. Nge2 e5 8. O-O Nc6 9. Nd5! { [%anno "Mikae5", mikae5] The key move here. The Nd5 + Bg5 combo is essential to face this ambitious line. Often, you don't play the typical f4 break because the pawn structure is too symmetrical to fight for an advantage as White on the kingside/center. } (9. h3? f4! 10. gxf4 Nh5 11. fxe5 (11. f5 gxf5 12. exf5 Bxf5) 11... dxe5 12. f4 exf4 (12... Qh4) 13. Nxf4 Bd4+ 14. Kh2 Nxf4 15. Bxf4 Rxf4) 9... Be6 (9... Nxd5 10. cxd5 Ne7 11. Be3 c6 12. dxc6 bxc6 13. Rc1) 10. Bg5! Qd7 11. Qd2 (11. Kh1) 11... Rae8 12. f3 { [%anno "Mikae5", mikae5] Keeping the center stable so White can focus on queenside expansion. } 12... Rf7 13. b4 *


