1. c4 e5 { The most principled response } 2. Nc3 (2. g3!? { Against both g3 setups, black can take a c6 approach to prepare d5 } 2... Nc6 (2... d5!? { [%anno "Mikae5", mikae5] Resembles a reversed Hyperaccelerated Dragon } 3. cxd5 Qxd5 4. Nf3 Nc6 { [%csl Gd4,Gd5,Gc6,Ge5,Ge2,Gd2][%cal Gd5d4,Ge5d4,Gc6d4,Gd2d4] } 5. Nc3 Qd8 6. Bg2 Nf6 7. O-O h6 8. d3 Bd6 { Ding, Liren - Carlsen, Magnus, 0-1, Chessable Masters Final, 2020, https://lichess.org/IfUbjn7n } 9. Nd2 (9. a3 a5 { [%csl Yb4,Ga5] } 10. b3 O-O 11. Bb2 Re8 12. Rc1 Bf5 13. h3 Qd7 14. Kh2 Nd4! { [%cal Gf3d4,Ge5d4,Gc7c5] } 15. Nd2 c6 16. e3 Nb5 17. Nxb5 cxb5 18. Nf3 Rad8 19. e4 Be6 20. Bxe5 Bxe5 21. Nxe5 Qd6 22. f4 Qxa3 23. d4 Bxb3 { Ding, Liren - Carlsen, Magnus, 0-1, Chessable Masters Final, 2020, https://lichess.org/IfUbjn7n } { [%csl Ga5,Gb5] }) 9... O-O 10. Nc4 Be6 11. Nxd6 cxd6 12. Bd2 d5) (2... Nf6 { The main move, influencing the center } 3. Bg2 (3. Nc3 d5 4. cxd5 Nxd5 5. Bg2 Nb6 { [%anno "Mikae5", mikae5] Transposes to a Reversed Dragon, see below. }) 3... d5 4. cxd5 Nxd5 { [%anno "Mikae5", mikae5] Transposes to the Reversed Dragon (s.u.), which is a major tabiya in the English. White has many plans: Postponing the development of either knights, develop the bishop to b2 or e3 etc. } 5. Nc3 (5. Nf3 Nc6 6. O-O Nb6 7. Nc3 Be7) 5... Nb6 6. Nf3 (6. e3 Nc6 7. Nge2 Be7 8. O-O O-O 9. f4 f6 10. f5 Bd7 11. d4 Be8 12. d5 Nb8 13. d6 cxd6 14. Bxb7 Bc6 15. Bxc6 (15. Bxa8 Bxa8 $15) 15... Nxc6 $10) 6... Nc6 { [%anno "Mikae5", mikae5] Many development schemes for white here, see transposition below. }) 3. Nc3 (3. Bg2 Bc5!? 4. Nc3 a6) 3... Bc5!? 4. Bg2 a6 { [%anno "Mikae5", mikae5] Transposition to the Serbian System seen below. }) (2. Nf3!? { A specialty of GM Rakhmanov, leading to a reversed Alekhine sort of position } 2... e4 (2... Nc6 3. Nc3 Nf6 { [%anno "Mikae5", mikae5] Transposition. }) 3. Nd4 Nc6 4. e3 (4. Nc2 { Just retreating } 4... d5 5. cxd5 Qxd5 { The queen seems vulnerable but is quite active and is only vulnerable to one tempo loss } 6. Nc3 Qe5 { White should play d4 to gain a tempo and remove the annoying e4 pawn } 7. d4 (7. g3?! Nf6 8. Bg2 Bf5 { [%csl Gf5,Ge5,Gf6,Gc6][%cal Ge8c8] } 9. O-O (9. Ne3 O-O-O 10. Nxf5 Qxf5 11. O-O h5) 9... O-O-O 10. Ne3 (10. a3 h5 11. b4 h4 12. b5 (12. Ne3 hxg3 13. Nxf5 gxf2+ 14. Rxf2 Ng4 15. d4 Qxh2+ 16. Kf1 Nxf2) 12... hxg3 13. hxg3 Bc5 { [%csl Rg3] } 14. e3 Bg4 15. Qe1 Qh5) 10... Be6! { [%cal Gh7h5,Gg7g5,Gh5h4] } (10... h5 11. Nxf5 Qxf5 12. Qa4 h4 13. Bxe4 Nxe4 14. Nxe4 (14. Qxe4 Qh3 15. Qg2 Qe6) 14... Nd4 { [%csl Ge2,Ge4][%cal Gd4e2,Gf5e4] }) 11. Qa4? (11. a3 h5 12. h4 g5 13. hxg5 Qxg5) 11... Rd4 12. Qb5 Bc5) 7... exd3 8. Qxd3 Nf6 { Black has easy play } 9. e4 Nb4 10. Nxb4 Bxb4 { And e4 is hanging because of the pin now } { [%csl Re4] } 11. Qb5+ { Clearly trading pieces is not a problem for black } 11... Qxb5 12. Bxb5+ Bd7 13. Bd3 (13. Bxd7+ Nxd7 $15 { Black's pieces jump to life } { [%cal Be8c8,Gd7e5] }) 13... O-O-O 14. f3 Be6 15. Be2 Nd7 $15 { The knight travels to e5 or c5 and has some weak squares to work with } { [%cal Bd7e5,Yd7c5] }) (4. Nxc6?! { Has been tried but it is not very impressive and black has easy development } 4... dxc6 5. Nc3 { More logical } (5. e3 { Weakening the d3 square } { [%csl Rd3] } 5... Nf6 6. h3 { Preventing Ng4 or Bg4 } 6... h5! { Gaining some space and preparing Bf5 without allowing g4 } 7. Qb3 (7. d4 exd3 8. Bxd3 Be6 $15 { With either Nd7-e5 or Qd7 and 0-0-0. Black has a great position } { [%cal Gf6d7,Gd7e5,Bd8d7,Be8c8] }) 7... Nd7 $17 { Preparing Nc5 to abuse the d3 square } { [%csl Rd3][%cal Bd7c5] } (7... h4 8. Nc3 b6 9. Qc2 Bf5 { Black is doing very well } { Rakhmanov, Ale - Alsina Leal, D., 1-0, TCh-ESP Div 1 2016, https://lichess.org/8SiZ758o })) 5... Nf6 6. e3 { White is preparing Qc2, b3-Bb2 } { [%cal Gb2b3,Gc1b2,Bd1c2] } (6. g3 Bc5 7. Bg2 O-O 8. O-O Re8 9. Na4 Bf8) 6... Bd6 7. h3 Bf5 { Supporting the pawn } 8. Qc2 (8. g4?! { Too weakening } 8... Bg6 9. Bg2 Qe7 10. Qc2 O-O-O { Not caring about the e4 pawn dropping } 11. g5 Nd7 12. Bxe4 Bb4! $17 { With the threat of Nc5-d3 and white is stuck with many weaknesses } { [%csl Rg5][%cal Bd7c5,Bc5d3] }) 8... Qe7 { Supporting the pawn and preparing 0-0-0 } 9. b3 O-O-O 10. Bb2 Bg6 { Prophylaxis against f4 because black prepares to play Be5 now } { [%cal Bd6e5] } 11. O-O-O Be5 { Improving the bishop } 12. d4 exd3 13. Bxd3 Rxd3 14. Rxd3 Re8 { Still keeping white thinking about the pin } 15. Qe2 Bxd3 16. Qxd3 b6 $17 { Black has a lot of play in this position } { [%cal Bg7g6,Ga7a5,Ye5c3,Rf6e4,Gc8b7] }) 4... Nf6 { Just developing } 5. Nc3 { Makes most sense } (5. Nxc6 { Leads to an extraneous option } 5... dxc6 6. d4 exd3 7. Bxd3 { White gets a favorable pawn structure but black's pieces get out very easily } 7... Bb4+ { The first problem arises as no piece can block on d2 as d3 will hang } 8. Nc3 Bxc3+ 9. bxc3 Be6 $17 { White also has a poor pawn structure and black has a nice game ahead } { [%cal Bf6d7,Bd7e5,Yd8e7,Ge8c8,Ye7f6] }) 5... Nxd4 6. exd4 d5! 7. cxd5 (7. c5?! { Overextended } 7... b6 8. b4 a5 $15) (7. Qb3 { Trying to increase the pressure } 7... Bd6 8. cxd5 (8. c5 Bf4 9. g3 Bh6) 8... O-O $15 { White has tripled pawns which is not good } { [%csl Rd4,Rd5,Rd2] } 9. Be2 Re8 10. O-O Ng4!) (7. d3 exd3 8. Bxd3 dxc4 9. Bxc4 { Leaving white with an isolated pawn } { [%csl Rd4] } 9... Bd6 10. O-O O-O { Looks like a QGA position which is fine for black } 11. Bg5 h6 12. Bh4 c6 { Covering the d5 square } 13. Re1 { Increasing the pressure } (13. d5 { Leads to many exchanges } 13... cxd5 14. Nxd5 g5 15. Bg3 Bxg3 16. hxg3 (16. fxg3!? { Trying to get funky } 16... Nxd5 17. Bxd5 Be6 { Trading more pieces } 18. Bxe6 Qb6+ 19. Kh1 Qxe6 $10 { Clearly black is fine here }) 16... Be6 $10 { Completely fine and equal }) 13... g5 14. Bg3 Bxg3 15. hxg3 (15. fxg3!? Bf5 $15 { Black can consolidate with Bg6 } { [%cal Gf5g6] }) 15... Bf5 $10 { Black has easy play with Qd6, Rae8, Bg6, Kg7 and a completely healthy position } { [%cal Gf5g6,Bd8d6,Ba8d8,Yg8g7] }) 7... Nxd5 8. d3 (8. Nxe4?! { Walks into a pin } 8... Qe7 9. Qe2 { Halting white's development } 9... Bf5 10. d3 O-O-O $17 { Black's lead in development is massive } { [%cal Gg7g6,Gf8g7,Ge7d7] }) 8... exd3 9. Bxd3 Be6 $10 { Black has simple play with Be7 and 0-0 } { [%cal Gf8e7,Be8g8] }) (2. d3 { Quite passive } 2... Nf6 3. Nf3 Nc6 4. a3 { Ready to go into a Reversed Najdorf } (4. Nc3 d5 5. cxd5 Nxd5 { Reaches a reversed Classical Sicilian which is fine for black } 6. e4 { Critical } (6. e3 Nxc3 { Simple } 7. bxc3 Bd6 8. Be2 O-O 9. O-O b6 { With Bb7, Re8, Qf6-g6 and a very reasonable position } { [%cal Bc8b7,Gf8e8,Yf6g6,Yd8f6] }) (6. g3 { A classical Sicilian + Dragon hybrid } 6... Be7 { Developing classically } 7. Bg2 Be6 8. O-O O-O 9. Bd2 Qd7 { Looks very nice for black where the tempo doesn't matter much } 10. Rc1 f6 11. Ne4 { Preparing Nc5 } 11... Rfd8 { Just developing } 12. Nc5 Bxc5 13. Rxc5 b6 { Kicking the rook back } 14. Rc1 a5 { Expanding on the queenside with quite a harmonious position }) 6... Nf6 { Just playing positionally } 7. h3 { Preventing Bg4 } 7... Bc5 { Taking over the diagonal } 8. Be3 Qe7 { Black doesn't want to give white the whole center by taking on e3 so Qe7 is a useful move and this also vacates the d8 square for black's rook } { [%csl Gd8] } 9. Be2 O-O 10. O-O Rd8 { Black has an excellent setup } 11. Rc1 Nd4 12. Bxd4 Bxd4 13. Nxd4 Rxd4 14. Qd2 c5 15. f4 b6 16. Qe3 exf4 17. Rxf4 Be6 $15 { Edouard, Romain - Wojtaszek, Radoslaw, 0-1, Biel rapid 53rd, 2020, https://lichess.org/eaca0Dqp } { With an easy plan of doubling on the d-file, and playing Nd7-e5 with a better position } { [%csl Gd4,Ga8][%cal Ga8d8,Bf6d7,Bd7e5] }) 4... d5 5. cxd5 Nxd5 6. e4 { Critical } (6. e3 Bd6 { Just developing the bishop on a good diagonal in the future if it opens up } 7. b4 O-O 8. Bb2 Re8 9. Be2 a5 { Typical undermining of the queenside } 10. b5 Na7 11. a4 c6 12. bxc6 Nxc6 { With nice control over the b4 square } { [%csl Bb4] }) 6... Nf6 { Again the knight goes to f6 like usual just like in the Reversed Classical Sicilian }) (2. e3!? { Tried by Carlsen a few times } 2... Nf6 3. Nc3 (3. a3 d5 4. cxd5 Nxd5 { Leads to a reversed Kan Sicilian } 5. Nf3 (5. Nc3 Nxc3 6. bxc3 { Reaching a position where white has played a not so useful a3 move } 6... Bd6 { Developing } 7. d4 O-O { White is underdeveloped and black has a great position. Black can even play c5 and Nc6 because the knight isn't on c6 yet } { [%cal Gb8c6,Bc7c5] })) (3. d4 { Not very threatening } 3... exd4 4. exd4 d5 { Eventually white is going to be left with an isolated d4 pawn } { [%csl Rd4] } 5. Nc3 Bb4 6. Nf3 O-O { Reaching a QGA like position which is completely fine for black and I like to play against the IQP after dxc4 } { [%csl Rd4] } 7. Be2 dxc4 8. Bxc4 Bg4 { Pinning the knight } 9. O-O Nc6 { Clearly with a very healthy position for black } 10. Be3 Qd6 11. a3) 3... Nc6 { Waiting to see what white wants to do } 4. Qb3!? { Preventing d5 } (4. a3 d5 5. cxd5 Nxd5 { Reaches a reversed Kan Sicilian } 6. Qc2 Nxc3 7. bxc3 Qg5 { The queen looks a little bit awkward on g5 because of Nf3, but the idea is to trade on g6 } 8. Nf3 Qg6 9. Qxg6 (9. d3 { Avoiding the exchange } 9... Be7 { Black just develops } 10. Be2 O-O 11. O-O b6 $15 { With an easy game ahead } { [%cal Bc8b7,Ga8d8,Yf7f5] }) 9... hxg6 { With an open h-file and a solid position to work with }) (4. d4 Bb4) (4. Nd5 Bc5 5. Ne2 Nxd5 6. cxd5 Ne7 7. Nc3 O-O) 4... Bb4 { As d5 is no longer possible, black switches plans } 5. Nge2 (5. Nd5 Bc5 6. Ne2 O-O 7. Nec3 Nxd5 8. Nxd5 Ne7) 5... O-O 6. a3 Bxc3 7. Nxc3 Ne7 $146 { Just preparing c6 & d5 } { [%cal Bc7c6,Bd7d5] } 8. Be2 c6 9. O-O { White is ready to respond to d5 with d4, so a waiting move is sensible } 9... Re8 { A useful move to support the e-pawn, and d5 is coming next if white does nothing } { [%cal Gd7d5] } 10. d4 exd4 11. exd4 d5 { And recapturing on d5 with a knight would be a dream } { [%cal Be7d5] } 12. Bg5 (12. c5 Nf5 { Clearly something has gone wrong for white with the knight jumping to life } { [%cal Bf6e4] }) 12... Nf5 13. Rfd1 h6 14. Bf4 Qe7 15. cxd5 Nxd5 16. Be5 (16. Nxd5 Qxe2 17. Nc7 Nxd4 18. Qb4)) (2. b3!? { A very rare move } 2... Ne7!? $146 { Much more clever! The idea is to offer a possible gambit } 3. Nf3 (3. Bb2 Nbc6 4. Nc3 d5 5. cxd5 Nxd5 6. e3 (6. Nxd5 Qxd5 7. e3 Bf5 8. a3 Na5) 6... Nxc3) 3... Nbc6 4. Bb2 d5! { Sacrificing e5 } 5. cxd5 (5. Nxe5?! { Taking the pawn is dangerous } 5... Nxe5 6. Bxe5 d4 { And the knight is ready to come to c6 or g6 next } { [%cal Ye7g6,Ge7c6] } 7. e3 Nc6 8. Bxd4 (8. Bg3 h5! 9. h4 Qf6 { With problems against the rook } { [%csl Ra1] } 10. Qf3 Qg6 $17 { With tremendous activity for the pawn } { [%cal Rc6b4,Rc8g4,Yf8b4] }) 8... Nxd4 9. exd4 Qxd4 10. Nc3 Be6 { Just preparing 0-0-0 next with an excellent game } { [%cal Be8c8] }) 5... Nxd5 6. Nxe5 Nxe5 7. Bxe5 Qe7 8. Bb2 Nb4 9. d3 Bf5) 2... Nf6 { [%cal Gd7d5] } (2... Nc6!? { [%anno "Mikae5", mikae5] Serbian System against everything but Nf3 which transposes to the Four Knights. } 3. g3 (3. Nf3 Nf6 { [%anno "Mikae5", mikae5] Transposes to the four knights. } (3... g6 { Also possible, very complex position & not so much theory as there are many possible move orders & theory hasn't been established yet }) (3... f5!? { A reversed Grand-Prix, quite risky though! } 4. d4) (3... Bc5?! 4. Nxe5 Bxf2+ 5. Kxf2 Nxe5 6. d4 Ng4+ 7. Ke1 d5 8. cxd5 Qf6 9. Ne4 Qf5 10. Qd3 (10. e3) 10... N8f6)) 3... Bc5! { This move is only possible if white plays g3 and doesn't include Nf3 because of Nxe5 and d4 trick } 4. Bg2 a6! { [%cal Gc5a7,Ge2e3,Gg1f3] } 5. e3 (5. Nf3 d6 6. O-O (6. d3 Ba7 7. a3 h6 { [%csl Bg5][%cal Gg8f6,Yc1g5] }) 6... Nge7! { [%anno "Mikae5", mikae5] Der Schlüsselzug. } { [%csl Gf5,Bc6] } 7. e3 { [%csl Ge3,Gf3] } (7. d3 { [%csl Gd3,Gf3] } 7... O-O { [%cal Gf7f5,Gc5a7,Gf5f4,Yh7h6,Yc8e6,Yd8d7] }) 7... Ba7 8. d4 (8. h3!? { [%csl Bg4] } 8... O-O 9. d4 (9. g4? f5) 9... exd4 10. exd4 (10. Nxd4 Nxd4 11. exd4 Nf5 12. d5?? { [%csl Ga7