1. c4 e5 { The most principled response } 2. Nc3 (2. g3!? { Against both g3 setups, black can take a c6 approach to prepare d5 } 2... Nc6 (2... d5!? { Resembles a reversed Hyperaccelerated Dragon } 3. cxd5 Qxd5 4. Nf3 Nc6 { [%csl Gd4,Gd5,Gc6,Ge5,Ge2,Gd2][%cal Gd5d4,Ge5d4,Gc6d4,Gd2d4] } 5. Nc3 Qd8 6. Bg2 Nf6 7. O-O h6 8. d3 Bd6 { Ding, Liren - Carlsen, Magnus, 0-1, Chessable Masters Final, 2020, https://lichess.org/IfUbjn7n } 9. Nd2 (9. a3 a5 { [%csl Yb4,Ga5] } 10. b3 O-O 11. Bb2 Re8 12. Rc1 Bf5 13. h3 Qd7 14. Kh2 Nd4! { [%cal Gf3d4,Ge5d4,Gc7c5] } 15. Nd2 c6 16. e3 Nb5 17. Nxb5 cxb5 18. Nf3 Rad8 19. e4 Be6 20. Bxe5 Bxe5 21. Nxe5 Qd6 22. f4 Qxa3 23. d4 Bxb3 { Ding, Liren - Carlsen, Magnus, 0-1, Chessable Masters Final, 2020, https://lichess.org/IfUbjn7n } { [%csl Ga5,Gb5] }) 9... O-O 10. Nc4 Be6 11. Nxd6 cxd6 12. Bd2 d5) (2... Nf6 { The main move, influencing the center } 3. Bg2 (3. Nc3 d5 (3... c6 4. d4 { Principled, like in the Alapin } (4. Nf3 { Allowing the advancement of the pawn } 4... e4 5. Nd4 Qb6 { Harassing the knight } 6. Nb3 (6. Nc2 { Also natural } 6... d5 7. cxd5 cxd5 8. Bg2 Nc6 { Just developing harmoniously } 9. O-O Be7 10. d3 O-O 11. dxe4 (11. Rb1!? { Preparing to play with b4 } { [%cal Gb2b4] } 11... Rd8 { Lining up the queen and rook } 12. b4 Bg4 { Putting some pressure on e2 with a healthy position }) 11... dxe4) (6. e3 { Defending the knight but causing many weaknesses on d3 and f3 } { [%csl Rd3,Rf3] } 6... d5 7. cxd5 cxd5 8. d3 Bb4 9. Bg2 Nc6 { Hoping to trade white's best piece, the knight on d4 } 10. O-O Bxc3 11. bxc3 O-O { Looks completely fine for black, black has ideas to play Ne5 or put some pressure on c3 } { [%csl Rc3][%cal Bf8e8,Gc6e5] }) 6... a5 { Threatening a4 } { [%cal Ra5a4] } 7. d3 (7. Na4)) 4... exd4 5. Qxd4 d5 { And if white takes on d5 ever, then the c6 square will be open for the knight and white has no convenient response with Bb5 for example as white has committed to g3 already } { [%cal Yb8c6] } 6. Bg2 (6. Nf3 Na6 { Preparing Nb4 or Bc5 with tempo } { [%cal Ya6b4] } 7. Bg2 Bc5 8. Qe5+ (8. Qh4 O-O 9. O-O dxc4 10. Qxc4 Be6 { Black has a very healthy position with moves such as h6, Nd5, Re8, Bb6 and activating the a6 knight easily } { [%cal Bf6d5,Gh7h6,Bf8e8,Yc5b6,Ga6c5] })) 6... Be6 { Threatening to capture on c4 } 7. cxd5 (7. Nf3?! { Allowing the capture on c4 } 7... dxc4 8. Qxd8+ Kxd8 9. Ng5 (9. O-O Nbd7 10. Rd1 a5 { Gaining space on the queenside } 11. Nd4 Bc5 12. Na4 Ba7 13. Bf4 Ke7 { Black has a perfectly healthy position and is up a pawn } 14. Rac1 Rhe8 15. Nxe6 fxe6 $15 { And the c4 pawn cannot be taken on account of b5 } { [%csl Yc4][%cal Rb7b5] }) 9... Nbd7 10. O-O Bd6 $17 { With Kc7 next and black will remain up a pawn for the rest of the game } { [%cal Bd8c7] }) 7... cxd5 { Black gets in Nc6 without having to face Bb5 } { [%cal Bb8c6] }) 4. cxd5 Nxd5 5. Bg2 Nb6 { Transposes to a Reversed Dragon, see below. }) 3... d5 (3... c6 { Fabiano Caruana is a big expert in this line } 4. d4 (4. Nf3 { Allowing e4 in Alapin fashion } 4... e4 5. Nd4 d5 6. cxd5 { Most logical } (6. d3 { Undermining black's center, black should be fine if they know the next move } 6... Bc5 { The mainline, provoking Nb3 and only then giving a check on b4 } (6... exd3 { Alternative } 7. Qxd3 (7. cxd5 Bb4+ { Leads to incredible complications after } 8. Nc3 c5 9. Nb3 c4 10. Nd2 O-O 11. O-O Bxc3 12. bxc3 Bg4 13. f3 Nxd5! { A deep Sacrifice } (13... dxe2?! 14. Qxe2 Bf5 15. Nxc4 Qxd5 16. Rd1 $16 { Ding Liren - Inarkiev, E., 1-0, Moscow Grand Prix 2017, https://lichess.org/fR9pkrmf }) 14. fxg4 Nxc3 15. Qe1 Nxe2+ 16. Kh1 c3 17. Ba3 { Leads to white getting the initiative } (17. Bxb7? Nd7 18. Nb3 Rb8 19. Bf3 Re8 20. Qf2 Nxc1 21. Raxc1 Rc8 $17 { [%cal Bd7e5] }) 17... Re8 18. Qf2 { Threatening f7 } { [%csl Rf7] } 18... Qd7 19. Ne4 Nc6 20. Nc5 Qd6! { Only move, but this is crazy to step into the discovery } 21. Qxf7+ Kh8 22. Qf5 Qh6 (22... d2? 23. Ne4 { With some Ng5 threats } { [%csl Ra3][%cal Ge4g5] }) 23. Qxd3 Re3 24. Bc1! Nxg3+ 25. Kg1 Nxf1 26. Qxf1 Nd4 27. Kh1 Nc2 28. Bxe3 Nxe3 29. Qf3 Nxg2 30. Kxg2 Qd2+ 31. Kh1 c2 32. Nb3 Qd6 33. Rf1 h6 $13 { Black has decent chances to hold this endgame due to the strong c2 pawn but it's still very unpleasant I feel }) (7. exd3 Bg4 8. Qb3 Bc5 9. Qxb7 Bxd4 10. Qxa8 Qe7+ 11. Be3 O-O $19) 7... dxc4 8. Qxc4 Nbd7 { Isn't a disaster, but I'd say slightly unpleasant }) (6... Qb6 { Does not equalize according to practice } 7. Nb3 (7. dxe4 Bc5 8. e3 dxe4 9. O-O O-O 10. Nc3 $14 { Shankland, S. - Piorun, K., 1/2-1/2, 52nd Capablanca Mem Elite, 2017, https://lichess.org/uJa5RVvt } { With the e4 pawn being quite weak } { [%csl Re4] }) 7... dxc4 8. dxc4 Qb4+ 9. Nc3 (9. N1d2 e3!) 9... Be7 10. O-O O-O (10... Qxc4 11. Nd2 $14 { The point, and the position isn't fun to play as black } { [%cal Gd2e4] }) 11. Qc2 Qxc4 12. Nd2 Qe6 13. Ndxe4 Nxe4 14. Bxe4 $14 { Nihal, Sarin - Inarkiev, E., 1/2-1/2, TCh-TUR Super League 2019, https://lichess.org/BFzAhOAh } { Quite unpleasant as black's queenside is not developed and white's pieces are so active }) 7. Nb3 (7. dxe4?! dxe4 8. e3 O-O 9. O-O h5 $15 $36 { Dangerous for white }) 7... Bb4+ (7... Bb6 { Doesn't equalize } 8. cxd5 Qxd5 9. Nc3 Qf5 { Threatening f2 } { [%csl Rf2] } 10. O-O exd3 11. e4 { A great move, just gaining space in the center } 11... Qh5 12. Qxh5 Nxh5 13. Na4 $14 { With a very unpleasant position } { [%cal Gf1d1,Ya4c5,Yc5d3] }) 8. Bd2 a5! { More active of an option } (8... Bxd2+ { An alternative } 9. Qxd2 exd3 10. cxd5 { Critical } (10. Qxd3 dxc4 11. Qxc4 O-O { Black is doing well in development with a fine position } { [%cal Bd8e7,Gc8f5,Gc8e6] }) 10... dxe2 11. Qxe2+ Qe7 12. Qxe7+ Kxe7 13. Nc3 cxd5 { The computer claims this endgame to be slightly better because of the pressure that the g2 bishop exerts, but black should be able to hold this }) 9. cxd5 (9. O-O exd3 10. Bxb4 axb4 11. Qxd3 dxc4 12. Qxc4 Na6 { With Be6 next and the opening of the a-file favors black for sure } { [%cal Bc8e6] }) 9... exd3 10. Bxb4 { Critical } (10. a3 Be7 { Keeping the bishops on the board } 11. Nc3 O-O $10 { Black will get an isolated pawn on d5, but so will white } { [%cal Ge2d3,Bc6d5] }) 10... axb4 11. dxc6 (11. Qxd3 cxd5 12. O-O O-O { The pawn on b4 shows it's role as being very important as it stops Nc3 to put pressure on the d5 pawn, and black as a result has a fine position } { [%csl Bb4] } 13. N1d2 Nc6 { With Re8 and Bg4 next, compensating for the isolated d-pawn } { [%cal Bf8e8,Gc8g4] }) 11... Nxc6 12. Qxd3 Qxd3 13. exd3 O-O $10 { The extra pawn doesn't matter because the d3 pawn is weak, and the a-file is open which puts significant pressure on white's position. } { [%cal Ga8a1] } 14. N1d2 (14. a3 Re8+ 15. Kd2 Be6 16. Bxc6 bxc6 17. Nd4 Bd5 18. Re1 Red8 { Black has tremendous activity for the pawn }) (14. O-O Rd8 15. N1d2 Rxd3 16. Nc4 { Optically it seems that white is far more active, but black also has their fair share of activity } 16... Be6 17. Nb6 Ra7 { Keeping an eye on the a2 pawn and black is doing completely fine } { [%csl Ra2] } 18. Nc5 Rd2) 14... Ne5 { Attacking the d3 pawn } 15. Nc5 Rd8 16. Ndb3 Nxd3+ 17. Nxd3 Rxd3 18. O-O g6 19. Rfc1 Kg7 20. Bf1 Rd6 21. Rc4 Rb6 $10 { The weak a2 pawn compensates for the doubled pawns and so this position is fine for black } { [%csl Ra2][%cal Gc8e6] }) 6... Qb6 7. Nb3 { Critical } (7. Nc2 cxd5 8. d3 Nc6 9. O-O Be7 10. Nc3 O-O 11. dxe4 dxe4 12. Nxe4 Nxe4 13. Bxe4 Rd8 14. Qe1 Bh3 15. Bg2 Bxg2 16. Kxg2 Rac8 (16... Qb5)) (7. e3 { Just defending the knight, but this causes many light-squared weaknesses } { [%csl Rd3,Re2,Rf3] } 7... cxd5 { With Be7, 0-0, Nc6, and black has a very fine position } { [%cal Bf8e7,Ge8g8,Gb8c6] } (7... Bc5 8. Nb3 cxd5 9. Nc3 Bb4 { Also possible })) (7. Qa4!? { Trying to win some material in the complications } 7... Bc5 8. dxc6 O-O 9. e3 Rd8 { White is very much underdeveloped and in trouble } { [%csl Rc1,Rb1,Rd4][%cal Gb8c6] }) 7... a5 { Threatening a4 to trap the knight } { [%cal Ra5a4] } 8. a4 { Creates a big weakness on b4 } { [%csl Rb4] } (8. d3 a4) 8... cxd5 (8... Nxd5! { Sacrificing the e4 pawn } 9. O-O (9. Bxe4?! { Allows } 9... Nf6 { Forcing the bishop to an awkward square } 10. Bc2 (10. Bg2?? Be6 { Wins the b3 knight } { [%csl Rb3] }) 10... Bh3 { Some technical problems }) 9... Be6 { Most dynamic } 10. Bxe4 Nf6 (10... f5!? { Leads to some very interesting complications } 11. Bxf5 (11. Bc2?? Nb4 $19 { [%csl Rc2,Rb3] }) (11. Bg2?? Nf6 $19 { b3 falls } { [%csl Rb3] }) 11... Bxf5 12. e4 Be6 13. exd5 Bxd5 14. Re1+ Kf7 15. Re3 Nd7 16. d4 Bb4 $13 { With a very difficult position to assess } { [%cal Bh8e8] }) 11. Bc2)) (4. Nc3 { Gives black a very comfortable game } 4... d5 5. cxd5 cxd5 6. Qb3 { Leads to some interesting complications } (6. d3 { Too complacent } 6... d4 7. Ne4 Nxe4 8. Bxe4 Be7 9. Bd2 Na6 { Preparing Nc5 } { [%cal Ba6c5] } 10. Bg2 O-O 11. Nf3 f6 12. O-O Be6 $15 { And white is fighting for equality } { [%cal Bd8d7] }) 6... Nc6 7. Nxd5 Nd4 8. Nxf6+ Qxf6 9. Qd1 Bf5 10. d3 Rc8 11. Kf1 (11. Rb1 Bb4+ 12. Kf1 (12. Bd2 Bxd2+ 13. Qxd2 Rc2 14. Qd1 (14. Qe3 Rxe2+ $19 { [%cal Rd4c2] }) 14... Qa6 15. e3 Bxd3 16. exd4 Qc4 { Threatening Qb4 with mate coming } { [%cal Rc4b4] } 17. a3 Qxd4 $19 { White cannot defend from all the threats } { [%csl Rf2] } 18. Qf3 e4 19. Qe3 Qxe3+ 20. fxe3 Rxg2) 12... O-O 13. Nf3 Nxe2! 14. Kxe2 e4 15. Nh4 Bg4+ 16. f3 Rfe8 17. Kf2 exd3 18. Qxd3 Qb6+ $19) 11... Bc5 $15) 4... e4 5. d5 (5. Nc3 d5 6. cxd5 cxd5 7. Bg5 Nbd7 8. Qb3 Bd6 9. Nh3 h6 10. Bf4 Bxf4 11. Nxf4 Nb6 12. a4 a5 13. Rc1 O-O 14. Nb5 g5 15. Nc7 gxf4 16. Qxb6 Ra6 17. Qb5 Rd6 18. O-O (18. Rc3 Kh8) (18. gxf4 Kh8 19. e3 Rg8 20. Bf1 Bg4 21. h3 Bf3 22. Rh2 Kh7 { Reed, Arthur F. - Jefferson, N. W.., 0-1, BCCA/Rog/A (ENG), 2019, https://lichess.org/6hNFVtqx })) 5... Bb4+ 6. Bd2 Qe7 7. Nc3 (7. Nh3 cxd5 8. cxd5 Bxd2+ 9. Qxd2 d6 10. Nc3 h6 11. O-O O-O 12. Rac1 Bf5 13. f3 Nbd7 14. Qf4 exf3 15. exf3 Bxh3 16. Bxh3 Rae8 17. Nb5 a6 18. Nd4 Nxd5 19. Qd2 N7f6 20. Nf5 Qd8 21. Kh1 Qb6 22. Rfe1 Rxe1+ 23. Rxe1 Rd8 24. a3 Qb3) 7... O-O 8. Nh3 cxd5 9. cxd5 d6 10. Nxe4 Bxh3 11. Nxf6+ Qxf6 12. Bxh3 Qxb2 13. Rc1 Na6 14. Rc2 Bxd2+ 15. Qxd2 Qa1+ 16. Rc1 Qe5 17. O-O Nc5 18. Rc2 b5 (18... Rfe8) 19. Rb1 a6 (19... Rfb8 20. Rcb2 a6)) 4. cxd5 Nxd5 { Transposes to the Reversed Dragon (s.u.), which is a major tabiya in the English. White has many plans: Postponing the development of either knights, develop the bishop to b2 or e3 etc. } 5. Nc3 (5. Nf3 Nc6 6. O-O Nb6 7. Nc3 Be7) 5... Nb6 6. Nf3 (6. e3 Nc6 7. Nge2 Be7 8. O-O O-O 9. f4 f6 10. f5 Bd7 11. d4 Be8 12. d5 Nb8 13. d6 cxd6 14. Bxb7 Bc6 15. Bxc6 Nxc6 $10) 6... Nc6 { Many development schemes for white here, see transposition below. }) (2... c6 { Anand is a big expert in this line, the difference between 2...c6 and including Nf6 is quite subtle but after 3.Nf3 her } 3. d4 (3. Nf3 { Anand is a big expert in this line, the difference between 2...c6 and including Nf6 is quite subtle but after 3.Nf3 here } 3... e4 4. Nd4 d5 5. d3 (5. cxd5 Qxd5 6. Nc2 (6. Nb3 Nf6 7. Nc3 Qh5 8. h3 a5) 6... Nf6 7. Nc3 Qe5 8. Bg2 Na6 9. O-O Be7 10. Ne3 h5 (10... O-O 11. Qc2 Nc5 12. b4 Ne6 13. Bb2 Nd4 14. Qb1 Rd8) 11. d4 exd3 12. exd3 Qd4 13. Nc2 Qg4 14. Bf4 Qxd1 15. Raxd1 Bg4 16. Rd2 O-O-O 17. d4 Nc7 18. Ne3 Be6 19. d5 Ncxd5 20. Ncxd5 Nxd5 21. Nxd5 Bxd5 22. Rxd5 Rxd5 23. Bxd5 cxd5 24. Rc1+ Kd7 25. Rc7+ Ke6 26. Rxb7 Rc8 27. Rxa7 Rc2 28. Be3 Bf6 29. Ra4 Bxb2 30. Kg2 d4 31. Bxd4 Bxd4 32. Rxd4 Rxa2 33. Re4+ Kf6 34. Rf4+ Ke6 35. Re4+ Kf6 36. Rf4+ Ke6 37. Re4+ Kf6 38. Rf4+ Ke6 39. h4 g6 40. Re4+ Kf6 { Giri, Anish - Grischuk, Alexander, 1/2-1/2, Candidates Tournament, 2020, https://lichess.org/EUN0YCpZ }) 5... Bc5 6. Nb3 Bb4+ 7. Bd2 Bxd2+ 8. Qxd2 dxc4 9. dxc4 f5! { Because the knight is on g8 is better for black because f5 is possible with Nf6 to come and support black's center } { Ding Liren - Yu Yangyi, 1/2-1/2, FIDE World Cup 2019, https://lichess.org/zMqmleFL } { [%cal Bg8f6] }) 3... e4 4. Nc3 (4. d5 Bb4+ 5. Bd2 Qe7) 4... d5 5. Bg2 Nf6) 3. Nc3 (3. Bg2 Bc5!? 4. Nc3 a6) 3... Bc5!? 4. Bg2 a6 { Transposition to the Serbian System seen below. }) (2. d3 { Quite passive } 2... Nf6 3. Nf3 Nc6 4. a3 { Ready to go into a Reversed Najdorf } (4. Nc3 d5 5. cxd5 Nxd5 { Reaches a reversed Classical Sicilian which is fine for black } 6. e4 { Critical } (6. e3 Nxc3 { Simple } 7. bxc3 Bd6 8. Be2 O-O 9. O-O b6 { With Bb7, Re8, Qf6-g6 and a very reasonable position } { [%cal Bc8b7,Gf8e8,Yf6g6,Yd8f6] }) (6. g3 { A classical Sicilian + Dragon hybrid } 6... Be7 { Developing classically } 7. Bg2 Be6 8. O-O O-O 9. Bd2 Qd7 { Looks very nice for black where the tempo doesn't matter much } 10. Rc1 f6 11. Ne4 { Preparing Nc5 } 11... Rfd8 { Just developing } 12. Nc5 Bxc5 13. Rxc5 b6 { Kicking the rook back } 14. Rc1 a5 { Expanding on the queenside with quite a harmonious position }) 6... Nf6 { Just playing positionally } 7. h3 { Preventing Bg4 } 7... Bc5 { Taking over the diagonal } 8. Be3 Qe7 { Black doesn't want to give white the whole center by taking on e3 so Qe7 is a useful move and this also vacates the d8 square for black's rook } { [%csl Gd8] } 9. Be2 O-O 10. O-O Rd8 { Black has an excellent setup } 11. Rc1 Nd4 12. Bxd4 Bxd4 13. Nxd4 Rxd4 14. Qd2 c5 15. f4 b6 16. Qe3 exf4 17. Rxf4 Be6 $15 { With an easy plan of doubling on the d-file, and playing Nd7-e5 with a better position } { Edouard, Romain - Wojtaszek, Radoslaw, 0-1, Biel rapid 53rd, 2020, https://lichess.org/eaca0Dqp } { [%cal Ga8d8,Bf6d7,Bd7e5] }) 4... d5 5. cxd5 Nxd5 6. e4 { Critical } (6. e3 Bd6 { Just developing the bishop on a good diagonal in the future if it opens up } 7. b4 O-O 8. Bb2 Re8 9. Be2 a5 { Typical undermining of the queenside } 10. b5 Na7 11. a4 c6 12. bxc6 Nxc6 { With nice control over the b4 square } { [%csl Bb4] }) 6... Nf6 { Again the knight goes to f6 like usual just like in the Reversed Classical Sicilian }) (2. Nf3!? { A specialty of GM Rakhmanov, leading to a reversed Alekhine sort of position } 2... e4 (2... Nc6 3. Nc3 Nf6 { Transposition. }) 3. Nd4 Nc6 4. e3 (4. Nc2 { Just retreating } 4... d5 5. cxd5 Qxd5 { The queen seems vulnerable but is quite active and is only vulnerable to one tempo loss } 6. Nc3 Qe5 { White should play d4 to gain a tempo and remove the annoying e4 pawn } 7. d4 (7. g3 Nf6 8. Bg2 Bf5 9. O-O (9. Ne3 O-O-O 10. Nxf5 Qxf5 11. O-O h5) 9... O-O-O 10. Ne3 h5 (10... Be6 11. Qa4? (11. a3 h5 12. h4 g5) 11... Rd4 12. Qb5 Bc5) 11. Nxf5 Qxf5 12. Qa4 (12. h4 g5) 12... h4 13. Bxe4 Nxe4 14. Nxe4 (14. Qxe4 Qh3) 14... Nd4) 7... exd3 8. Qxd3 Nf6 { Black has easy play } 9. e4 Nb4 10. Nxb4 Bxb4 { And e4 is hanging because of the pin now } { [%csl Re4] } 11. Qb5+ { Clearly trading pieces is not a problem for black } 11... Qxb5 12. Bxb5+ Bd7 13. Bd3 (13. Bxd7+ Nxd7 $15 { Black's pieces jump to life } { [%cal Be8c8,Gd7e5] }) 13... O-O-O 14. f3 Be6 15. Be2 Nd7 $15 { The knight travels to e5 or c5 and has some weak squares to work with } { [%cal Bd7e5,Yd7c5] }) (4. Nxc6?! { Has been tried but it is not very impressive and black has easy development } 4... dxc6 5. Nc3 { More logical } (5. e3 { Weakening the d3 square } { [%csl Rd3] } 5... Nf6 6. h3 { Preventing Ng4 or Bg4 } 6... h5! { Gaining some space and preparing Bf5 without allowing g4 } 7. Qb3 (7. d4 exd3 8. Bxd3 Be6 $15 { With either Nd7-e5 or Qd7 and 0-0-0. Black has a great position } { [%cal Gf6d7,Gd7e5,Bd8d7,Be8c8] }) 7... Nd7 $17 { Preparing Nc5 to abuse the d3 square } { [%csl Rd3][%cal Bd7c5] } (7... h4 8. Nc3 b6 9. Qc2 Bf5 { Rakhmanov, Ale - Alsina Leal, D., 1-0, TCh-ESP Div 1 2016, https://lichess.org/8SiZ758o } { Black is doing very well })) 5... Nf6 6. e3 { White is preparing Qc2, b3-Bb2 } { [%cal Gb2b3,Gc1b2,Bd1c2] } (6. g3 Bc5 7. Bg2 O-O 8. O-O Re8 9. Na4 Bf8) 6... Bd6 7. h3 Bf5 { Supporting the pawn } 8. Qc2 (8. g4?! { Too weakening } 8... Bg6 9. Bg2 Qe7 10. Qc2 O-O-O { Not caring about the e4 pawn dropping } 11. g5 Nd7 12. Bxe4 Bb4! $17 { With the threat of Nc5-d3 and white is stuck with many weaknesses } { [%csl Rg5][%cal Bd7c5,Bc5d3] }) 8... Qe7 { Supporting the pawn and preparing 0-0-0 } 9. b3 O-O-O 10. Bb2 Bg6 { Prophylaxis against f4 because black prepares to play Be5 now } { [%cal Bd6e5] } 11. O-O-O Be5 { Improving the bishop } 12. d4 exd3 13. Bxd3 Rxd3 14. Rxd3 Re8 { Still keeping white thinking about the pin } 15. Qe2 Bxd3 16. Qxd3 b6 $17 { Black has a lot of play in this position } { [%cal Bg7g6,Ga7a5,Ye5c3,Rf6e4,Gc8b7] }) 4... Nf6 { Just developing } 5. Nc3 { Makes most sense } (5. Nxc6 { Leads to an extraneous option } 5... dxc6 6. d4 exd3 7. Bxd3 { White gets a favorable pawn structure but black's pieces get out very easily } 7... Bb4+ { The first problem arises as no piece can block on d2 as d3 will hang } 8. Nc3 Bxc3+ 9. bxc3 Be6 $17 { White also has a poor pawn structure and black has a nice game ahead } { [%cal Bf6d7,Bd7e5,Yd8e7,Ge8c8,Ye7f6] }) 5... Nxd4 6. exd4 d5! 7. cxd5 (7. c5?! { Overextended } 7... b6 8. b4 a5 $15) (7. Qb3 { Trying to increase the pressure } 7... Bd6 8. cxd5 (8. c5 Bf4 9. g3 Bh6) 8... O-O $15 { White has tripled pawns which is not good } { [%csl Rd4,Rd5,Rd2] } 9. Be2 Re8 10. O-O Ng4!) (7. d3 exd3 8. Bxd3 dxc4 9. Bxc4 { Leaving white with an isolated pawn } { [%csl Rd4] } 9... Bd6 10. O-O O-O { Looks like a QGA position which is fine for black } 11. Bg5 h6 12. Bh4 c6 { Covering the d5 square } 13. Re1 { Increasing the pressure } (13. d5 { Leads to many exchanges } 13... cxd5 14. Nxd5 g5 15. Bg3 Bxg3 16. hxg3 (16. fxg3!? { Trying to get funky } 16... Nxd5 17. Bxd5 Be6 { Trading more pieces } 18. Bxe6 Qb6+ 19. Kh1 Qxe6 $10 { Clearly black is fine here }) 16... Be6 $10 { Completely fine and equal }) 13... g5 14. Bg3 Bxg3 15. hxg3 (15. fxg3!? Bf5 $15 { Black can consolidate with Bg6 } { [%cal Gf5g6] }) 15... Bf5 $10 { Black has easy play with Qd6, Rae8, Bg6, Kg7 and a completely healthy position } { [%cal Gf5g6,Bd8d6,Ba8d8,Yg8g7] }) 7... Nxd5 8. d3 (8. Nxe4?! { Walks into a pin } 8... Qe7 9. Qe2 { Halting white's development } 9... Bf5 10. d3 O-O-O $17 { Black's lead in development is massive } { [%cal Gg7g6,Gf8g7,Ge7d7] }) 8... exd3 9. Bxd3 Be6 $10 { Black has simple play with Be7 and 0-0 } { [%cal Gf8e7,Be8g8] }) (2. e3!? { Tried by Carlsen a few times } 2... Nf6 3. Nc3 (3. a3 d5 4. cxd5 Nxd5 { Leads to a reversed Kan Sicilian } 5. Nf3 (5. Nc3 Nxc3 6. bxc3 { Reaching a position where white has played a not so useful a3 move } 6... Bd6 { Developing } 7. d4 O-O { White is underdeveloped and black has a great position. Black can even play c5 and Nc6 because the knight isn't on c6 yet } { [%cal Gb8c6,Bc7c5] })) (3. d4 { Not very threatening } 3... exd4 4. exd4 d5 { Eventually white is going to be left with an isolated d4 pawn } { [%csl Rd4] } 5. Nc3 Bb4 6. Nf3 O-O { Reaching a QGA like position which is completely fine for black and I like to play against the IQP after dxc4 } { [%csl Rd4] } 7. Be2 dxc4 8. Bxc4 Bg4 { Pinning the knight } 9. O-O Nc6 { Clearly with a very healthy position for black } 10. Be3 Qd6 11. a3) 3... Nc6 { Waiting to see what white wants to do } 4. Qb3!? { Preventing d5 } (4. a3 d5 5. cxd5 Nxd5 { Reaches a reversed Kan Sicilian } 6. Qc2 Nxc3 7. bxc3 Qg5 { The queen looks a little bit awkward on g5 because of Nf3, but the idea is to trade on g6 } 8. Nf3 Qg6 9. Qxg6 (9. d3 { Avoiding the exchange } 9... Be7 { Black just develops } 10. Be2 O-O 11. O-O b6 $15 { With an easy game ahead } { [%cal Bc8b7,Ga8d8,Yf7f5] }) 9... hxg6 { With an open h-file and a solid position to work with }) (4. d4 Bb4) (4. Nd5 Bc5 5. Ne2 Nxd5 6. cxd5 Ne7 7. Nc3 O-O) 4... Bb4 { As d5 is no longer possible, black switches plans } 5. Nge2 (5. Nd5 Bc5 6. Ne2 O-O 7. Nec3 Nxd5 8. Nxd5 Ne7) 5... O-O 6. a3 Bxc3 7. Nxc3 Ne7 $146 { Just preparing c6 & d5 } { [%cal Bc7c6,Bd7d5] } 8. Be2 c6 9. O-O { White is ready to respond to d5 with d4, so a waiting move is sensible } 9... Re8 { A useful move to support the e-pawn, and d5 is coming next if white does nothing } { [%cal Gd7d5] } 10. d4 exd4 11. exd4 d5 { And recapturing on d5 with a knight would be a dream } { [%cal Be7d5] } 12. Bg5 (12. c5 Nf5 { Clearly something has gone wrong for white with the knight jumping to life } { [%cal Bf6e4] }) 12... Nf5 13. Rfd1 h6 14. Bf4 Qe7 15. cxd5 Nxd5 16. Be5 (16. Nxd5 Qxe2 17. Nc7 Nxd4 18. Qb4)) (2. b3!? { A very rare move } 2... Ne7!? $146 { Much more clever! The idea is to offer a possible gambit } 3. Nf3 (3. Bb2 Nbc6 4. Nc3 d5 5. cxd5 Nxd5 6. e3 (6. Nxd5 Qxd5 7. e3 Bf5 8. a3 Na5) 6... Nxc3) 3... Nbc6 4. Bb2 d5! { Sacrificing e5 } 5. cxd5 (5. Nxe5?! { Taking the pawn is dangerous } 5... Nxe5 6. Bxe5 d4 { And the knight is ready to come to c6 or g6 next } { [%cal Ye7g6,Ge7c6] } 7. e3 Nc6 8. Bxd4 (8. Bg3 h5! 9. h4 Qf6 { With problems against the rook } { [%csl Ra1] } 10. Qf3 Qg6 $17 { With tremendous activity for the pawn } { [%cal Rc6b4,Rc8g4,Yf8b4] }) 8... Nxd4 9. exd4 Qxd4 10. Nc3 Be6 { Just preparing 0-0-0 next with an excellent game } { [%cal Be8c8] }) 5... Nxd5 6. Nxe5 Nxe5 7. Bxe5 Qe7 8. Bb2 Nb4 9. d3 Bf5) 2... Nf6 { [%cal Gd7d5] } (2... Nc6!? { Serbian System against everything but Nf3 which transposes to the Four Knights. } 3. g3 (3. Nf3 Nf6 { Transposes to the main line. } (3... g6 { Also possible, very complex position & not so much theory as there are many possible move orders & theory hasn't been established yet }) (3... f5!? { A reversed Grand-Prix, quite risky though! } 4. d4 (4. g3)) (3... Bc5?! 4. Nxe5 Bxf2+ 5. Kxf2 Nxe5 6. d4 Ng4+ 7. Ke1 d5 8. cxd5 Qf6 9. Ne4 Qf5 10. Qd3 (10. e3) 10... N8f6)) 3... Bc5! { This move is only possible if white plays g3 and doesn't include Nf3 because of Nxe5 and d4 trick } 4. Bg2 a6! { [%cal Gc5a7,Ge2e3,Gg1f3] } 5. e3 (5. Nf3 d6 6. O-O (6. d3 Ba7 7. a3 h6 { [%csl Bg5][%cal Gg8f6,Yc1g5] }) 6... Nge7! { Der Schlüsselzug. } { [%csl Gf5,Bc6] } 7. e3 { [%csl Ge3,Gf3] } (7. d3 { [%csl Gd3,Gf3] } 7... O-O { [%cal Gf7f5,Gc5a7,Gf5f4,Yh7h6,Yc8e6,Yd8d7] }) 7... Ba7 8. d4 (8. h3!? { [%csl Bg4] } 8... O-O 9. d4 (9. g4? f5) 9... exd4 10. exd4 (10. Nxd4 Nxd4 11. exd4 Nf5 12. d5?? { [%csl Ga7] 