 Be6 11. b3 Qf6 12. Nc3 Qg6 13. h4 (13. g3?! { Weakening } 13... h5! 14. h4 Nc5 15. Bg2 Bg4 16. f3 Bd6 17. fxg4 Bxg3+ 18. Kf1 hxg4 19. Kg1 f5! 20. Bb2 O-O 21. h5 Qg5 22. h6 gxh6 23. Na4 Nd7 24. Qf1 Rad8 { With a crushing position } { [%cal Bf5f4,Yh6h5,Yh5h4] }) 13... h5 14. Bb2) 10. Qb3 Bf5 11. Qxb7 Nd7 12. Nc3 (12. Qxc6 Rc8 13. Qd5 Bg7)) 9... Bc5 10. Be2 Bf5 11. Qb3? Nd7 12. Qc3 (12. Qxb7 O-O 13. Qxc6 Ne5 14. Qxc5 Nxd3+ 15. Bxd3 Qxd3 $19 { With Rfe8 coming next being decisive, white has to give up the queen with Qe3 to prevent getting mated } { [%cal Bf8e8] }) 12... Qb6 13. O-O Bd4 14. Qb3 Nc5 15. Qxb6 axb6 $17 { Black has a massive lead in development and the c1 bishop is dead. Black will win back the pawn eventually but is in no rush to do so } { [%csl Rd3,Rc1] }) 8... f5 9. Ng3 h5 (9... Na6 10. e3 h5 11. Qf3 g6) 10. e3 Na6 11. a3 Nc5 12. b4 h4 13. Nh5 Ne6 14. Be2 g6 15. Nf4 Bg7 16. Bb2 Qg5 17. Nxe6 Bxe6 18. O-O O-O-O) 7... cxd5 8. Ng3 (8. Nc3 d4 9. Ne4 Nc6 10. d3 (10. e3 d3) 10... Be6 11. g3 Bb4+! { Provoking Bd2 so that the d2 square is covered by the bishop } 12. Bd2 (12. Nd2?! Bxa2! { What a tactical shot! } 13. Bg2 Bd5 14. O-O Bxg2 { The easiest solution } (14... O-O 15. Bxd5 Qxd5 16. Qb3 Qd7) 15. Kxg2 O-O 16. Nf3 Re8 17. Bd2 Bc5 18. Qb3 Bb6 $15 { Black has enough support on the d4 pawn and remains with a much better pawn structure as the e2 pawn remains backwards } { [%csl Re2,Bd4] }) 12... Be7 { Now threatening a clever f5! } 13. f4 $7 { Only good move, clearing up the f2 square for the knight in case of f5 } (13. Bg2?? f5 $19 { Wins a piece }) 13... O-O 14. Bg2 h6 { Taking away the g5 square so that black can play f5 next without running into Ng5 } 15. O-O f5 16. Nf2 a5 { Positionally threatening a4 } { [%cal Ra5a4] } 17. Qa4 Qb6 { And it's not easy for white to move }) 8... h5 9. e3 (9. h4?! Bg4! { And white cannot play e3 or g3 to open up the f1 bishop and thus faces some serious development problems } { [%cal Bb8c6,Gf8d6] } 10. Qb3 Nc6 11. e3 Bd6 12. d4 (12. Ne2? d4!)) 9... h4 10. Ne2 Nc6 11. d4 Qf6 12. Nc3 Qg6 13. h3 Bd6 14. Nb5 Bb8 15. Bd2) 4. g3 (4. e3!? Bb4 5. Qc2 { Defending against the threat of pushing e4 } (5. Nd5 e4! { White has some serious problems } (5... Bd6) 6. Ng1 { The knight is virtually forced to retreat } (6. Nxb4 Nxb4 7. a3 (7. Nd4 c5 8. Nb5 d5 9. a3 Nd3+ 10. Bxd3 exd3 11. cxd5 Nxd5 12. O-O O-O 13. Qf3 f5 { Preventing e4 } 14. Nc3 Be6 $17 { With a big advantage } { Rogers, Jonathan - Adair, James, 0-1, BCF-chT1 1920 (4NCL) online, 2020, https://lichess.org/yFZrRNMQ }) 7... Nc6 8. Ng1) 6... O-O 7. Nh3 Re8 8. Nhf4 Nxd5 9. Nxd5 a5 10. a3 Bc5 11. Be2 Ne5 12. O-O Bf8 { With c6-d5 and a nice position } { [%cal Bc7c6,Bd7d5] }) 5... Bxc3 (5... O-O 6. Nd5 a5 (6... Re8 7. Qf5 d6 8. Nxf6+ gxf6 9. Qh5 e4 10. a3 exf3 11. gxf3 Re5 12. Rg1+ Rg5 13. Rxg5+ fxg5 14. Bd3 Qf6 15. Qxh7+ Kf8 16. axb4) 7. a3 Bc5 8. Ng5 (8. Nxe5 Nxe5 9. d4 Nxd5 10. cxd5 Bxd4 11. exd4 Ng6 12. d6 c6 13. Be3 Qf6 14. h4 Qxd6) 8... g6 9. Nxf6+ Qxf6 10. Ne4 Qe7 11. b3 d6 12. h4) 6. Qxc3 (6. bxc3 O-O 7. Bd3 (7. e4 d6 8. g3 Ne7 (8... a6 9. a4 Nd7 10. Nh4 Nc5 11. d3 Na5 12. Be3 Bd7) 9. Bg2 Be6 10. d3 Ng6) (7. Be2 e4 8. Nd4 Ne5 9. f4 Nd3+ 10. Bxd3 exd3 11. Qxd3 c5 12. Nf3 b6) 7... d6 8. Ng5 h6 9. Ne4 Nxe4 10. Bxe4 f5 11. Bd3 e4 12. Be2 Ne5 13. O-O Be6 14. c5 dxc5 15. c4 Qd6) 6... Qe7 { With d5 next } { [%cal Bd7d5] } 7. a3 (7. d4 e4 8. Nd2 d5 { Black has a nice center } 9. a3 (9. cxd5 Nxd5 10. Qb3 Nf6 { [%cal Gf1b5,Gc8d7,Ge8c8,Be8g8] }) 9... O-O 10. h3 dxc4 11. Bxc4 Be6 { With Nd5 and f5 possible to come and an easy game ahead } { [%cal Bf6d5,Gf7f5] }) (7. d3 d5 { [%csl Gd3] } 8. b3 Bg4 (8... e4?! 9. cxd5 Qb4 10. Qxb4 Nxb4 11. dxe4 (11. Nd4 exd3 12. a3 Nbxd5 13. Bxd3) 11... Nc2+ 12. Kd2 Nxa1 13. Bd3) 9. Be2 O-O-O 10. O-O h5) (7. Be2 O-O 8. O-O d5 9. cxd5 Nxd5 10. Qc2 (10. Qb3 Nb6 11. a3) 10... e4 11. Ne1 Bf5) 7... d5 8. cxd5 (8. d4 exd4 9. Nxd4 Nxd4 10. Qxd4 c5 11. Qd2 O-O 12. cxd5 Rd8 13. Bd3 (13. Bc4? Qe4 { [%csl Rg2,Rc4] } 14. Bf1 Rxd5 $17) 13... Rxd5) 8... Nxd5 9. Qb3 (9. Qc2 e4 10. Ng1 O-O 11. b4 (11. Bc4 Nb6 12. Ba2 Bg4 13. h3 Bd7) (11. Ne2 Ndb4! { [%csl Gd3,Gf2][%cal Gc6b4,Gb4d3,Gd3f2] } 12. axb4 Nxb4 { [%csl Gd3][%cal Gb4d3] } 13. Qb1 Nd3+ 14. Kd1 Nxf2+) 11... Bf5 12. Bb2 Ne5 13. Bxe5 Qxe5 $17) 9... Nb6 10. d3 O-O 11. Be2 a5 (11... Bf5) 12. Qc2 (12. O-O Be6 13. Qc2 a4 { [%cal Ge6b3,Gc6a5] }) 12... a4) (4. d4 exd4 5. Nxd4 Bb4 6. Bg5 (6. Nxc6 bxc6 7. Bd2 (7. Qb3 a5 8. a3 Bd6) 7... O-O 8. e3 d5 { [%cal Gb4d6,Ga2a3] } 9. a3 (9. Be2 dxc4 10. Bxc4 Ng4! { [%csl Rh2] } 11. O-O Qd6 (11... Nxh2 12. Kxh2 Qh4+ 13. Kg1 Qxc4)) (9. cxd5 cxd5 10. Be2 c6 11. O-O Bd6 { Kraidman, Yair - Kortschnoj, Viktor, 0-1, Jerusalem, 1986, https://lichess.org/twVQ8BDS } { [%cal Ga8b8] }) 9... Bd6) 6... h6 7. Bh4 O-O 8. Rc1 Re8 9. a3 (9. e3) 9... Bxc3+ 10. Rxc3 g5 11. Bg3 Nxd4 12. Qxd4 c5! 13. Qxc5 (13. Qd6? Ne4 14. Qxh6 Re6 15. Qh5 Nxc3) 13... Ne4) (4. a3 { Another possibility } 4... d5 5. cxd5 Nxd5 6. Qc2 (6. e4 { Critical } 6... Nxc3 7. bxc3 Bd6 8. d4 O-O 9. Be2 Qe7 { White has a center but it's not flexible and thus black is doing great }) (6. e3?! { Actually gets a very similar position normally with reversed colors. But here it's just good for black } 6... Nxc3 7. bxc3 e4 8. Nd4 Ne5 { This position usually comes from reversed colors } 9. Qc2 f5 10. Qb3 c5 11. Bb5+ Ke7 { Normally in this line, Ba3 should be the move which is clearly not possible. Black has Be6 next } { [%cal Bc8e6] } 12. f4 Nf7 13. Ne2 Nd6 $17 { With Be6 coming with a tempo } { [%cal Bc8e6,Ra7a6] }) 6... Nxc3 7. bxc3 (7. dxc3 a5 8. e4 Bc5 9. Bc4 Qf6 10. a4 h6 11. O-O O-O { With nothing to worry about } { [%cal Bc6e7,Be7g6] }) 7... Bd6 { With 0-0 next } { [%cal Be8g8] }) (4. e4!? { Just clamping down on d5, very interesting } { [%csl Gd5] } 4... Bb4 (4... Bc5 5. Nxe5 { A problem } 5... Nxe5 6. d4) 5. d3 (5. Nd5 Nxe4 6. Nxb4 Nxb4 7. Nxe5 Qh4 8. g3 Qf6 9. Nf3 (9. Ng4 Qd4) 9... Nc2+ 10. Qxc2 Qxf3) 5... d6 6. a3 { Taking on c3 would give white the whole center } (6. Be2 { Not very dangerous, white has some ideas of possibly taking control over d5 with Nd2-f1-g3. But here Bg4 would not force the exchange of knight for bishop so we can do without it. } { [%cal Gf3d2,Gd2f1,Gf1e3] } 6... Bc5 7. O-O O-O { Simple } 8. Be3 (8. h3 { Eliminating all Bg4 options } 8... a5 { Making sure the bishop can stay on the long diagonal } 9. Be3 Nd4 10. Bxd4 exd4 11. Nb5 Nh5! $132 { Black has a lot of counterplay with Nf4 and Qf6 } { [%cal Bd8f6,Yc8h3,Gh5f4] }) 8... Bg4 { Time to remove one of the defenders of the d4 square } 9. Nxe5 { Trying to go tactical } (9. Ne1 { Trying to avoid exchanging the knights } 9... Bxe2 10. Qxe2 Nd4 11. Qd1 c6 { With a nice position } { [%cal Ga7a5,Yf6d7,Bf7f5] }) 9... Nxe5 10. d4 Nxc4! { A nasty surprise } 11. Bxg4 Nxe3 12. fxe3 Bb6 { With ideas of Qe7 and possibly c5! Black is not worse } { [%cal Bd8e7,Gc7c5] }) (6. h3 { Preventing all Bg4 ideas } 6... Bc5 { Similar to 6.Be2 } 7. Be2 { Can transpose from the 6.Be2 move order } 7... Nd4 8. Rb1 { Preparing b4 } 8... a5 9. a3 { Insisting on b4 } 9... h6 { Prophylaxis against Bg5 } 10. b4 axb4 11. axb4 Bb6 12. O-O O-O 13. Be3 Nxf3+ { Trading a couple of pieces without any problems } 14. Bxf3 Bxe3 15. fxe3 Be6 $10 { With c6 next and the position is very balanced } { [%cal Bc7c6] } 16. Qd2 c6 17. Ra1 Qb6 18. b5 Rfb8 19. Rfb1 Qd8 20. Rxa8 Rxa8 21. bxc6 bxc6 22. Nd1 Ra3 23. Nf2 g6 24. d4 Qc7 25. d5 Bd7 26. Kh2 cxd5 27. cxd5 Kg7 28. Nd1 h5 29. Qc1 Qa5 30. Rb7 h4 31. Qb2 Ra4 32. Qc3 Qxc3 33. Nxc3 Ra3 34. Nd1 Kf8 35. Rb6 Ke7 36. Rb7 g5 37. Nf2 Kd8 38. Rb8+ Ke7 39. Rb7 Kd8 40. Rb8+ Ke7 41. Rb7 { Carlsen, Magnus - Giri, Anish, 1/2-1/2, Chessable Masters Final, 2020, https://lichess.org/batBblAc }) (6. g3?! { Too weakening and it allows Bg4 so that black can remove the f3 knight so control over d4 is weakened } 6... Bg4 { And taking on f3 next to remove a defender of the d4 square } { [%csl Rf3] } 7. h3 Bxf3 8. Qxf3 Nd4 9. Qd1 c6 { Controlling all of the dark-squares } (9... a5 { Expanding on the queenside and preventing b4 } 10. a3 Bc5 $15 { With a fantastic position }) 10. Bg2 (10. a3 Bc5 11. Na4 a5 12. Be3 O-O 13. Bg2 b5 14. Nxc5 dxc5 15. cxb5 (15. Rc1 b4 $17 { With a nice initiative on the queenside }) 15... cxb5 16. Rc1 Rc8 $17 { With a nice queenside pawn majority } { [%cal Bd8d6,Ga5a4] }) 10... a5 { Preparing Bc5 and keeping the bishop on the a7-g1 diagonal } { [%cal Bb4c5] } 11. O-O { White's plan is simple: play f4-f5, continue with g4, and go for an attack } { [%cal Gf2f4,Yf4f5,Bg3g4] } 11... Bc5 { Black prevents f4 } 12. Kh1 { Best, not allowing any Ng4+ possibilities } (12. Kh2?! { The king is quite vulnerable on the h2 square to Ng4+ as it turns out } 12... h5! { Attacking immediately } 13. f4?! (13. Bg5? Ng4+ 14. Qxg4 hxg4 15. Bxd8 Kxd8 $17 { With decisive pressure along the h-file }) 13... Ng4+! 14. Kh1 exf4 15. Bxf4 (15. gxf4? { Allows the queen in } 15... Qh4 $19 { White is lost due to the many threats } { [%cal Gh4g3,Rg4f2] }) 15... Qd7 $17 $40 { With a dangerous attack } { [%cal Rh5h4,Yf7f6,Yg7g5,Ge8c8] }) 12... h5! { Again, just like in 12.Kh2 } 13. h4 { Weakens all the light-squares } { [%csl Rg4,Rh3] } (13. f4 h4! 14. g4 Nd7! { Very precise move, still keeping the center intact while keeping white's dark-squared bishop locked in } 15. f5 { g5 is a dangerous threat } { [%cal Rg4g5] } 15... g5! { A radical but strong positional move that closes up the position } { [%cal Bf7f6,Ye8f7,Yf7g7,Gb7b5] } 16. fxg6 fxg6 $17 { Black can continue with Qe7 and Rf8 to trade some rooks and then still maintain great control over the dark-squares } { [%cal Bd8e7,Gh8f8,Ye8f8,Yf8g7] }) 13... Qd7 { Taking control of the light-squares } { [%csl Gh3,Gg4] }) 6... Bc5 { Not giving up the bishop pair just yet } 7. b4 Bb6 8. Na4 { White will win the bishop pair, but } (8. Be3 { The old move, but black is fine here after } 8... Bg4 { Generally speaking, when white decides to fight for control over d4 with a move such as Be3 then Bg4 is useful to remove the f3 knight } 9. Be2 Bxf3 10. Bxf3 Nd4 { Black is threatening a5, so white should play Na5 and take the bishop } { [%cal Ga7a5] } 11. Na4 Nxf3+ { Trading pieces } 12. Qxf3 Bxe3 13. Qxe3 { The choise of Carlsen against Giri } (13. fxe3 O-O 14. O-O c6 { With no issues, black can play on the queenside } { [%cal Gb7b5,Ya7a5] }) 13... O-O 14. O-O Re8 { Just a useful move, preparing c6-d5 next possibly } { [%cal Gc7c6,Bd6d5] } 15. Nc3 a5 { Provoking b5 } 16. b5 c6 17. Rab1 (17. f4 { Trying to get some initiative } 17... Nh5! { Ready to jump into the f4 square } 18. f5 Nf6 { And the f5 advance is not very useful as black will blast open the center with d5 next } { [%cal Bd6d5] }) 17... d5 { Giri was fine here against Carlsen } { Carlsen, M. - Giri, A., 1/2-1/2, 7th Sinquefield Cup 2019, https://lichess.org/zWDS2Jed }) 8... Bg4 { And either white takes on b6 now or the bishop will escape to d4 } 9. Nxb6 (9. Be2 { Allows the dark-squared bishop to escape } 9... Bxf3 10. Bxf3 Bd4 { Planting the bishop on a nice square } 11. Rb1 (11. Bb2? { Turns out to be a big positional mistake } 11... Bxb2 12. Nxb2 { The knight is awful on b2 } { [%csl Rb2] } 12... Nd4 $17 { And black will undermine the queenside with a5 and if b5 then c6! } { [%cal Ra7a5,Gc7c6] }) 11... a6 { Preparing b5, Ba7, and then Nd4 } { [%cal Yd4a7,Bb7b5,Gc6d4] } 12. Qd2 (12. Bd2 { Allowing the knight to retreat to c3 in case of b5. One of the main moves } 12... Ba7 { Freeing up the d4 square for th eknight } { [%cal Gc6d4] } 13. g3 { Preparing Bg2 and f4 in the future } { [%cal Gf3g2] } (13. O-O { Complacent } 13... Nd4 14. Nc3 (14. g3 h5 15. h4 Qd7 16. Nc3 c6 { With b5 next } { [%cal Bb7b5] }) 14... O-O 15. Kh1 { Prophylaxis as at some point white may want to play f4 } 15... b5 16. Be2 c6 { Consolidating the queenside and taking control over d5 } 17. a4 (17. f4 exf4 18. Bxf4 Ne6 19. Bg3 Bd4 { With Be5 next and a solid center } { [%cal Bd4e5] }) 17... h6 { Preventing any Bg5 } 18. f4 { Deac, Bogdan-Daniel - Palac, M.., 1-0, European Hybrid Qual 2021, https://lichess.org/6uItn3tr } { Only way to make something happen but the complications favors black } 18... exf4 19. axb5 { Important for white to exchange some pieces on b5 first } (19. Bxf4?! { Allows } 19... bxc4 20. dxc4 Nxe2 21. Qxe2 Bd4 { Harassing the knight which is supporting the e4 pawn } { [%csl Rc3,Ge4] } 22. Rbc1 Re8 $17 { e4 drops } { [%csl Re4] }) 19... axb5 20. cxb5 Nxb5 21. Nxb5 cxb5 22. Bxf4 { If white manages to get in Bf3 and d4, then white would be better... luckily it is our move } { [%cal Ge2f3,Bd3d4] } 22... Bd4 $15 { Blockading the d-pawn with control over the a-file and just a great position }) 13... Nd4 14. Bg2 h5! { Preparing h4 } { [%cal Gh5h4] } 15. h4 { Quite weakening } (15. h3?! { Weakening } 15... h4 16. g4 { Now if black manages to get the knight to e6 then it would control all of the dark-squares nicely } { [%cal Yf6e6] } 16... Nh7! { Preparing Ng5-e6 next } { [%cal Gh7g5,Gg5e6] } 17. O-O Ng5 18. Nc3 c6 19. Kh1 Nge6 { Taking full control of the dark-squares } { [%csl Bf4] } 20. Ne2 { Mousavi, Se - Edouard, R., 0-1, 1st Stars Cup 2016, https://lichess.org/X0TKFm4h } 20... g5 $17 { Best } { [%cal Bd8f6,Ge6f4,Be8g8] } (20... Qf6 21. g5 Nxg5 22. f4 Nxe2 23. Qxe2 Ne6 24. f5 Nf4 { Giving back the pawn } 25. Bxf4 exf4 26. Rxf4 Qe5 $15 { With a clearly better position as the bishops are not even close to being comparable in value } { [%csl Ba7,Rg2] })) 15... O-O (15... Ng4 16. O-O Qf6 { Too optimistic perhaps } 17. c5! { Gives white a good position } { Esipenko, Andrey - Najer, Evgeniy, 1-0, Moscow Nutcracker 6th, 2020, https://lichess.org/lOgplFqQ }) 16. O-O Ng4 17. Kh1 (17. c5 { Not to be concerned of since } 17... Ne6! { Puts pressure on c5 and white will have to take on d6 at some point which would give black a nice center } { [%cal Bd8e7] }) (17. Nc3 { Going back with the knight } 17... f5! 18. Bg5 Qd7 19. exf5 Nxf5 { Increasing pressure on white's structure } { [%csl Rg3,Rf2,Rh4] } 20. Ne4 c6 { Preparing d5 next } { [%cal Bd6d5] } 21. Bh3 Nd4 { Threatening Nf3 } { [%cal Rd4f3] } 22. Bg2 { Black can repeat or go Ne6 now } 22... Ne6 $36 { With d5 next and white is fighting for survival } { [%cal Bd6d5] }) 17... Ne6 { Attacking f2 } { [%csl Rf2] } 18. Qe1 (18. Qe2 Nf4! { A nice move } 19. Qf3 Nxg2 20. Kxg2 Qd7 21. Nc3 c6 { Possibly f5 coming next, so white should probably play Qf5 } { [%cal Bf7f5] } 22. Qf5 Qxf5 23. exf5 b5 { Looks good for black } { [%cal Bf8d8] }) 18... b5 19. Nb2 Nd4 { Preventing f3 as after } 20. f3?! (20. Nd1 Qd7 { Possibly f5 coming next } { [%cal Bf7f5] } 21. f3 Nc2 22. Qe2 Nd4 $10 { Repetition }) 20... Nc2 21. Qe2 Nge3 22. Rfc1 f5! 23. Nd1 f4 24. Nxe3 Nxe3 25. c5 Nxg2 26. Kxg2 fxg3 27. Bg5 Qd7 28. Kxg3 Rf7 $17 { With a big advantage } { [%cal Ba8f8] }) (12. O-O b5! 13. Nb2 { Clearly something has gone wrong for the knight to land on b2 } (13. cxb5 axb5 14. Nb2? (14. Qc2 Rxa4 15. Qxc6+ Qd7 16. Qc2 O-O { With a fantastic position }) 14... Rxa3 $17 { Wins a pawn }) 13... O-O { And with the plan of Ba7 and Nd4 very soon } { [%cal Bd4a7,Gc6d4] })) 9... axb6 { Opening up the a-file at the cost of weakening the pawn structure } 10. Bb2 Nd7! { Played by Karjakin against Nakamura, preparing Nf8-e6 } { [%cal Gd7f8,Gf8e6] } 11. Be2 h5! $140 { A really clever idea, the point is that if an exchange on f3 was to happen then black cannot play Bg4 to take over the light-squares! } 12. O-O (12. b5 { Weakens the c5 square } { [%csl Rc5] } 12... Ne7 { With some nice knight maneuvers coming in and the b2 bishop does not do much there } { [%csl Rb2][%cal Be7g6,Bg6f4,Bf4e6,Gd7c5] } 13. Bc1 { Relocating the bishop } 13... Nc5 14. h3 Bxf3 15. Bxf3 { With 2 knights against 2 bishops } 15... Ng6! { Necessary } { [%cal Yg6h4,Gg6f4] } 16. Bxh5 Nxd3+ 17. Qxd3 Rxh5 { With a very safe and active position } 18. Qd5 Qc8 { With Ne7 to kick the queen out next } { [%cal Bg6e7] }) (12. h3 { Forcing the trade, but not best as black is very solid after } 12... Bxf3 13. Bxf3 g6 { A very safe position, white cannot play Bg4 } 14. h4 (14. b5?! Nd4! $15 { And the exchange on d4 would give black the e5 square to plant the knight in }) 14... Nf8 { Relocating the knight } { [%cal Bf8e6] } 15. b5 { And here Nd4 would not be as good as the knight has moved to f8 and cannot immediately jump into e5 } 15... Na5 16. g3 (16. d4?! { Going all in } 16... Nxc4 17. Bc3 Ne6 $17 { Just keeping the pawn }) 16... Ne6 17. Bg2 Qe7 { The knight on a5 is blocking the a-file so that white cannot attack on that side. Black can castle long and if white castles short then g5 would be deadly } { [%csl Ga5][%cal Be8c8,Rg6g5,Be6c5] }) 12... Bxf3 { Important to take now otherwise Ne1 would lead to a favorable exchange for white } 13. Bxf3 Qf6 { And the pawn is untouchable on account of Qh4/h6 } 14. Be2 g5! { A positional move preventing f4! Very important } 15. Qd2 (15. Bxh5? Qh6 16. g4 Nf6 { White will struggle to justify the weaknesses created after black wins back the pawn } { [%csl Rh5] }) 15... Nf8 { Black is one move away from playing Ne6 and completely dominating the dark-squares } { [%cal Bf8e6] } 16. f4! { Absolutely necessary } 16... gxf4 17. Qxf4 (17. Rxf4??) 17... Qxf4 18. Rxf4 Ng6 { Black is ready to consolidate in any other case of not Rf5 } { [%cal Be8e7,Ga8f8,Gc6d4,Yg6e5] } 19. Rf5 { Threatening the h5 pawn } (19. Rf2 Ke7 20. Raf1 Raf8 21. Rf5 f6 22. g3 h4 23. Bh5 Rfg8 24. g4 Nd8 { Preparing Ne6 next to jump into f4. Black is more than fine here } { [%cal Bd8e6,Gg6f4] }) 19... Rh7 { Just a nice prophylactic move to step away from the long diagonal and defending f7 } (19... h4 { An alternative } 20. Bh5 Nf4 21. Rxf4 (21. Bxf7+ Ke7 { With some problems on d3 } { [%csl Rd3] } 22. Rxf4 exf4 23. Bxh8 Rxh8 24. Bd5 Nd4 $44 { With excellent compensation } { [%cal Rc7c6] }) 21... Rxh5 $10) 20. Rxh5 (20. Bxh5 Nf4 { With d3 falling next } { [%csl Rd3] } 21. Rxf4 { Complicating matters } 21... exf4 22. Be2 Ne5 23. Rf1 Ng6 { Still black remains better } 24. Bg4 c5 25. b5 Ne5 26. Be2 Rh4 27. Bc1 Ng6 { White is fighting for equality }) (20. Raf1 Nd4 21. Bxd4 exd4 { With Ne5 next } { [%cal Bg6e5] } 22. Bxh5 Ne5 $15 { Loose pawns drop off } { [%csl Rd3,Ra3] }) 20... Rxh5 21. Bxh5 Nf4 { d3 drops off } { [%csl Rh5,Rd3] } 22. Bd1 Nxd3 23. Bc3 Ke7 { With Nd4 next, and white's rook is tied down to defending a3 and so black is more than fine here } { [%csl Ra3][%cal Bc6d4] }) (4. h3 Bb4 5. Nd5 e4 6. Nxb4 Nxb4 7. Nd4 O-O 8. a3 c5 $15) (4. d3 { Leads to a reversed Scheveningen. }) 4... d5 (4... Bb4 $140 { A very serious option, we can possibly get a position similar to the reversed Rossolimo where white has the bishop pair but doubled c-pawns } 5. Bg2 (5. Nd5) 5... Bxc3 6. bxc3) 5. cxd5 Nxd5 6. Bg2 { Reaches a Reversed Accelerated Dragon } 6... Nb6 7. O-O Be7 8. a3 { Preparing b4 } { [%cal Gb2b4] } (8. d3 O-O 9. Be3 { Another typical setup } 9... Re8 10. Rc1 Bf8 { Very calm and controlled } 11. Ne4 { Preparing Nc5 } (11. d4 { Liquidates } 11... exd4 12. Nxd4 Nxd4 13. Bxd4 c6 $10 { Completely equal with many pieces traded off }) 11... Nd4 12. Bxd4 (12. Nc5) 12... exd4 13. Nc5 { White has some ideas to play Nb3 } { [%cal Yc5b3] } 13... a5! 14. Nb3 (14. a4 c6) 14... g5! { Very surprising, but the point is that d4 is untouchable as either the g4 or a4 advance would hurt white } { [%cal Rg5g4,Ra5a4,Bf8g7] } 15. Qd2 g4 16. Nh4 { Granda Zuniga, J. - So, W., 0-1, chess.com IoM Masters, 2016, https://lichess.org/hImo2noK } 16... c6 17. Be4 a4 18. Nc5 Re5 19. b4 axb3 20. Nxb3 f5 21. Bh1 Qd6 $17 $36 { With an excellent and active position } { [%cal Rf8h6,Gc8e6] }) (8. b3 O-O 9. Bb2 Re8 10. Rc1 Bf8 11. d3 Nd4 12. Nd2 c6 13. e3 Ne6 14. Nf3 Ng5) 8... O-O 9. b4 Be6 (9... Re8! { [%csl Be5] } 10. b5 (10. d3 Bf8 11. Bb2 a5 12. b5 Nd4 13. Nd2 (13. e3 Nxf3+ 14. Bxf3 a4 15. Rc1 Ra5 16. Re1 Qd7) 13... a4 14. e3 Ne6 15. Nc4 Nxc4 16. dxc4 Nc5) 10... Nd4 11. Nxe5 Bf6 12. f4 (12. Nf3 Nxe2+ 13. Nxe2 Bxa1) 12... Bxe5 13. fxe5 Rxe5) 10. Rb1! { Important to cover the b3 square } { [%csl Bb3] } (10. b5 Nd4 { Doesn't win the e5 pawn as Bb3 would hurt } 11. Rb1 (11. Nxe5?? Bb3) 11... f6 12. e3 Nxf3+ 13. Bxf3 Qc8 { Transposes to 10.Rb1 f6 11.b5 }) (10. d3 a5 11. b5 Nd4 { Similar to 10.Rb1 f6 11.d3 } 12. Nd2 c6 13. bxc6 Nxc6 { Looks fine for black }) 10... f6 { Defending the pawn } 11. d3 (11. b5 Nd4 12. e3 Nxf3+ 13. Bxf3 c6! { A very important gambit equalizer } (13... Qc8 14. a4 a5 15. bxa6 Rxa6) 14. bxc6 bxc6 15. Bxc6 Rc8 16. Bb7 Rc7 17. Bf3 Qd3 18. Be2 Qd7 19. a4 Rd8 20. Nb5 Rcc8 21. d3 Bh3 22. Re1 a6 23. Nc3 Qc6 24. Qb3+ Kh8 25. e4 Bc5 26. a5 Be6 27. Qd1 Nd7) 11... a5 { Best to provoke b5 even though it makes it harder to play c6 } 12. b5 Nd4 13. Nd2 (13. e3 Nxf3+ 14. Bxf3 Qc8 { Just harmless } { [%cal Bf8d8] } 15. Qc2 Rd8 16. Bg2 (16. Rd1 Bg4) (16. a4 Bb4) 16... a4 17. d4 exd4 18. exd4 Bf8 19. Re1 Bf5 20. Qa2+ Kh8) 13... Qc8 14. e3 Nf5 15. Qc2 a4 16. Rd1 Nd6 17. Nde4 Nxe4 18. Nxe4 Bd5 19. Nc5 Bxg2 20. Kxg2 Bxc5 21. Qxc5 Qe6 22. Bb2 Rf7 23. Bc3 Rd7 24. Bb4 Rad8 25. e4 f5 26. exf5 Qxf5 27. Re1 Nd5 28. Qc4 Rf7 29. Rb2 h6 (29... Nxb4! 30. Qxb4 (30. axb4 Qf3+ 31. Kg1 Rxd3 32. Qa2 h5! { [%cal Rh5h4] } 33. h4 e4 { [%cal Re4e3] } 34. Rbe2 Rd4 35. Rc1 Qb3) 30... Rd4 31. Qc5 Qf3+ 32. Kg1 Rxd3 33. Qxe5 h6 34. Rbe2 (34. Ra2 Rxa3) 34... Rxa3 35. Qd4 Rf6 $15 { Black is the only one playing for a win }) 30. Qe4 Qd7 31. Qxe5 Qxb5 32. Qe4 Qd7 33. Qe6 Qxe6 34. Rxe6 Nxb4 35. Rxb4 Rxd3 36. f4 Rxa3 37. Rxb7 Ra2+ 38. Kh3 Rc2 39. Ra6 Rf5 40. Rxa4 Rh5+ 41. Kg4 Rhxh2 42. Ra8+ Kh7 43. Rbb8 Rc4 44. Rf8 Kg6 45. Rae8 h5+ 46. Kf3 Rcc2 47. Re6+ Kh7 48. Re3 Rhf2+ 49. Ke4 Rc6 50. Rf5 Kh6 51. Rd5 h4 52. gxh4 Rc4+ 53. Kd3 Rfxf4 54. Re6+ Kh7 55. Rh5+ Kg8 56. Re7 Rfd4+ 57. Ke3 Rd1 58. Ke2 Rg1 59. Rg5 Rxg5 60. hxg5 Kh7 61. Ke3 Kg6 62. Rd7 Rc5 63. Re7 Rc1 64. Kd2 Rc6 65. Ke2 Rc3 66. Kd2 Rc4 67. Kd3 Rc5 68. Ke3 c6 69. Rc7 Re5+ 70. Kf4 Rf5+ 71. Ke4 Rf1 72. Ke3 Re1+ 73. Kf3 Rc1 74. Kg4 Rc3 75. Kf4 Rc4+ 76. Kf3 Rc2 77. Kg4 Rc1 78. Kf4 c5 79. Kg4 c4 