1. c4 { As King's Indian Defence players, it is best to try and transpose to our prized opening. } (1. g3 d5 2. Nf3 g6 3. d4 Bg7) 1... g6 { Most accurate to start with g6. } (1... d5!? { Morra Reversed! (s. Studie) } 2. cxd5 c6 3. dxc6 Nxc6) (1... Nf6 2. g3 (2. Nc3 c5! { Avoids an early e4-e5 } 3. e4 Nc6! { Transposing back to the Anti-English } 4. Nf3 g6 5. d4!? cxd4 6. Nxd4 Nxd4 7. Qxd4 d6 { And black transposes to a Maróczy Bind in the Accelerated Dragon } 8. Be2 Bg7 9. Be3 O-O 10. Qd2 a5) 2... c5 3. Bg2 d5! 4. cxd5 Nxd5 5. Nc3 Nc7 6. Nf3 Nc6 { Reversed Maróczy Bind https://lichess.org/study/EUELGmHq/pJYBYgmH } { [%cal Ge7e5] }) 2. Nc3 (2. d4 Nf6 { Transposes }) 2... Bg7 3. g3 Nf6 4. Bg2 O-O { White has to decide how to play the position. } { [%cal Gd2d3,Ge2e3,Gg1f3,Ge2e4] } 5. e4 (5. Nf3 d6 6. O-O c5 7. d3 (7. d4 Nc6 8. d5 (8. dxc5 dxc5 9. Be3 Be6 10. Bxc5 Qa5 11. Ba3 Bxc4) 8... Na5 9. Nd2 (9. b3 Nxd5 10. cxd5) 9... a6 10. Qc2 b5 11. cxb5 axb5 12. Nxb5 Qb6) 7... Nc6 8. Bg5 (8. Bd2 Qd7 $140 { Preparing to fianchetto the LSB by protecting the knight } 9. a3 b6 10. Rb1 Bb7 11. b4 Nd4 12. Nxd4 (12. e3 Nxf3+ 13. Bxf3 Bxf3 14. Qxf3 Rac8) 12... Bxg2 13. Kxg2 cxd4 14. Nb5 d5 15. Nxd4 dxc4) 8... h6 9. Bd2 Be6 10. Qc1 Kh7) (5. e3 { [%cal Bg1e2,Bd2d3,Bd2d4] } 5... c5 6. Nge2 (6. d4 cxd4 7. exd4 Nc6 8. Nge2 (8. Nf3 d5 9. cxd5 Nxd5) 8... e6 { s.o. }) 6... Nc6 7. d3 (7. d4 cxd4 8. exd4 e6 9. O-O d5 10. cxd5 Nxd5) 7... e6 8. O-O d5 9. cxd5 (9. b3 b6 10. Bb2 Bb7) 9... exd5 10. d4 cxd4 11. Nxd4 { [%cal Gf8e8,Gc8g4,Gc6d4] } (11. exd4 Re8 12. Nf4?! Bg4 13. Qb3 (13. f3 Bd7 { We have induced a weakness. } 14. Ncxd5 Nxd5 15. Nxd5 Nxd4 { [%cal Bd7c6] }) 13... Nxd4 14. Qxb7 Nf3+ 15. Kh1 Ne1)) (5. d3 c5! { Although we can continue with d6 instead, c5 allows us to easily transpose if white plays the e3 - d3 setup or if they choose the Botvinnik setup with e4. } 6. Nf3 d5 7. cxd5 Nxd5 8. Bd2 Nc6 9. O-O b6 10. Nxd5 Qxd5 11. Bc3 Bb7) 5... c5! { White choose to adopt the Botvinnik setup. The entire idea is to create a bind over the position and control the d5 square. The drawback of this setup is the weak d4 square. Immediately, we need to grasp full control of d4 with c5. } { [%csl Rd4] } 6. Nge2 Nc6 7. O-O (7. d3 d6 8. O-O { A transposition to the mainline of the Botvinnik system. }) 7... d6 (7... Ne8!? { An interesting alternative, we delay d6 so that we can get our knight to e6 before white plays d4. } { [%csl Yd4][%cal Be8c7,Bc7e6,Be6d4] } 8. d3 Nc7 9. Be3 Ne6 10. Qd2 { We have good control of the central squares and are therefore better from a positional perspective. On the other hand, we are behind on development, at risk for a kingside attack with f4, and lack a concrete plan from here on out in general. Therefore, although 7. Ne8 can be a good surprise weapon, it feel to uncomfortable to recommend this entirely. } { [%csl Ge5,Gd4][%cal Rf2f4] } 10... Ned4) 8. d3! { If you have any experience in the Sicilian, you would be able to compare this position to those found in the Botvinnik setup against the Closed Sicilian. The position is practically the same, the colors are just reversed! } 8... Rb8! { Our plan is simple: we want to expand on the queenside and create counter-play there in the case of a kingside attack.
Note that 8. Ne8 is an alternative but it's perhaps better to play like this a move earlier. } { [%cal Bb7b5,Ba7a6] } 9. h3 (9. Rb1! a6 { White now has a choice: either allow b5 and try to follow suit with b4 or to slow up our break. } 10. a3 (10. a4 { This is how white would play in the mainline but it's not clear why he's put his rook on b1 here. } { [%csl Rb1] } 10... Bg4! { An important idea to remember in these structures. As the fight is over the dark squares, and d4 in particular, the knight is more important than the light-squared bishop. } { [%cal Bg4e2] } 11. f3 { White would rather not allow the exchange. } (11. h3 Bxe2 12. Qxe2 { White isn't able to prevent us opening the queenside with b5 when we have a very pleasant position. White's g2 bishop isn't very helpful here. }) 11... Bd7 12. Be3 Ne8 { The typical rerouting, the c7 square is a great one for our knight. } { [%cal Be8c7,Bb7b5] } 13. d4 { White can transform the game into a Maroczy Bind structure he can sometimes hope for a small edge but here we're fine. } 13... cxd4 14. Nxd4 Nxd4 15. Bxd4 b5 { The problem for white is the f2-f3 move. That has weakened his king and blocked in the bishop. We are doing just fine in this position. } { [%csl Rg1,Rf3] }) 10... b5 11. cxb5 axb5 12. b4 cxb4 13. axb4 e5!? { An odd looking move but in this symmetrical structure our f6 knight is better placed to control the centre than white's on e2. Therefore White's only try is to push the f pawn. } 14. h3 { Taking control of the g4 square: both preparing Be3 without it being hit by Ng4 and f2 f4 without allowing us to exchange our light-squared bishop with Bg4 - Bxe2. } { [%csl Yg4] } 14... Be6 { We prepare d5. } { [%cal Bd6d5] } 15. f4 { White's only try. } 15... exf4 16. gxf4 (16. Nxf4 Nh5! { Nh5 works since it reveals an attack on the c3 knight. } { [%csl Rc3,Rg3] } 17. Nce2 Nxf4 18. Nxf4? Ba2) 16... Ne7!? { A highly complicated position, and not the kind of thing white was hoping for. He stakes everything on his expansion on the kingside. } { [%csl Yf5] } 17. Be3 Qd7 18. Kh2 { From here on, we can continue by breaking in the center with f5 or d5. } { Karlsson, Lars - Grigore, George Gabriel, 1/2-1/2, Sitges op 34th, 2008, https://lichess.org/DNtRjjT2 } 18... Nh5 (18... Rfc8) 19. Nd4 d5 (19... Rfc8 20. Nxe6 Qxe6 21. Ne2 Ra8 (21... Nd5 22. Bd2 Ne7 23. Be3 Ra8) 22. Nd4 Qd7) 20. Nxe6 (20. e5 Rfc8 21. Nxe6 fxe6 22. Ne2 Nf5) 20... fxe6 21. Ne2 d4 22. Bd2 g5 23. fxg5 Rxf1 24. Qxf1 Rf8 25. Qc1 Ng6) (9. f4 Bg4! { We see this concept again. We win the fight for the d4-square. } { [%csl Gd4] } 10. h3 Bxe2 11. Nxe2 { [%csl Re5][%cal Re4e5,Re5f6,Rg2c6] } 11... Nd7! { We want to play b5 but we need to be careful with our knight still on f6. If we would have played b5 instead, e5, hitting both knights, would've been rather embarrassing.
It's difficult for White's kingside attack to advance any further. } { [%cal Bb7b5,Bc6d4] }) 9... a6 { Preparing b5. } { [%cal Bb7b5] } (9... Nd7 10. Be3 (10. f4 a6 11. Be3 b5) 10... Nd4) 10. a4 (10. Be3 b5 11. cxb5 (11. b3 b4 12. Na4 (12. Nd5 Nd7 { [%cal Be7e6] }) 12... e5 13. f4 exf4 14. gxf4 Nh5 { [%csl Ra4][%cal Bf7f5,Bc8d7,Bf8e8] }) 11... axb5 12. d4 b4 13. Nd5 Nd7 (13... Ba6 14. dxc5 (14. Nxf6+ Bxf6) 14... dxc5 15. Bxc5 Nd7 16. Be3 (16. Bd4?? Bxe2) 16... Nde5! { [%csl Gc4,Gd3,Rb2][%cal Be5d3,Be5c4,Re7e6] } 17. Re1 e6 18. Ndf4 Nc4) 14. e5 (14. dxc5 dxc5 15. Qa4 Bb7 16. Rab1 e6 17. Ndf4 { [%cal Gc6e5,Gd8e7,Gb8a8] }) 14... cxd4 15. Nxd4 Nxd4) (10. Rb1 b5 11. b3 (11. cxb5 axb5 12. b4 Nxb4 13. a3 Nc6) 11... Bb7 12. cxb5 (12. Be3 b4 13. Nd5 Nd7) 12... axb5 13. Nxb5 Ba6 14. a4 Bxb5 15. axb5 Rxb5) 10... Ne8! { [%csl Gc7,Gd4][%cal Ge8c7,Bc7b5,Bb7b5,Yc7e6] } 11. Be3 (11. f4 Nc7 12. f5 (12. Be3 b5) (12. a5 Ne6 13. f5 Ned4) 12... b5 13. axb5 axb5 14. cxb5 Nxb5) 11... b5! $146 12. axb5 axb5 { [%csl Rb2][%cal Rg7b2,Rb8b2] } 13. Nxb5 (13. cxb5 Nd4) 13... Nc7 14. Nxc7 (14. Nec3 Nxb5 15. Nxb5 Bxb2 16. Rb1 Bg7 17. Qd2 Ba6) 14... Qxc7 { [%csl Rb2] } 15. Rb1 Rxb2 16. Rxb2 Bxb2 17. Qb3 Bg7 *


