1. c4 { Okay, this brings me to the final chapter, and honestly one of the hardest chapters to write. Why? Because I play the English myself as White and I love to face the Kings Indian as white because of the strong Botvinnik attack. White has not committed the knight to f3, so the Botvinnik is still possible. } 1... d6 2. Nc3 g6 { My recommendation here is naturally the one where I have had the most difficulties facing as White. } 3. g3 Bg7 4. Bg2 e5 5. e4 { The start of the Botvinnik setup. Playing Nf6 against it is low-key be asking for trouble. Though I may have a biased opinion because I have had success on the White side. } (5. e3 Nc6 6. Nge2 h5 7. h4 Bg4 8. O-O Qd7) 5... Nc6 6. Nge2 f5 7. d3 Nf6 8. O-O O-O 9. Nd5! { The best move in this position } (9. h3? f4! 10. gxf4 Nh5 11. fxe5 dxe5 12. f4 Qh4 (12... exf4 13. Nxf4 Qd4+ 14. Kh2) 13. Qe1 Qxe1 14. Rxe1 exf4 15. Rf1 g5) 9... Be6 10. Bg5! Qd7 11. Qd2 Rf7 12. b4 (12. f4 fxe4 13. dxe4 Raf8 { Lines where White plays f4 are a bit overextending from experience. Black has no real weaknesses to exploit, so f4 is a bit overextending with space. It may leave permanent pawn weaknesses behind. } 14. f5 gxf5 15. exf5 Bxf5) 12... fxe4 13. dxe4 Raf8 14. a4 Nb8 { Black has a solid position, with a plan of c6 to kick back the knight on d5. The position is fairly balanced. } *


