1. c4 { The English Opening, white is generally aiming for a small edge or atypical positions to outplay their opponents in the middlegame } 1... e6 (1... Nf6 { This is another way to invite transpositions into 1.d4 openings, although this does have 1 issue } 2. Nc3 e6 (2... g6 { King's Indian Players may want to play this way but again have to know systems where white does not play d4 entirely } 3. g3 (3. e4 { Transposes into the normal King's Indian } 3... d6 4. d4 Bg7) 3... Bg7 4. Bg2 O-O 5. e4 { With a Botvinnik setup where white sets up with d3 next } { [%cal Gd2d3] }) 3. e4 { The Mikenas-Carls Variation, very sharp since black doesn't get to play d5 without some drawback of being pushed around following e5. Objectively still equal but black needs to follow a tight rope to equality in a dynamic position } 3... d5 { Best move } 4. e5 { The critical test } (4. cxd5 { This exchange is playable although not the most critical } 4... exd5 5. e5 (5. exd5 Nxd5 { Transposes into the Accelerated Panov variation (1. e4 c6 2. c4 d5 3. cxd5 cxd5 4. exd5 Nf6 5. Nc3 Nxd5) }) 5... Ne4 { This is fine for black } (5... d4 6. exf6 dxc3) 6. Nf3 (6. Nxe4 dxe4 7. Qa4+ Nc6 8. Qxe4 Be6 { Best, keeping more pieces on the board make it easier for black to prove active compenstaion } (8... Qd4! { The most common move, this is also good } 9. Qxd4 Nxd4 10. Bd3 Bc5 $15 { Black plays for activity with the bishop on c1 still struggling to develop. Black has an edge despite being down a pawn although active compensation needs to be proven } { [%csl Rc1][%cal Gc8d7,Gd7c6,Be8c8] })) (6. d4 Bb4 { A slight issue } 7. Qa4+ { Provoking the knight to come to c6 } 7... Nc6 8. Bb5 O-O! { Important to get the king out of a possible check } 9. Ne2 (9. Bxc6 a5! 10. Ne2 bxc6 11. O-O Ba6 12. Rd1 (12. Qc2) 12... f6! 13. Qxc6 Bc4) 9... Nxd4 10. Nxd4 c5 11. Ne2 d4 12. O-O dxc3 13. a3 Nd2! { Only move } 14. axb4 (14. bxc3 Nxf1 15. axb4 a6! 16. Qc2 Bf5! 17. Qxf5 Qd1 18. Bb2 Rfd8!! 19. Nd4 (19. Rxd1? Rxd1 { [%cal Rf1e3] } 20. Ba4 Re1! 21. f4 Ne3+ 22. Kf2 Rxe2+ 23. Kxe2 Nxf5 $19) 19... Ne3+ 20. Rxd1 Nxf5 21. Bc4 (21. Bd3? cxd4 22. c4 (22. Bxf5 dxc3 $19 { [%csl Rb2,Rd1] } 23. Rxd8+ Rxd8 { [%csl Rd1,Rb2] }) 22... Ne7 23. f4 Rac8 24. Kf2 b5! 25. cxb5 axb5 26. Kf3 Nd5 { Improvement } (26... Nc6?! 27. Bxb5 Nxb4 28. Rxd4 Nc2 29. Rd6 Rb8 30. Rxd8+ Rxd8 31. Bc4 (31. e6! { Would have caused some technical issues } 31... fxe6 32. Bc4 Kf7 33. f5 Nd4+ 34. Bxd4 Rxd4 35. Bxe6+ Kf6 { Black is better and should be technically winning, although it would be much more difficult from here }) 31... Rb8 32. e6 Rxb2 33. exf7+ Kf8 34. g4 Nd4+ 35. Ke4 Nc6 36. g5 Rb4 37. Kd5 Nd8 38. Kd4 { Inarkiev, E. - Cheparinov, I., 0-1, World Rapid 2018, https://lichess.org/fCaaF51x }) 27. Bxb5 Nc3! { [%csl Rb5,Rd1] } 28. Bxc3 dxc3 29. Rc1 Rd4 30. Ba6 Rc7 31. b5 Rb4 32. Ke3 Kf8 33. Kd3 Rb3 34. g3 Ke7 $19 { With f6 next and activating the king through the light-squares, and the bishop stuck on a6, this is a lost position for white. } { [%csl Re5][%cal Bf7f6] }) 21... cxd4 22. cxd4 Rac8 23. Bb3 Ne7 24. d5 Nxd5! { Forced } 25. Bxd5 Rc2 { With some problems on the back rank if white moves the bishop } 26. Bxf7+ (26. Ba3 { Also does not escape equality } 26... Ra2! { Only move but good enough } 27. Kf1 Kf8 28. Bc1 Ra1 29. Ke2 Rxc1 30. Rxc1 Rxd5 31. Rc8+ Ke7 32. f4 Rd4 33. Rc7+ Ke6 34. Rxb7 Rxf4 35. g3 Rg4 36. Kf3 Rc4 37. Ke3 h5 38. Rb6+ Kxe5 39. Rxa6 Rxb4 40. Ra5+ $10) 26... Kxf7 27. Rxd8 Rxb2 28. f4 Rxb4 29. f5 a5 30. e6+ Ke7 31. Rd7+ Kf8 32. Rd8+ Ke7 33. Rd7+ Kf8 34. Rf7+ Kg8 35. Rc7 Kf8 36. Rf7+ { Piorun, K. - Wojtaszek, R., 1/2-1/2, Qatar Masters Open 2015, https://lichess.org/vPbZzTzz }) 14... Nxf1 15. bxc3 (15. Kxf1 a6 16. Nxc3 axb5 17. Qxa8 cxb4 { [%csl Rd1,Rc3] } 18. Qa5 Qd3+ 19. Ke1 bxc3 20. Qxc3 Qe4+ 21. Kf1 Be6 22. f3 Qg6 23. Be3 Rc8 24. Qd4 h6 25. Rc1 Rc4 26. Rxc4 bxc4 27. Bd2 b5 { Keeps the game going with an equal position but an exposed white king and good piece play for black } (27... Qd3+ { A lame way to draw } 28. Qxd3 cxd3 29. Kf2 Bc4 30. Ke3 Kf8 { Chatalbashev, B. - Rusev, K., 1/2-1/2, 80th ch-BUL 2016, https://lichess.org/rnL5AixT })) 15... a6 16. Qc2 Bf5 { Transposes to the variation with 14.bxc3 } { Inarkiev, E. - Cheparinov, I., 0-1, World Rapid 2018, https://lichess.org/fCaaF51x }) 6... Bf5 7. d3 (7. Be2 d4 8. Nxe4 Bxe4 9. Bc4 (9. d3 Bd5 10. O-O Nc6 { [%csl Re5] } (10... c5 11. Nd2 Nc6 12. f4 $36)) 9... Bd5 10. Qb3 Bxc4 11. Qxc4 Nc6 12. O-O Qd7 13. d3 Qe6 (13... O-O-O?! 14. Bg5 Re8 15. Rfe1 h6 16. Bd2 g5 17. Re4 $14 { [%csl Rd4] }) 14. Qxe6+ fxe6 15. Ng5 Kd7 16. f4 Be7 17. Ne4 Raf8 18. Bd2 g5! 19. g3 Rhg8 20. Kh1 gxf4 21. gxf4 Rg6 22. Rac1 Rfg8 23. a3 a5 24. Rf3 b6 25. Rh3 R8g7 26. Ng3 Nd8 27. b4 axb4 28. axb4 b5 29. Kg2 Nc6 30. Rb1 Rf7 31. Rh5 Rg8 32. Rh6 Bf8 33. Rh3 Be7 34. Rh6 Bf8 35. Rh3 Be7 { Aronian, L. - Ding Liren, 1/2-1/2, FIDE World Cup 2017, https://lichess.org/OR7H7Abm }) 7... Nxc3 8. bxc3 c5 9. d4 c4 10. g3 (10. a4 Nc6 11. Be2 Be7 12. O-O O-O) 10... Be7 11. Bg2 Bd3 12. Ng1 Qa5 13. Bd2 Nc6 14. Ne2 Ba3 15. O-O O-O 16. Re1 Rad8 17. Bg5 Bb2 18. Bxd8 Rxd8 19. Rc1 Bxe2 20. Qxe2 Bxc1 21. Rxc1 Qa3 22. Qe3 b5 23. e6 f6 24. h4 Ne7 25. Rb1 a6 26. Qd2 Qd6 27. a4 bxa4 28. Qa2 a3 29. Rb4 h5 30. Qxa3 Nc6 31. Ra4 Qxa3 32. Rxa3 a5 33. e7 Nxe7 34. Rxa5 Rb8 35. Bxd5+ Nxd5 36. Rxd5 Rb3 37. Rxh5 Rxc3 38. Rc5 Rc1+ 39. Kg2 c3 40. Kf3 c2 41. Kf4 Rd1 42. Rxc2 Rxd4+ 43. Kf5 Kh7 44. f4 Ra4 45. Rc5 Kh6 46. Rd5 Ra6 47. g4 g6+ 48. Ke4 Ra4+ 49. Rd4 Ra5 50. Rd5 Ra4+ 51. Ke3 Ra3+ 52. Rd3 Ra6 { Carlsen, M. - Aronian, L., 1/2-1/2, GRENKE Chess Classic 2018, https://lichess.org/9Zjy5aC3 }) 4... d4 5. exf6 dxc3 6. bxc3 Qxf6 7. Nf3 (7. d4 { Also possible } 7... e5 8. Nf3 exd4 9. Bg5 Qe6+ 10. Be2 Be7 11. cxd4 Bxg5 12. Nxg5 Qe7 (12... Qg6 13. f4 O-O 14. O-O Nc6 (14... h6)) 13. Nf3) 7... e5 8. Bd3) 2. Nf3 { White generally brings the knight out to f3 first, but of course g3-Bg2 can be played as well } (2. Nc3 { This move order discourages Nf6 since it allows a transposition into the Mikenas-Carls variation } 2... d5 { White should play d4 and transpose to a QGD } 3. g3?! { This is already suspicious because the knight on f3 is vulnerable to a central advance } (3. d4) 3... d4 { Very principled move } 4. Na4 { The only move to not be much worse, but still black has a great game following } (4. Nb1 { This has been played twice, though black gets a great game following } 4... e5 { Building a big center } 5. Bg2 c5 { And another pawn in the center } 6. d3 Bd6 { Black doesn't want to play Nf6 so early since it allows Bg4, the dark-squared bishop being a problem piece for white } 7. e3 Nc6 $17 { And black has an impressive center with white having spent 2 moves playing Nb1-c3-b1. h6 followed by Nf6 and 0-0 will give black a solid advantage, and the capture on d4 will always be met with exd4 so that the e5 pawn doesn't become a target } { [%cal Gh7h6,Bg8f6] }) 4... e5 { Building a big center } 5. Bg2 c5 { With a nice center } 6. d3 h6 { Preventing Bg5 is crucial } 7. Nf3 Nc6 8. O-O Nf6 9. e3 Be7 $17 { Closing down the e-file as to not allow Re1+ with a much better position } 10. exd4 exd4) (2. b3 { This approach of playing a b3 setup is also possible, though it does not fight for the center and so black can create some problems } 2... d5 3. Bb2 { White has no care for the center, and this will cost them } (3. e3 { White has played too many pawn moves in the opening and can be quickly punished with } 3... d4!? { Most principled is to take advantage of white's delayed development and prevent white from occupying the center. Black is ready to play e5 and Nc6 to defend the center } { [%cal Be6e5] } (3... Nf6 { Black can also easily transpose into white's desired e3 and b3 system } 4. Bb2 Be7 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Nc3 b6 { And we enter a transposition }) 4. exd4 Nc6 { This is the point, intending Nxd4! } (4... Qxd4 5. Nc3) 5. Bb2 Nxd4 6. Nf3 Bc5 { Supporting the knight on it's centralized outpost } (6... c5 { Another reasonable option }) 7. b4 { Trying to distract the bishop from defending the knight } (7. g3 { This looks like it makes sense, but after the strong } 7... e5! $17 { Preparing Bg4, white is suddenly in worse shape! e5 cannot be taken on account of Qe7 } { [%cal Gc8g4] }) 7... Qf6! { A nice tactical move, threatening Nxf3 and winning the bishop } 8. Nxd4 Bxd4 9. Bxd4 Qxd4 $15 { And black gets a very comfortable position by preventing d2-d4 and white having worse development! Black already has the edge } 10. Na3 { White intends Nc2 followed by d4, so black should develop accordingly against white's potential setup } 10... Ne7 { The knight belongs on f5 to control the d4 square, a crucial point on the board } { [%cal Be7f5] } 11. Be2 { Finishing development } (11. Nc2 { This works less well } 11... Qe4+ { And the knight is forced to block where white still lags in development }) 11... O-O 12. Nc2 Qf6 13. d4 { White achieves d4, but at the cost of accepting a weak d-pawn! } 13... Rd8 { Nicely putting pressure on it, Nf5 is nex } { [%csl Rd4] } 14. Qd3 Nf5 15. Rd1 Nxd4! { The tactics flow } 16. Nxd4 e5 { Winning the piece back with a pawn up } 17. Qf3 { Only way to muddy the endgame } 17... exd4 18. Qxf6 gxf6 { Black has a bad pawn structure, but the passed extra d-pawn matters! } { [%csl Gd4] } 19. Kd2 Be6 20. Bd3 b6 { Preparing c5 to hold on to the extra pawn } 21. f4 c5 22. bxc5 bxc5 23. Rb1 Rab8 { Not allowing white to activate } 24. Rb3 Rb6 25. Rhb1 Rdb8 { Fighting for the b-file is key } 26. Kc2 Bd7 { Threatening Ba4 and just improving the bishop onto a better diagonal } 27. Kc1 Bc6 28. Ra3 Rxb1+ 29. Bxb1 Rb4 30. Rg3+ Kh8 31. Bd3 Bd7 32. Kc2 Be6 33. a3 Rb8 34. Rf3 Bd7 35. Kc1 Rb3 36. Be4 Rb8 37. Bd3 h6 38. Kc2 Kg7 39. Kc1 Kf8 40. Kc2 Ke7 41. Kc1 h5 42. Bc2 h4 43. Rb3 Rb6 44. Rf3 Be6 45. Bd3 Bg4 46. Rf2 Rb3 47. Kd2 Bd7 48. f5 Rxa3 49. Rf4 Ra2+ 50. Kc1 h3 { Grigoriants, Sergey - Shirov, Alexei, 0-1, RUS-chT, 2008, https://lichess.org/I2ugLvG4 }) 3... d4 { Principled, gaining space and shutting down the bishop. Black will play Nc6 and e5 with a nice advantage } { [%cal Gb8c6,Be6e5] } 4. Nf3 Nc6 5. d3 e5 $15 { And we get a good Reversed Benoni since the bishop does not belong on b2. We can stop here and claim an edge, but also going a bit further to show the typical middlegame makes sense } { [%csl Rb2] } 6. g3 (6. e3 { Challenging the center } 6... Bc5 { Reinforces the center, and threatening dxe3 so white needs to respond in the center } 7. exd4 Nxd4 { And of course taking on d4 wouldn't make white's task simpler while taking on e5 loses to Qe7! } 8. Be2 f6 { Reinforcing the center } 9. O-O Ne7 { With a beautiful bind over the position, Bf5 and 0-0 is next } { [%cal Gc8f5,Be8g8] }) 6... Bb4+!? { The point of this move is to provoke Nbd2 so that white doesn't have the opportunity to play Ba3 and exchange their bad bishop anymore } 7. Nbd2 a5 { Preventing white's a3-b4 advance } 8. a3 Be7 { With Nf6, 0-0, and Re8 next giving black a solid edge } { [%cal Bg8f6,Ge8g8,Yf8e8] }) (2. g3 { This would most likely transpose to the mainline soon, it's just important to know when to play certain moves } 2... d5 3. Bg2 dxc4 { Of course black takes on c4 with the goal of luring the queen to a4-c4 so that black can gain time against the queen on c4 with a6-b5 } 4. Qa4+ Nd7 5. Qxc4 a6 { Intending to kick the queen back with b5 } 6. Qc2 { White usually plays prophylactically to prevent b5 with a tempo } (6. d3 { What happens if white doesn't play Nf3 early on to discourage b5 and Bb7? } 6... Ngf6 7. Qc2 { Typical play, putting a pawn in the center } 7... c5 { Typical play, putting a pawn in the cente } 8. Nc3 { The knight on c3 is not ideal because if black manages to play b5 and white plays a4, then black can advance the pawn further with b4 and attack the knight. The knight on c3 does do one useful thing though in discouraging b5... because once white plays Nf3 and black plays b5, then white can play Ne5! and black's typical response of Nd5 is not possible since the knight on c3 attacks the d5 knight as well } 8... Be7 { Finishing development } { [%csl Rc3][%cal Gb7b5,Ga2a4] } 9. Nf3 O-O 10. O-O Ra7 { Getting the rook off the long diagonal since b5 is not possible before, now b5 is a threat or if white pushes a4 then b6 and Bb7 is fine } { [%cal Bb7b5] } (10... b5? 11. Ne5! $16 { And the typical response of Nd5 is no longer possible as it gives up at least a pawn, white is winning material }) 11. Rd1 b5 12. a4 b4 13. Nb1 Bb7) (6. d4 { White can also delay Nf3 by putting a pawn on d4, though here it can be target with a later c5! and without a knight on f3, cxd4 Nxd4 is not possible } 6... Ngf6 7. Qc2 { Prophylaxis } 7... c5 { And black will play b5 whenever white plays Nf3, this transposes to the mainline } 8. Nf3 b5 9. Ne5 Nd5 10. Nxd7 Bxd7 11. dxc5 Rc8) (6. Nf3 b5) 6... Ngf6 { Finishing development, playing c5 also here is fine } 7. Nf3 { White intends to put a pawn on d4, so it's important to prevent that by playing d4 ourselves! } 7... c5 8. O-O (8. d4 b5 { With Bb7 next } { [%cal Bc8b7] } 9. Ne5 Nd5 { This is generally fine for black }) 8... b5 { Bb7 next giving a fine position and transposing to the mainline }) 2... d5 { The Queen's Gambit Declined approach is a really nice way for black, since it often gets rid of any problems on the long diagonal with the white bishop typically placed on the long diagonal } 3. g3 { White intends to utilise the long diagonal for their bishop and to play against black’s inability to put their bishop on the long diagonal as quickly } (3. e3 { Another big system is to play this c3 and e3 system where white is hoping for a small edge by playing against black's light-squared bishop and often inflicting hanging pawns or an IQP on black's position } 3... Nf6 { White can play b3 or Nc3 } 4. b3 (4. Nc3 { Will soon transpose } 4... Be7 5. b3 (5. d4 O-O { Transposes into the Queen's Gambit Declined but with the dark-squared bishop stuck behind the pawn chain } { [%csl Rc1] }) 5... O-O { Transposes to 4.b3 }) 4... Be7 { Black finishes normal development quickly with Be7 and 0-0 before deciding on what to do with the queenside } 5. Bb2 O-O 6. Nc3 { And black is faced with a positional dilemma here, since black will have to accept one concession or another } (6. Be2 b6 7. O-O (7. cxd5 Nxd5 8. O-O { Not including Nc3? Sooner or later white should } 8... Bb7 9. a3 c5 10. Qc2 (10. d4 cxd4 11. Nxd4 Nc6 12. Nxc6 Bxc6 13. Bf3 Rc8 { [%cal Be7f6] }) (10. d3?! { [%csl Re2] } 10... Nc6!) 10... Nc6 11. Nc3 Rc8 { With a very fine game }) 7... Bb7 { Black gets a very comfortable game as cxd5 allows Nxd5 to keep the bishop's diagonal opened }) 6... b6 { I like this move because black doesn't accept a structural weakness and is threatening Bb7 so that exd5 can be met with Nxd5 to keep the bishop's diagonal opened, this means that white has to act fast and take on d5 } (6... dxc4 { An interesting way to play, though quite unexplored so it's worth trying } 7. Bxc4 (7. bxc4 c5 8. Qc2 Nc6 9. a3 b6 { Also another fine way to play } { [%cal Bc8b7] } 10. Be2 Bb7 11. Rg1 Na5 12. d3) 7... a6 8. a4 (8. Qc2 b5 9. Bd3 Bb7 10. Ne4 Nxe4 11. Bxe4 Bxe4 12. Qxe4 Nd7 { This is perfectly fine for black with c5 and Rc8 next } { [%cal Bc7c5,Ga8c8] }) 8... b6 9. O-O Bb7 10. Ne2 c5 { With an equal game though highly unexplored }) (6... c5 { I think that this gives white exactly what they want } 7. cxd5 Nxd5 (7... exd5 { Slightly inferior to Nxd5 since it doesn't exchange a pair of pieces while still retaining the same structure } 8. d4 $14 { I like white's position already }) 8. Qc2 b6 9. Nxd5 exd5 (9... Qxd5 10. Bd3) 10. d4 Nc6 { Objectively this position is fine, but at some point white can take on c5 and inflict hanging pawns on d5 and c5 which is somewhat of a concession }) 7. cxd5 (7. g3 { This is a rare hybrid system using the double fianchetto that I actually quite like for white as it leads to non-standard positions } 7... Bb7 8. Bg2 c5 { Black can wait on capturing on c4, which is fine but taking on c4 immediately is probably easier } (8... dxc4 { Immediately releases the tension and opens up the light-squared bishop } 9. bxc4 c5 10. O-O Ne4 { This is the equalizing }) 9. O-O Nbd7 10. Ne1!? { This move was suggested to me by NM Dan Gallagher (BishopPair on twitter). The idea is to prevent black from the equalizing plan to go dxc4 and Ne4, and also to advance with f4 next move and try to create an attack! } { [%cal Bf2f4] } (10. Qe2 dxc4 11. bxc4 Ne4 { This equalizes quite handily })) 7... exd5 8. d4 { And the point of white's play is that black ends up with the light-squared bishop on a poor diagonal, though as we will see black is managing to put their other pieces on good square and will be doing well despite having a poor light-squared bishop as it turns out that white has done the same! } { [%csl Rd5,Rb2][%cal Gc8b7] } 8... Bb7 { Developing, fighting for control over e4 } 9. Bd3 Nbd7 10. O-O a6 { An important move! The point is that black is preventing Nb4 so that black is ready for Bd6 at this junction } { [%cal Be7d6] } 11. Ne2!? { Immediately trying to reroute the knight to the kingside and generate some kingside play } { [%cal Ge2g3] } (11. Rc1 Bd6 12. Ne2 Qe7 (12... Re8 13. Nf4) 13. Ng3 (13. Nf4 Ne4 14. Bb1 c6 { [%csl Gd5,Gc6,Gb6,Ga6][%cal Gd1c2] }) 13... g6 14. Qc2 Rfc8 (14... c6 15. Rfe1 Rfe8 16. e4! $132 { This is quite unpleasant }) 15. Rfe1 Ne4) (11. Ne5 Bd6 12. f4 Ne4 { [%cal Bd7f6,Yf7f6] }) 11... Bd6 { Improving the bishop, this is black's whole idea } 12. Ng3 (12. Rc1 Qe7 13. Nf4 Ne4 14. Bb1 c6 { [%cal Ga6a5,Gf8e8] }) 12... g6 { Preventing Nf5, with Re8 coming next to stick a knight on e4! } (12... Re8 { Allowing Nf5 is a bit dangerous } 13. Nf5 Bf8 14. Ne5 $36 { White gets a bit too active for my liking }) 13. Ne5 { White tries to generate a kingside initiative followe } (13. Qc2 { White can also fight for the e4 break, and black should prevent that otherwise their dark-squared bishop will be completely unopposed on the long diagonal } 13... Qe7 { Typically black brings the queen to e7 so that the f8 rook can develop to c8 and defend c7 } 14. Rac1 Rfc8 { Over-protecting the c-pawn so that } (14... c6?! { White is fighting for control over e4, so even though this move is typical for this variation it doesn't fight for the e4 square sufficiently } 15. Rfe1 Rfe8 16. e4! $36 { This is unpleasant }) (14... Rac8 15. Qe2 { Offers white a chance to repeat }) 15. Rfe1 { Preparing e4 } 15... Ne4 { Blockading the pawn with a fine position, black can later play a5 followed by c6 to solidify the queenside if allowed so that the rook can go to e8 and put some e-file pressure. Later black can also think about expanding with b5 and getting queenside play } { [%cal Ga6a5,Bc7c6,Yb6b5,Gc8e8] }) 13... Ne4! { The cleanest equalizer, this is a very typical response to white trying to setup a kind of stonewall or put a Pillsbury knight on e5 } (13... Qe7!? { Putting pressure on the knight this way is a bit useless since white has the reply } 14. f4 { And the Pillsbury knight on e5 is not easy to deal with } { [%csl Ge5] } 14... Ne4 15. Bxe4 dxe4 16. Nxd7 Qxd7 17. d5! { White gets some good play across the long diagonal } 17... f5 18. Qd4 $36 { White has some play even though the position is objectively equal after Kf7, but clearly white is having all the fun }) 14. Bxe4 { This is most challenging, since the resulting pawn on e4 can be a target potentially } (14. Qc2 { Keeping the tension is also possible, but black can inflict some weaknesses following } 14... Nxg3 15. hxg3 Qe7 { Threatening Nxe5, and also now f4 is not possible since the e3 pawn becomes too weak } 16. Rac1 { The point being that c7 will drop and white will get active if allowed } (16. f4 f6 17. Nxd7 Qxd7) 16... Rac8 { Defending the c-pawn } 17. Qe2 Nxe5 18. dxe5 Bxe5 { Liquidating } 19. Bxe5 Qxe5 20. Bxa6 Bxa6 21. Qxa6 Qd6 { Preparing c5 next and play later on the a-file following Ra8, this is at least equal and black can play on against white's structural weakness } {