age is limited }) 17... Bf6 18. Nf4 Bxb2 19. Nxb2 Qf6 20. Nc4 Rf7 21. d6 c6 { [%cal Ga8d8] } 22. b4 Rd8 { And black has 2 ideas } { [%cal Gb6b5,Yg6g5,Gd7e5,Rf5f4] }) 15... Nf6 { White has many moves here that are not worse than each other, but with the simple idea or Rfe8, Qe7, and maybe expanding with a5 or c5 to strike the center gives black decent counterplay } { [%cal Bf8e8,Ga6a5,Gd8e7,Yc7c5] } (15... f5 16. Nxd7 (16. Qc4+ Kg7 17. Nxd7 Qxd7 18. d5+) 16... Qxd7 17. d5) 16. d5! { This is quite challenging and gets quite forcing } 16... Nxd5 (16... Bxd5?? 17. Qc3! $18 { Matthias Bleubaum vs Vahap Sanal, FIDE Grand Swiss 2023, Round 4 } 17... h5 18. Nd7 Qxd7 19. Qxf6) 17. Nxe4 Bxe5 18. Bxe5 Nb4 19. Qc4 Bxe4 20. Qxe4 (20. Qxb4 Bxg2! 21. Rfd1 (21. Kxg2?? Qd5+ $17) 21... Qg5 22. Qf4 Qxf4 23. Bxf4 Bf3 24. Rd7 c6 25. h4 Rfd8 26. Rb7 b5 27. Kh2 c5 28. Rc7 Rac8 29. Rc1 Rxc7 30. Bxc7 Rd5 31. Bb6 $10) 20... Qe7 $10 { [%cal Bf8e8,Ga8d8] }) 3... dxc4! (3... Nf6 { Also possible to include Nf6 } 4. Bg2 dxc4 5. Qa4+ Nbd7 6. Qxc4 a6 (6... c5 7. O-O b6 (7... a6 8. Qc2 b5 9. a4) 8. d4 Bb7) 7. Qc2 c5 (7... Ra7!? { A very rare move, but the second choice of Stockfish! The idea is just to continue with c5 and b5 next with Bb7 so that the rook is not on the long diagonal of white's bishop. } 8. d4 (8. O-O c5 9. d4 (9. a4 b6 10. d4 Bb7) 9... b6) 8... c5 9. Nc3 b6 10. O-O Bb7 11. e4 cxd4 (11... Be7) 12. Nxd4) 8. O-O (8. Nc3 Qc7 9. O-O (9. d4 b5 10. Bf4 Qa7 11. dxc5 Bxc5 12. O-O Bb7 13. Rad1 O-O 14. Ng5 Bxg2 15. Kxg2 h6 16. Nge4 Rac8 17. Qd3 Be7)) (8. a4 b6 9. O-O (9. Ne5 Nd5) 9... Bb7 10. Nc3 (10. d3 Be7)) 8... b5) 4. Qa4+ (4. Bg2 { White can also sacrifice the pawn for more long-term compensation } 4... a6 5. O-O (5. a4?! Nc6 6. Na3 (6. O-O e5 { [%cal Gc8e6] } 7. Na3 e4! 8. Ne1 Qd5 9. d3 cxd3 10. Nxd3 (10. exd3 Nf6) 10... Nf6 $17) 6... e5! 7. Nxc4 e4 8. Ng1 (8. Nfe5 Nxe5 9. Nxe5 Qd4) 8... Bc5 9. Bxe4 Bxf2+ 10. Kxf2 Qd4+ 11. Ne3 Qxe4 12. Nf3 Nf6 13. d3 Qe7 14. Qb3 O-O $17 { [%cal Bf8e8,Gc8e6] }) 5... Nf6 (5... Nc6 { Trying to hold on to the pawn with b5 is not very good } 6. Qc2 b5 7. b3 Nb4 8. Qb2 cxb3 9. Ne5 Nd5 10. a4 { Prithu, Gupta - Grandelius, N., 0-1, GAMMA Reykjavik Open 2019, https://lichess.org/99s4kBGV } 10... bxa4 11. Rxa4 Ngf6 12. Qxb3 Bd6 13. Bb2 O-O 14. Rc1 c5 { White has an edge, but it should definitely lead to equality eventually. The position is very dynamic and slightly uncomfortable in my opinion }) 6. Qc2 Bd6 7. Qxc4 (7. a4?! O-O 8. Qxc4 c5 { With Nc6 next } { [%cal Bb8c6] } 9. d4 cxd4 10. Qxd4 Nc6 11. Qh4 Be7 { Successfully neutralizing white's Catalan! } 12. Nc3 e5 { [%cal Gc8f5,Gc8e6,Ye6f5] } 13. Rd1 Qa5 14. Bg5 h6 15. Bxf6 Bxf6 16. Qh5 Be6 { With a good position }) 7... b5 8. Qc2 (8. Qb3 Bb7 9. a4 Nbd7 (9... b4 { [%csl Gc4] } 10. d3) 10. axb5 Bd5 11. Qc2 (11. Qd3 Be4 12. Qb3 axb5 13. Rxa8 Qxa8) 11... axb5 12. Rxa8 Qxa8 13. Na3 (13. Nc3 Bc6 14. d3 O-O 15. Bg5 h6 16. Bxf6 Nxf6 17. Rc1 Rc8 18. Qd1 Qb7 19. Ne1 Bxg2 20. Nxg2 c5 21. Qb3 Rb8 22. Ne1 Qa6 23. Nf3 c4 24. dxc4 bxc4 25. Qc2 Bb4 26. Nd1 Rc8 27. Ne5 c3 28. Nd3 Nd5 29. e4 Bf8 30. exd5 cxb2 31. Qxb2 Qxd3 32. dxe6 Rxc1 33. exf7+ Kxf7 34. Qxc1 Bc5 35. Nc3 Qf3 36. Qc2 Kg8 37. Ne4 Ba7 38. Kf1 Kh8 39. Qc8+ Kh7 40. Qb7 Qf5 41. Kg2 Bd4 42. Qc6 Qf7 43. Qd6 Bf6 44. h4 Be7 45. Qd7 Kg8 46. Qc6 Kh7 47. Nc3 Qf5 48. Qc4 Qc2 49. Kf3 Qf5+ 50. Ke2 Qc2+ 51. Ke3 Bf6 52. Qd3+ Qxd3+ 53. Kxd3 g6 54. Ne4 Bd8 55. Kd4 Kg7 56. Ke5 Kf7 57. Kd6 Be7+ 58. Kd5 Bd8 59. Kd6 Be7+ 60. Kd7 Bb4 61. g4 Ba3 62. h5 gxh5 63. gxh5 Bc1 64. Kd6 Bf4+ 65. Kd5 Ke7 66. f3 Kf7 67. Nf2 Kf6 68. Ke4 Bg3 69. Nh3 Bb8 70. f4 Ba7 71. f5 Bb8 72. Nf4 Kg5 73. Ne6+ Kxh5 74. Kd5 Bg3 75. Ng7+ Kg4 76. f6 Bh4 77. f7 Be7 78. Ke6 Bf8 79. Kf6 h5 80. Nxh5 { Howell, D. - Shankland, S., 1/2-1/2, St Louis Winter Classic A, 2017, https://lichess.org/4ijDMef3 }) 13... Qb7 14. d3 c5 15. Bg5 O-O 16. Rc1 Rc8 17. Nb1 h6 18. Bxf6 Nxf6 19. Nc3 Bc6 20. Qb3 Be7 21. Ra1 Ne8 22. Ne1 Bxg2 23. Nxg2 Nd6 24. Ne3 Bg5 25. Nf1 b4 26. Nb1 c4 27. dxc4 Nxc4 28. e3 Nxe3 29. fxe3 Rc1 30. Kf2 Qh1 31. Qd3 f5 32. h4 Be7 33. Ra5 f4 34. exf4 Qc6 35. Re5 Bc5+ 36. Rxc5 Qxc5+ 37. Ne3 b3 38. Nd2 Rc2 39. Kf3 Rxb2 40. Qd8+ Kh7 41. Qd3+ Kh8 42. Qd8+ Kh7 43. Qd3+ { Safarli, E. - Eljanov, P., 1/2-1/2, Vugar Gashimov Mem 2016, https://lichess.org/rW5cGc7P }) 8... Bb7 9. a4 (9. d4 Nbd7 10. Bg5 (10. Rd1 c5 11. Nc3 Qe7) 10... Rc8 { [%cal Bc7c5] } 11. a4 O-O (11... c5?! 12. axb5 axb5 13. Nc3 { [%csl Gb5] } 13... Qb6 14. Bxf6 Nxf6 15. Qb3 $14 $36 { [%csl Rb5] }) 12. axb5 axb5 13. Nc3 b4 14. Nb5 h6 15. Bxf6 Nxf6 16. Nxd6 cxd6 { [%cal Bd8b6] }) (9. Nc3 O-O 10. e4 b4) 9... c5 10. Nc3 Qb6 $10 { Very typical position }) (4. Nc3 a6 5. Bg2 b5) 4... Nd7 5. Bg2 (5. Qxc4 a6 6. Qc2 b6 (6... b5 7. Bg2 (7. a4 Bb7 8. Bg2 c5) 7... Bb7 8. a4 (8. O-O Ngf6 9. d3 c5 10. a4 Be7 11. Nc3 Qb6 12. Qb3 b4 13. a5 Qd8 (13... Qc7 { A better square for the queen }) 14. Nb1 Bd5 15. Qc2 O-O 16. Nbd2 Nb8 17. Rd1 Ra7 18. e4 Ba8 19. Nb3 Rc7 20. Be3 Nbd7 21. Qe2 Bc6 22. Nfd2 Bb5 23. Nc4 Rc8 24. e5 Nd5 25. Bd2 Nb8 26. h4 Nc6 27. Qg4 Kh8 28. Bg5 $14 $36 Rc7 29. Be4 h6 30. Bd2 f5 31. exf6 Nxf6 32. Qxe6 Nxe4 33. Qxe4 Bf6 34. Be3 Re7 35. Qg4 Bxb2 36. Rab1 Bd4 37. Nxd4 cxd4 38. Bf4 Bxc4 39. dxc4 Qe8 40. Qh3 Re2 41. Qg2 Qg6 42. Qf3 Re4 43. Qg2 Qe6 44. Bd6 Re8 45. Bxb4 Nxb4 46. Kh2 Qd6 47. Qf3 d3 48. Kg2 Re2 49. Qh5 Rf8 { Nakamura, Hi - Kramnik, Vladimir, 0-1, 4th Zurich CC Rapid, 2015, https://lichess.org/3FTLV7mh }) 8... c5) 7. Bg2 Bb7 8. O-O Ngf6 9. d3 (9. d4 c5! { This contact between the pawn on c5 and d4 is favorable for black because black can play Rc8 and virtually force white to take on c5 and help black to develop the bishop in one go }) 9... c5 10. b3 (10. e4!? { This was tried a few times with fair results for white, though conceptually it doesn't make much sense }) 10... Be7 { Black will develop normally with 0-0, Rc8, and only play b5 then so that Qb6 can support the b5 pawn without worry of being underdeveloped } { [%cal Be8g8,Ga8c8,Gd8b6,Bb6b5] }) 5... a6 { [%cal Gb7b5] } 6. Qxc4 (6. Nc3 Nf6 7. O-O Be7 (7... c5 8. e4) 8. Qxc4 b5 9. Qb3 Bb7 10. d3 O-O 11. a4 (11. Qc2?! c5 12. a4 Qb6 13. Bg5 h6 14. Be3 Rac8 $17 (14... Ng4 15. Bd2 (15. Bf4 Rfc8 { Play is on the queenside }) 15... Rfc8 16. Qb3 c4 17. dxc4 Nc5 18. Qb4 a5 19. Qxb5 Qa7 20. b4 Bc6 21. Qxa5 Qb7 22. Qxa8 Qxa8 23. bxc5 Bxc5 24. Nb5 Rd8 25. Bc3 Nf6 26. Bxf6 gxf6 27. a5 Qa6 28. Rfb1 Rc8 29. Nd2 Bxg2 30. Kxg2 Kg7 31. Ra2 Be7 32. Rb3 f5 33. Rd3 Bg5 34. e3 Be7 35. Kg1 Bb4 36. Rd4 Bc5 37. Rd7 Bb4 38. Ra1 Qc6 39. Rd4 Ra8 40. Nb3 Be7 41. Rad1 Bb4 42. Ra1 Be7 43. Rc1 Bb4 44. h4 Bxa5 45. Nxa5 Rxa5 46. Nc3 Rc5 47. Ne2 e5 48. Rdd1 Rxc4 49. Rxc4 Qxc4 50. Kf1 Qb5 51. Ke1 Qb4+ 52. Kf1 Qb5 53. Rc1 Qa6 54. Rd1 Qc4 { Aleksandrov, Aleksej - Vitiugov, Nikita, 1/2-1/2, Warsaw AIG Life rapid 8th playoff, 2008, https://lichess.org/Vf9K5lK0 })) 11... b4 12. Nb1 a5 13. Nbd2 c5) 6... b5 7. Qc2 Bb7 8. O-O Ngf6 9. a4 (9. d4?! { Gives black a target to strike on d4 } 9... c5! 10. a4 (10. Rd1?! { Fails to } 10... Rc8 { And white's queen is running out of squares as it turns out } { [%csl Rc2] } 11. dxc5 Bxc5 12. Qd3 Be4 { Harassing the queen } 13. Qd2 (13. Qb3?? Bc2! $19) 13... Bxf2+! 14. Kxf2 Bxb1 { [%cal Rf6e4] } 15. Qf4 Be4 $17 { [%cal Ge8g8] }) 10... cxd4 11. Nxd4 (11. axb5?! axb5 12. Rxa8 Qxa8 13. Na3 Bxa3 14. bxa3 O-O 15. Qb2 e5! 16. Qxb5 Rb8 $17 { [%csl Ra3,Ge5,Gd4] }) 11... Bxg2 12. Kxg2 Rc8 13. Qd1 b4 14. Nd2 Be7 15. N2b3 (15. N2f3 O-O 16. Bf4 Nc5 $17 { [%cal Gd8d5,Yd8b6] }) 15... O-O $15) (9. b3 c5 10. Bb2 Be7 11. d3 O-O 12. Nbd2 (12. a4 Nd5 13. Nbd2 Nb4 14. Qc1 Nf6 15. Qb1 h6 16. Rc1 Qb6 17. Be5 Ng4 18. Bc3 Nd5 19. Bb2 Rac8 $15 { [%cal Bf8d8,Gg4f6,Gd5b4] } 20. h3 Ngf6 21. Ne5 Rfd8 22. Ndf3 Nb4 23. axb5 axb5 24. Bc3 Nfd5 25. Bb2 Ra8 26. Rxa8 Rxa8 27. d4 Nf6 28. Rd1 Be4 29. Qc1 Bc2 30. Rd2 Bxb3 31. Nxf7 Bd5 32. N7e5 c4 33. Rd1 Qb7 34. Ng6 Bd6 35. Nf4 Bxf4 36. Qxf4 Na2 37. Nh4 c3 38. Bc1 Bxg2 39. Nxg2 b4 40. Qd6 c2 41. Re1 Qa6 42. Qe7 Nxc1 43. Rxc1 b3 44. Nf4 b2 45. Rf1 Re8 { Gulko, Boris F - Aleksandrov, Aleksej, 0-1, FIDE-Wch k.o., 1997, https://lichess.org/tLoSVstg }) 12... Rc8) (9. Nc3 { Nc3 is very commital since white's a4 advance can always be met with b4 so this doesn't make much sense } 9... c5 { Black gets good play } 10. a4 Qb6 { Keeping the structure together for now makes a lot of sense }) 9... c5 10. d3 (10. axb5 axb5 11. Rxa8 Qxa8 12. Na3 Qa6 (12... b4 13. Nc4) 13. Qb3 Bc6) 10... Be7 11. Nc3 Qb6 12. axb5 (12. Qb3 b4 13. a5 (13. Nb1 { [%cal Gb1d2,Gd2c4] } 13... a5 14. Nbd2 O-O 15. Nc4 Qa6 16. Qc2 (16. e4?! Nb6) (16. Bg5?! h6 17. Be3 (17. Bxf6 Bxf6 $15 { [%cal Gf6b2] }) 17... Nd5) 16... Nb6 17. Nxb6 (17. Nfe5 Bxg2 18. Kxg2 Nxc4 19. Nxc4 Rfd8) 17... Qxb6 18. b3 { [%cal Gc1b2] } 18... Rfd8 19. Bb2 Nd7 { [%cal Ge7f6] } 20. Nd2 (20. Bh3? Bxf3 21. exf3 Bf6 $17 { [%csl Rd3,Rf3] }) 20... Bxg2 21. Kxg2 Qb7+ 22. Kg1 h6 (22... Nb6 23. Rac1 h5 { [%cal Gh5h4] } 24. h4 f6) 23. Nc4 Nb6) 13... Qc7 14. Nb1 Bd5 15. Qc2 Rd8 (15... e5)) 12... axb5 13. Rxa8+ Bxa8 14. Qb3 b4 15. Nb1 { This makes most sense as the knight tries to reroute via d2-c4 } (15. Na4?! Qa7 { Any queen move is fine, but now the knight is just offside on a4 } { [%csl Ra4] }) (15. Nd1 { The knight can go to e3, but it doesn't do much there } 15... O-O) 15... O-O 16. Nbd2 { Preparing Nc4 } 16... Qa6 { Black can always improve and is already taking over the a-file! } { [%cal Gh7h6,Ga8c6,Bf8a8] } 17. Nc4 h6 18. e4 Bc6 19. Qc2 Ra8 20. b3 Nb6 { Preparing to take on c4 } 21. Nfe5 Bb5 22. Bb2 { [%csl Ge5] } 22... Nfd7 23. Rc1 Nxe5 24. Bxe5 Rd8 { With some targets to play against, white's position is difficult to improve } { [%csl Rd3][%cal Gb5c6,Ba6b7] } *


[Event "Fighting the English 🇬🇧: 1...b6!?"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/study/biRarCEJ/5eER3nYz"]
[Date "2026.09.19"]
[Result "*"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/vladislavdemchenko"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ECO "A40"]
[Opening "English Defense: Perrin Variation"]
[StudyName "English Opening Repertoire"]
[ChapterName "English Defense"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/fTpa2qZW/q9YlAPFx"]
[UTCDate "2026.09.19"]
[UTCTime "15:53:18"]

{ [%anno "Mikae5", mikae5] The material of this study is under the heavy influence of the book "English Defence" (1999) by GM Daniel King & also with the help of modern computer analysis }
1. c4 { [%anno "Mikae5", mikae5] The English Defence is primarily an opening against 1.c4 but can occur against 1.d4 if white complies by choosing a d4 & c4 setup } (1. d4 { [%anno "Mikae5", mikae5] Black can try to play the English Defence against 1.d4 but needs to be ready to transpose either into the Owen's Defence or the French } 1... e6 (1... b6 2. e4 { [%anno "Mikae5", mikae5] Owen's }) 2. c4 (2. e4 { [%anno "Mikae5", mikae5] French }) 2... b6) 1... b6! { I first discovered this from GM Ben Finegold who used this as a tricky blitz weapon, but more seriously it was also played in Lev Polugaevsky vs Viktor Korchnoi, Candidates Semifinal (1977) & many times by Tony Miles who is a big proponent of this opening.
It doesn't stand the test of time due to the lack of concern of space, and so white has quite a few lines that secure an advantage, though it is very tricky if white plays too slowly and black can quickly take over } 2. d4 (2. Nc3 Bb7 3. e4 e6 4. Nge2 c5 5. g3 (5. d3) (5. d4 cxd4 6. Nxd4 a6 7. Be2 d6) 5... Nc6 6. Bg2 Nge7 7. O-O (7. a3 Nd4 8. Nxd4 cxd4 9. Ne2 Nc6 10. d3 a5 (10... g6 11. b4 Bg7 12. h4 h5 13. Bg5 Qc7 14. O-O d6 15. Rc1 O-O 16. Bh3 e5 17. c5 dxc5 18. bxc5 Bc8 19. Bxc8 Rfxc8 20. cxb6 Qxb6 21. Rc4 Qa6 22. Qa4 Qxa4 23. Rxa4 f6 24. Bd2 Rab8 25. Rd1 Kf7 26. Kf1 Bf8 27. Rc1 Ke8 28. Bb4 Bxb4 29. axb4 Kd7 30. Rc4 Rb7 31. Nc1 Nxb4 32. Rxc8 Kxc8 33. Ke2 Nc6 34. Ra2 Kc7 35. Kd2 a5 { Cornette, M. - Abdusattorov, Nodirbek, 0-1, 5th Purtichju Open 2018, https://lichess.org/GsLcsoCH }) 11. b3 Bc5) 7... Nd4) 2... e6 { [%anno "Mikae5", mikae5] Black should play e6 first because it really provokes white into fully occupying the center with e4, and that is the essence of this opening for black: Black allows white all the center they could ever want but will challenge & put pressure on it with various ideas.
White can either play e4 now or a3 to prevent one of black's key ideas (Bb4 to pin the c3 knight). } { [%cal Gc8b7] } (2... Bb7) 3. e4 (3. a3 { White prevents one of the key themes of the English Defence, pinning the knight on c3 with the bishop to control the center } 3... Bb7 4. Nc3 Nf6 5. d5 Bd6!? 6. Nf3 (6. e4) 6... O-O 7. g3 (7. e4 exd5 8. exd5 (8. cxd5 Qe8!))) 3... Bb7 4. Bd3 { White's best way to defend e4 has been shown in practice to be with the bishop as other ways of defending allows black to undermine white's center further } (4. Nc3 Bb4 { This is the main idea of the English defence, undermine the center with a pin } 5. f3 { Defending the pawn this way } (5. Bd3 { Another way of defending e4. But now that the bishop is not on f8 anymore, the king has f8 to run to so now f5 is really powerful! } 5... f5! 6. Qh5+ (6. d5!?) (6. Qe2 Nf6) (6. f3 Qh4+ 7. g3 Qh5 8. exf5 Nf6) 6... g6) 5... Qh4+ { Provoking some weaknesses! } (5... f5 { As far as I see, this is not worse than Qh4+ but white has all the fun and creates a dangerous attack } 6. exf5 Nh6!? { If Bxh6 then Qh4+ } 7. fxe6 Nf5 8. Bf4 dxe6 9. Qa4+ Nc6 10. d5! Bxc3+ 11. bxc3 exd5 12. O-O-O O-O (12... Qf6 13. cxd5 Qxc3+ 14. Kb1 Qb4+ 15. Qxb4 Nxb4 16. Bb5+ Kf7 17. Nh3 h6 (17... Nxd5?! 18. Bc4 Ne7 19. Ng5+ Kf6 20. Ne4+ Kg6 21. Bc1 Rhd8 22. h4 $14 $36) 18. d6! Nxd6 19. Bxd6 cxd6 20. Rd4! (20. Rxd6?! Rhd8 21. Rhd1 Nd5 22. Rxd8 Rxd8 23. Kb2 Ke7 $15 { Black has more active pieces and the knight on h3 is misplaced, black's king can activate quickly via d6-c5 and black has the majority on the queenside which gives good chances for an edge } { [%csl Ba7,Bb6][%cal Ge7d6,Gd6c5] }) 20... a5 21. Rxd6 Rhd8 22. Rxb6 Rab8 { Threatening Be4+ } 23. Bc4+ Bd5 24. Rxb8 Rxb8 25. Bxd5+ Nxd5+ 26. Kc1 Nc3 { Black wins back the pawn with more active pieces, but this is objectively equal } { [%csl Ra2] }) 13. cxd5 Na5 14. Nh3 Qf6 15. Qc2 (15. Kb2) 15... h6 16. g4 Nd6 17. Bd3 Ndc4 18. g5 hxg5 19. Bxg5 Qxf3 20. Rhg1 Ne3 21. Bh6!! Qxd1+!! 22. Qxd1 Nxd1 23. Rxg7+ Kh8 { White can go for the draw or more with Kxd1 } 24. Kxd1 Rf6 25. Rh7+ Kg8 26. Ng5 Rxh6 { Removing a dangerous attacker } 27. Rxh6 Bxd5 $10 { Ultimately reaching an equal endgame }) 6. g3 Bxc3+ { Important to take now before white is able to play a move like Qb3 and not worsen their pawn structure } 7. bxc3 Qe7 { Staying humble }) (4. f3) (4. d5) (4. Qc2) (4. Nd2) 4... Nc6 { Black aims to win the light-squared bishop with Nb4 as challenging white's pawn center with f5 is very risky. First tried in Top-level by Miles against Sosonko, Tunis Interzonal 1985 with a peaceful 9 move draw. } (4... Bb4+!? { Black tries to exchange pieces or force white into moving the king } 5. Kf1! { This is the best move, white claims that they can easily castle by hand and the bishop is misplaced on b4 } (5. Bd2 { Black achieves a nice exchange of the bishops so that it's no longer a problem piece! } 5... Bxd2+ (5... Qe7 6. Ne2 (6. Nc3 f5! 7. Nge2 fxe4 8. Bxe4 Bxe4 9. Nxe4 Bxd2+ 10. Qxd2) (6. Bxb4?! { Already gives black a very comfortable position following } 6... Qxb4+ { Qd2 is forced as b2 is hanging } 7. Qd2 (7. Nc3 { Sacrificing b2 } 7... Qxb2 8. Nge2 Qa3 { Black can safely get away with an extra pawn but white has good compensation of course with the massive center and lead in development. Black can play Qe7 next and possibly Nh6 and 0-0 } { [%cal Ga3e7,Be8g8,Bg8h6] }) 7... Qxd2+ 8. Nxd2 f5! { This move gives black equal chacnes } 9. Ngf3 { Most natural } (9. f3 fxe4 10. fxe4 e5! { Undermining the center and leading to a favorable structure } 11. d5 { Gaining some space } (11. dxe5 Nc6 { With Nge7-g6 next to pick up the pawn back with a slightly better position } { [%csl Re5][%cal Bg8e7,Be7g6] }) 11... a5 { Black secures the c5 square for the knight so that b4 cannot kick out the knight and black is very comfortable already } { [%cal Bb8a6,Ba6c5] } 12. Ngf3 d6 { Black has a very solid position } { [%cal Bg8f6,Ge8e7] } 13. Ng5?! Bc8 $15 { Covering e6 and next black can comfortably play Nf6 and h6 to kick the knight back and Ng5 turns out not to accomplish much. Black is already better } { [%cal Bg8f6,Ye8g8,Gh7h6] }) 9... fxe4 10. Bxe4 (10. Nxe4 Nf6 { With a completely fine and equal position }) 10... Bxe4 { Trading pieces i s good } 11. Nxe4 Nf6 { The endgame after gxf6 is quite nice as black has nice central control } 12. Nc3 Nc6 13. O-O O-O-O { With equal chances, black doesn't have any particular weaknesses }) 6... Bxd2+ 7. Qxd2 f5 8. exf5 Bxg2 9. Rg1 Bb7 10. Nbc3 (10. fxe6 dxe6 11. Nbc3 Nc6 12. O-O-O O-O-O 13. d5 exd5 14. Nxd5 Qf7)) 6. Qxd2 (6. Nxd2)) (5. Nc3 f5 { Transposing into 4.Nc3 })) (4... f5? { Just does not work } 5. exf5 { Black doesn't have sufficient compensation } 5... Bb4+ { Freeing up the f8 square with tempo } 6. Kf1 { Black just objectively does not have enough compensation. A sample line goes as follows } (6. Nc3?? Bxg2 $19 { Now Qh5+ can be safely met with Kf8. Black is winning } { [%cal Be8f8] }) 6... Nf6 7. a3 Bd6 8. Nc3 O-O 9. h4! { Preparing Rh3 ideas. White has very active play and is up a clean pawn. }) 5. Ne2 { It looks strange that the knight would go to e2 instead of f3, but the knight is not blocking the f-pawn so that f4 can come to go for an attack } (5. Nf3 Nb4 { Winning the bishop pair } 6. O-O Nxd3 7. Qxd3 { Black is far behind in development but if they can complete development then the closed position gives black fair chances to fight } 7... Ne7 { Preparing to bring the knight to g6, fairly slow but it's better than getting kicked around after Nf6 } 8. Nc3 (8. h4) 8... Ng6 { The knight cannot be harassed with the h-pawn advance and so it is quite secure here } 9. d5 { White immediately tries to gain the initiative with this advance as otherwise black would consolidate } (9. b3 { Developing the bishop to b2 also makes a lot of sense } { [%cal Bc1b2] } 9... Be7 10. d5 O-O { Black secures the king first and is only now ready to play e5 to close the cente } (10... e5?! { Imprecise timing, because black hasn't castled yet } { Magerramov, Elmar - Ehlvest, Jaan, 0-1, Alekhine op, 1992, https://lichess.org/NbBsmwpU } 11. h4! { Critical } 11... h5 { Black is forced to weaken the kingside } (11... Bxh4? 12. Nxh4 Qxh4 13. Nb5 $16 { With some d6 ideas, best for black is to move the king to d8 but clearly that's already very bad } { [%cal Rd5d6] } 13... Kd8) 12. g3 d6 13. a4 a5 14. Ng5 { White looks to be the only side with counterplay, but black has some potential. The big problem is that none of the following moves are forced by any means and so just strategically this is very risky } 14... Bc8 { Covering the e6 square and preparing f6 } { [%cal Gf7f6] } 15. Nb5 f6 16. Ne6 Bxe6 17. dxe6 c6 { Now if the knight retreats then the e6 pawn will fall very soon following Nf8 } 18. Be3! { With a very interesting piece sacrifice } (18. Nc3?! Nf8 $17 { e6 falls } { [%csl Re6] }) 18... cxb5 19. cxb5 O-O $13 { The position is highly unclear but playable for both sides, black has some serious problems in development but is up a whole piece. The computer suggests the following line that leads to a perpetual at the very end of the complications } { [%csl Rb6] } 20. Rac1 d5 21. exd5 e4! 22. Qxe4 f5 23. Qd3 f4 24. Qxg6 fxe3 25. fxe3 Qxd5 26. Rf7 Bf6 27. Rf1 Rxf7 28. Qxf7+ Kh8 29. Rxf6 gxf6 30. Qxf6+ Kg8 31. Qg6+ Kh8 32. Qh6+ Kg8 33. Qg6+ $10 { Perpetual }) 11. Bb2 { Black secures the king first and is only now ready to play e5 to close the center } 11... e5! $140 { Black is ready to play d6 and Bc8 next with typical counterplay on the kingside } { [%cal Bd7d6,Gb7c8] }) (9. Re1!? { This move doesn't make too much sense as it's quite possible that the rook is better on f1 to support f4 in the future rather than supporting the e-file } 9... Be7 10. d5 O-O 11. Be3 e5! { Closing the position, very thematic } 12. Rac1 Bc8 { Either d6 first or Bc8 is interchangeable } 13. Nd2 a5 14. a3 d6 15. b4 { White decides to play on the queenside, a rather different strategy than usual } 15... f5! 16. exf5 { Considered best } (16. f3?! { Weakens the structure a lot and here black can play energetically! } 16... Bh4! { Provoking either a weakness with g3 or gaining a tempo on the rook } (16... Nh4 { Chosen by King against Mednis } 17. Kh1 Rf6 18. c5 bxc5 19. bxc5 Rg6 20. Rg1 Ba6 21. Qc2 f4 22. Bf2 Rh6 23. a4 Qe8 24. Nb5 Nf5 25. h3 Bxb5 26. axb5 Ne3 { Mednis, Edmar John - King, Daniel J, 1/2-1/2, Stavanger op, 1989, https://lichess.org/7uVGdIyk }) 17. Rf1 (17. g3? { Loses to } 17... f4! 18. gxh4 (18. Bf2 fxg3 19. hxg3 Qg5 20. Kh2 Bxg3+ 21. Bxg3 Nf4! { [%cal Bg5h5] } 22. Qc2 Rf6 23. Nf1 Bh3! $19 { Threatening to take the knight, white is lost } { [%csl Rf1] }) 18... fxe3 19. Qxe3 Qxh4 $17 $40 { With a crushing attack } { [%csl Rg1][%cal Bg6f4] }) 17... f4 18. Bf2 Bxf2+ { Successfully trading off white's dark-squared bishop and weakening the kingside this way } 19. Rxf2 Nh4 $17 $36 { Black has a dangerous initiative with h5, g5-g4 } { [%cal Bg7g5,Yg8h8,Gd8f6,Bg5g4,Bh7h5] }) 16... Bxf5 17. Nde4 Nh4!? $140 { With the idea of Qe8-g6 and pressuring white's kingside } { [%cal Yd8e8,Ye8g6] } 18. f3 axb4 19. axb4 Qe8 20. Kh1 Qg6 21. Qe2 Bxe4 22. Nxe4 { Still covering the g5 square } { [%csl Gg5] } (22. fxe4 Bg5 23. Bxg5 Qxg5 { Black dominates the dark squares and has a very pleasant position } { [%csl Rf4][%cal Ba8f8,Bf8f6,Yh4g6,Yg6f4] }) 22... Ra3 23. Bg1 Rfa8 24. Rc3 R3a4 25. c5 bxc5 26. bxc5 Nf5 { Black is certainly no worse and can play against white's center pawn in the future } { [%csl Rd5][%cal Ga4a5,Yg6f7] }) 9... Be7 10. Be3 O-O 11. Rad1 e5! { Now that black is fully developed, closing the center now would limit white's play and later black will open up the position for the 2 bishops } 12. Ne2 d6 { With a similar position to the King's Indian with this structure, black will break eventually with f5. White's normal counterplay with b4-c5 is too slow and well covered by the d6 & b6 pawns. } 13. Ng3 Bc8 { Activating the bishop through the other diagonal & threatening Bg4 } 14. h3 Nh4 { Restoring some piece harmony by trading the better knight on f3 with the g6 knight } 15. Nxh4 Bxh4 16. Nf5 Bg5! { Improvement, research } (16... g6? { Tried in the game Ruzele vs Kengis, 1996 } 17. Nxh4 Qxh4 18. f4! $16 { With a big advantage } (18. b3 { Played in the game } 18... Bd7 19. f3 f5 20. exf5 gxf5 21. f4 exf4 22. Qd4 Rae8 23. Qxf4 Qxf4 24. Bxf4 { Ruzele, Darius - Kengis, Edvins, 1/2-1/2, Bonn GSK, 1996, https://lichess.org/HTQzv2z8 }))) (5. d5 { Committing the pawn to d5 so early has it's drawbacks, such as giving away the e5 square } 5... Ne5 6. Nc3 { Best not to care about the bishop right now and focus on development } (6. Be2 { Played in all previous encounters } 6... f5! { I like choosing the most radical of solutions, it is the English defence after all } 7. exf5 (7. f4?! { Opens up the long-diagonal } 7... Ng4! 8. Bxg4 (8. exf5 exf5 $17 { Black has nice squares for development with Bc5 and Nf6 next, white on the other hand white has weakened the kingside with f4. } { [%csl Rf2][%cal Bf8c5,Gg8f6] }) 8... Qh4+ $17 { Typical tactic, g3 would weaken the long-diagonal too much so the king is forced to move. Black has a big advantage. }) 7... exd5 { Taking this way is correct as the f-pawn will be weak anyways so it's best to take d5 instead } 8. cxd5 Bb4+ { Nicely developing with tempo } 9. Nc3 Qf6 { Attacking f5 } 10. Qd4 { Attacking the bishop and discouraging Qxf5 on account of f4 } 10... Bxc3+ 11. bxc3 { Best } (11. Qxc3 Qxf5 12. Nf3 { Logical development } (12. f4 { Most drastic solution } 12... Ng6 13. Qxc7 Bxd5 14. Nf3 Nf6 $17 { Black will 0-0 next or if Qd6 then play Kd8 and bring the rook to e8 quickly where the black king will be very secure! Black's pieces are just so much more active and the rooks activate quickly } { [%cal Be8g8,Ye8d8,Rc7d6] } 15. Qd6) 12... Nxf3+ 13. Bxf3 Nf6 14. Qxc7 Bxd5 15. Bxd5 Nxd5 $15 { With a very active position and slight edge }) 11... Ne7 { Preparing to capture on f5 with the knight } { [%csl Rf5] } 12. f4 (12. Bf4 d6 { Securing the knight } 13. Bxe5 dxe5 14. Qa4+ Kf7 15. Nf3 Bxd5 { Winning back the pawn and with Rhd8 and Kf8 next, black has a very nice and active position. } { [%cal Bh8d8] }) 12... Ng4! { Tactically solving all the problems } 13. Qxf6 { Best } (13. Bxg4? { Giving up the bishop pair is not good, obviously this is tactically justifiable } 13... Qh4+ { g3 would be weakening and Kf1 would misplace the king } 14. Qf2 Qxg4 $17 { Black just has a big lead in development and white's pawn structure is ruined } { [%csl Rf5,Rd5] }) 13... Nxf6 { d5 and f5 are weak } { [%csl Rd5,Rf5] } 14. c4 Nxf5 { Winning back the pawn } (14... O-O { Computer recommended } 15. g4 b5! { The computer claims full compensation here, but this is less clear after both g5 and Rb1 here }) 15. Bd3 Nd6 16. Ba3 b5! { Undermining white's center } 17. cxb5 (17. Bxd6?! { Giving up the bishop pair to ruin black's pawn structure is a dubious decision in such an open board } 17... bxc4! { Nice intermezzo } 18. Bxc4 cxd6 $17 { d5 will fall next, and if Rd1 then black can activate quickly with Rc8 and maintains a big advantage. } { [%csl Rd5,Rf4] }) 17... Bxd5 { g2 is attacked } 18. Nf3 Nh5! { Very annoying to deal with the f-pawn, Bc1 can be met with 0-0. Black is doing great }) 6... Bb4 7. Ne2 Ne7 8. Bb1) 5... Nb4 { Eliminating the bishop pair when given the chances } (5... e5?! { Interesting but not good, the b7 bishop is just shut out for the rest of the game. The idea is to prevent white from occupying the entire center with f4, but as an English defence player, we should embrace it and undermine it with moves like f5. } 6. d5 Nb4 7. Nbc3 Nxd3+ 8. Qxd3 { The b7 bishop is just dead } { [%csl Rb7] } 8... d6 9. O-O Nf6 10. f4 $16 { Black's center is fragile }) 6. Nbc3 { b1 is now free for the bishop so black should take the bishop now } (6. O-O { I think that this is the most accurate continuation, although less popular. The point is that black cannot pin the c3 knight and so finding a good move to develop is difficult. } 6... Nxd3 7. Qxd3 d6 { Chosen by Ivanchyk against Kramnik in 2002, black just wants to play Nf6, Be7 and 0-0 if allowed. White has enough time to grab the initiative so this plan is not possible } { [%cal Bg8f6,Gf8e7] } (7... Ne7 8. Nbc3 Ng6 (8... g6?! { g6 and e6 almost never works together } 9. d5! Bg7 10. Qh3 $16 { With Bh6 next to exchange a key defender, black is cramped and cannot move at all }) 9. f4 f5 10. exf5 Nh4 11. fxe6 dxe6 12. f5 (12. d5) 12... Nxf5 13. g4 Nd6 14. Nf4 $18 { Black is completely busted } { [%csl Re6] }) 8. Nbc3 (8. d5 Nf6 9. Nbc3 { Transposes }) 8... Nf6 9. d5 Be7 (9... e5?! { Black's center will not survive after } 10. f4! { Breaking through in the center } 10... Be7 11. c5! { bxc5 cannot be played on account of Qb5+, black is busted } 11... c6!? { Played once before, trying to confuse white } 12. Ng3 $18 { The knight jumps into f5 and black's entire position just collapses } { [%cal Bg3f5] } (12. Rd1?! bxc5 { Gual Pascual, Antonio - Gonzalez Velez, Fermin, 0-1, Montcada op, 1997, https://lichess.org/bwvJ7DC9 } { Gives black a fine position })) 10. Nd4 { Under no circumstance does black want to voluntarily play e5 as it will weaken f5 and provide white with a hook in the center to break with f4 } (10. Qg3 { Played against Meier once }) 10... Qd7 11. a4 { Trying to create some weaknesses with a5! } { [%cal Ra4a5] } (11. f4! { Looks tremendous for white }) 11... c5! { Black should respond dramatically! } 12. Nde2 { Still retains a bug advantage, white doesn't have to do much but slowly improve as black doesn't have an active plan } (12. dxc6?! { Chosen by Kramnik, this wasn't necessary as releasing the tension improves the pawn structure that could occur if white still goes for the a5 advance } 12... Bxc6 13. Nxc6 (13. a5 bxa5 14. Rxa5) (13. b3) 13... Qxc6 14. Be3 { [%csl Ra6,Rb7] } (14. b3))) 6... Nxd3+ 7. Qxd3 Bb4!? { The additional option available instead of 6.0-0. This move is clearly not as effective as if the knight were on f3 because white is ready to recapture on c3 with the knight, but the bishop stands better here than on e7 anyways } (7... d6 { Trying to go for Nf6, Be7 and 0-0 next } { [%cal Gf8e7,Be8g8,Bg8f6] } 8. O-O Nf6 { The knight belongs on f6, on e7 it does nothing and white can easily target it with f4-f5 } 9. d5 Be7 10. Nd4 Qd7 { Transposes to the mainline of 6.0-0 as seen in the Kramnik vs Ivanchuk game } 11. f4) (7... g6?! { Creates many weaknesses, typically the g6 and e6 complex does not go well together } 8. h4! $16 { Black has too many weaknesses and is severely lacking in space }) 8. O-O { Black has only one good developing move clearly with Ne7 as Nf6 will get hit with e5 } (8. Qg3!? { Sacrificing the e4 pawn for g7 is not the best trade for white } 8... Bxe4 { Snatching a central pawn } 9. Qxg7 Qf6 10. Qg3 (10. Bh6!? { A tricky move, but black can just sacrifice the exchange and continue with development quickly } 10... Qxh6 11. Qxh8 O-O-O! { Nf6 is the threat to trap the queen } 12. Qe5 Bxg2 13. Rg1 Bb7 { Black has tremendous compensation for the exchange due to the bishop pair and white's inability to castle long. Ideas of Ba6 to pressure c4 as well as Ne7-f5, and Bd6 is a possibility to win h2. } { [%cal Bg8e7,Ge7f5,Yb4d6,Gb7a6] } 14. Qg5 { Trying to trade the queens off this way, h2 is poisoned on account of Qxg8!! } (14. Qf4 { Trying to trade queens at the cost of weakening the structure } 14... Bxc3+ { White is forced to take back with the pawn since the knight is overloaded } 15. bxc3) 14... Ba6 { Not minding the exchange but instead targeting the weaknesses in white's position as well as freeing up the back-rank so Qxh2 is a threat } { [%csl Rc4] } 15. Qxh6 Nxh6 16. Rg7 { Staying active } (16. b3?! d5! $17 { Black loses a pawn at least }) 16... Rh8 { Passive, but necessary for now } 17. c5! { Only move to keep the balance as b3 still runs into d5! } 17... bxc5 18. a3 Bxc3+ 19. bxc3 cxd4 20. Nxd4 Bd3! { Preventing Rb1! and also threatening Bg6 to trap the rook, white is forced to retreat. } 21. Rg5 d5! $44 { Closing the fifth rank and black has 2 pawns for the exchange, prevents white from occupying the b-file, and can expand with c5. Black's compensation is clear as day }) 10... Qg6!) 8... Ne7 9. Na4! { The best move according to the computer, leading to a mess. White is threatening to trap the bishop } (9. a3 { Winning the bishop pair } 9... Bxc3 10. Nxc3 O-O 11. d5 f5! (11... d6?! { Franco Ocampos, Zenon - Teske, Henrik, 1/2-1/2, Capablanca Memorial Premier 33rd, 1998, https://lichess.org/lnohgXib } 12. f4 $16) 12. dxe6 dxe6 13. Qxd8 Raxd8 14. Bg5 Rd7 15. Rfd1 Rxd1+ 16. Rxd1 Nc6 17. exf5 (17. Rd7 Rf7 $10) 17... Rxf5) (9. Nd1!? { With a similar idea to Na4 but without the option of playing c5 to trap in the b4 bishop } 9... f5! 10. f3 (10. Qb3 Ba5 11. Qa3 c5 12. dxc5 bxc5 13. Be3 (13. Qxc5 Bb6 14. Qb4 a5 15. Qb3 Bxe4) 13... Bb4 14. Qb3) (10. e5 Ng6 11. c5 bxc5 12. a3 c4! 13. Qxc4 Be7 $17) 10... fxe4 11. fxe4 Ng6 { Freeing e7 for the bishop to be able to retreat }) 9... Ng6 10. c5! { Threatening again to trap the bishop } 10... Bc6 { Only move } (10... bxc5?! 11. dxc5 { Still threatening a3-b4, and playing c6 would lock up the light-squared bishop forever and create chronic weakness on d7 } 11... Bc6 12. a3 Ba5 13. Qc2! $16 { Defending the knight and now b4 will come } { [%cal Bb2b4] }) 11. Qc2 (11. f4! $146 { A very interesting piece sacrifice, going for the immediate f5 instead of caring about material for now as black's pieces are not coordinating correctly and can easily be targetted } 11... Bxa4 12. f5 Ne5! { Only move, now dxe5 would allow black's dark-squared bishop to escape } 13. Qg3 (13. dxe5?! { Loses c5 with check } 13... Bxc5+ 14. Kh1 { Now white seems to have a great position and strong initiative, but it turns out that the bishop pair here really matter and black can often castle by hand to the queenside! } { [%cal Ba4c6] } 14... a5! { Preventing any b4 advances and with the idea of a crafty castling by hand without running into Qa6+ } 15. Nf4 Qe7 16. a3 Bc6 { Securing the bishop } 17. Nh5 { White will win g7, but it doesn't matter } 17... Kd8! { Just escaping and castling by hand } { [%cal Gc8b7,Gd8c8] } 18. Nxg7 Kc8 19. f6 Qd8 20. Bd2 d5! $17 { Opening up the center and activating black's bishop pair, black has a big advantage already } 21. exd5 Qxd5 22. Qxd5 Bxd5 23. Rfe1 Bb3 $17 { Halting the pawn's advance and black can play Rd8 next to activate with the active bishop pair. } { [%cal Gh8d8] }) 13... O-O { Giving back the piece } 14. f6 { Immediately threatening mate } (14. a3 Bxc5 15. f6 (15. dxc5?! f6) 15... Ng6 16. dxc5 Bb5 { Transposes to 14.f6 }) (14. Bh6 Ng6 15. fxg6 hxg6 16. Bf4 bxc5 17. a3 e5! 18. Bg5 (18. Bxe5 Bd2 19. Bxc7 (19. dxc5 Qe7 $15 { [%csl Rc5] }) 19... Qg5 20. Qxg5 Bxg5 21. dxc5 Be3+ 22. Kh1 Bxc5 $15 { [%csl Ba4,Bc5] }) 18... f6 19. axb4 Bb5 20. Bd2 Bxe2 21. Rf2 Bc4 22. Rc1 Ba6 23. dxe5 fxe5 24. Rxf8+ Qxf8 25. Qxe5 $13) (14. dxe5 f6 $10) 14... Ng6 15. a3 { A highly complex but fun position never seen before in practice } 15... Bxc5 16. dxc5 Bb5! { Only move, eliminating the knight which had potential on c3 } 17. Rf2 (17. fxg7 { Possible to include this first } 17... Kxg7 18. Rf2 Bxe2 { Eliminating the knight at the first opportunity } 19. Rxe2 bxc5 20. Qc3+ e5! { Blocking the diagonal and gaining space. This also makes it so white can never play e5 } 21. Qxc5 f5! { Undermining white's center at the cost of opening up the king position. Black will get a nice center pawn formation going } 22. exf5 Rxf5 23. Be3 d6 24. Qd5 { Still keeping the queen on an active outpost } 24... Rc8 { a7 cannot be taken because of Nf4. Black will play Qd7 and prepare c6 followed by d5 next } { [%cal Bd8d7,Gc7c6,Gd6d5] } 25. Rc1 Qd7 { c6 and d5 is next. Black has good play } 26. Qc6? { Looks logical to stop the pawn advancing to c6, but the trade of queens allows black's king to occupy the center as then there is no danger and black's central pawn majority is decisive } 26... Qxc6 27. Rxc6 Ne7 28. Ra6 Kf6 29. Bxa7 Ke6 $17 { Black's rooks will get very active and the knight can make use of d5. If allowed then the pawn mass can run forward with c5 and d5 for example. } { [%cal Bc8f8,Ge7d5] }) 17... Bxe2 18. cxb6 { bxc7 cannot be allowed } 18... axb6 19. fxg7 { Another important intermezzo to not allow Qxf6 } 19... Kxg7 20. Rxe2 f6 { Important move, covering the e5 square. Ideas of d6 to keep the center in-tact, Qe7 to connect rooks, Rf7 if necessary, and even Ra5 at some point to activate the rook. Black has good play } { [%cal Bd7d6,Gd8e7,Gf8f7,Ya8a5] }) 11... f5! { With drastic measures } 12. a3! (12. exf5 { Gives black a chance to fight back } 12... Nh4! 13. f3 Nxf5 14. a3 b5 15. axb4 bxa4 16. Bf4 O-O 17. Qd2 { Rognes, Øystein - Gaujens, Artis, 1-0, WC32/pr05, 2008, https://lichess.org/16qYrELu } 17... a6 18. d5 Bxd5 19. Rxa4 Bc4 { d6 coming next } { [%cal Bd7d6] }) 12... Bxe4 13. Qd1 b5 14. axb4 bxa4 15. Nc3 Bb7 16. d5 a6 17. Qxa4 exd5 18. Re1+ Kf7 19. Qb3 c6 20. Ne2 $16 { This is a really bad position but it's not dead lost immediately } { [%csl Rb7,Rf7] } *


