1. d4 b6 2. c4 Bb7 { Black chooses a different move order but we will soon get the same position as recommended in the previous chapter } 3. Nc3 e6 4. e4 Bb4! { Key theme #1: Pinning the knight on c3 and thus indirectly putting pressure on the center } 5. Qc2?! { A weak defensive move } 5... Qh4! { Key theme #2: Activating the queen to put pressure on the center immediately, and if white pushes g3 to kick the queen away then the bishop on b7 would have an open line of pressure } 6. Bd3 f5! { Key theme #3: Undermining the center with the f-pawn. Usually when black plays f5, the kingside can become a mess but in return white's center will collapse and make kingside castling not recommended. } 7. g3 Qh5 { And now the light-squares look really bad for white around the kingside } 8. Be2 Qf7 9. f3 fxe4 10. fxe4 Nf6 { Putting pressure on the center, and white struggles to keep it all together } 11. d5 { Desperation, temporarily closing the diagonal but white's center is still under pressure } 11... O-O 12. Nf3 Qg6 { More pressure on e4, forcing white to react } 13. Bd3 Qh5 14. O-O Na6! { Preparing the knight jump to c5 where it would look fantastic to attack the bishop & pressure e4 } 15. a3 Bxc3 { Only now that white has wasted a tempo with a3 does black take the time to capture on c3 } 16. bxc3 Nc5 17. Be3 Nxd3 { Taking away a key defender of white's center } 18. Qxd3 exd5 19. exd5 Nxd5 { Unleashing an attack on the f3 knight } 20. cxd5 Rxf3 21. Rxf3 Qxf3 { Black is just up a pawn and still has a monster b7 bishop staring down white, white is already dead lost by move 21. } 22. Rd1 Ba6 23. Qd2 Bc4 24. Bf4 d6 25. h4 Rf8 26. Kh2 Be2 27. Rg1 Re8 28. Rg2 Bc4 29. Rf2 Qe4 30. Qd4 Bxd5 31. Qxe4 Rxe4 32. h5 h6 33. g4 Rc4 { 0-1 White resigns. } 0-1


